\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Contraception, gay marriage: \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  signals new targets for supreme court\b0\par\par\uc2 Joan E Greve in Washington \par \uc2 972 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 20:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rightwing justice appears to offer preview of the court\u8217\'20\'19s potential future rulings after decision to remove US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights\par \par \uc2 Many Americans reacted to the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to reverse Roe v Wade and remove federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US with shock, but many also asked a terrified question: what might be next?\par \par \uc2 The conservative justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  appeared to offer a preview of the court\u8217\'20\'19s potential future rulings, suggesting the rightwing-controlled court may return to the issues of contraception access and marriage equality, threatening LGBTQ rights.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn future cases, we should reconsider all of this court\u8217\'20\'19s substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell,\u8221\'20\'1D Thomas wrote in his concurring opinion to the ruling on Roe.\par \par \uc2 Griswold v Connecticut established a married couple\u8217\'20\'19s right to use contraception without government interference in 1965. The court ruled in the 2003 case of Lawrence v Texas that states could not criminalize sodomy, and Obergefell v Hodges established the right for same-sex couples to marry in 2015.\par \par \uc2 In the decision written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , the conservative majority makes it clear that the decision in Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization should not be interpreted as a threat to other major precedent cases. But the court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan \u8211\'20\'13 dismissed that logic as a farce in their fiery dissenting opinion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEither the mass of the majority\u8217\'20\'19s opinion is hypocrisy or additional constitutional rights are under threat,\u8221\'20\'1D the liberal justices wrote. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is one or the other.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Thomas\u8217\'20\'19s concurring opinion confirmed what many progressive lawmakers and reproductive rights advocates have feared for years. The end of Roe marks the beginning, not the end, of judicial overreach by the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority, they say.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is important that Americans understand that this supreme court and Republicans in Congress will not stop here,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , the chair of the Congressional Progressive Caucus. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is clear [Thomas] and the court\u8217\'20\'19s majority have no respect for other precedents that have been won in recent decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Adam Green, co-founder of the Progressive Change Campaign Committee, warned that the court\u8217\'20\'19s decision to overturn Roe would only intensify its \u8220\'20\'1Cgiant legitimacy crisis\u8221\'20\'1D with millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFive Republican justices appointed by presidents who lost the popular vote are routinely making hyper-partisan decisions that take away the rights of Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D Green said.\par \par \uc2 For now, Thomas does not seem to have the support of his conservative colleagues in overturning other major cases, as they did not join his opinion. But the majority decision written by Alito could lay the foundation for discarding decades-old precedents that have become central to the American way of life, said \uc2 Paul Schiff Berman\uc2 , a professor at \uc2 George Washington University Law School\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe logic of Justice Alito\u8217\'20\'19s opinion, as the dissent pointed out, would absolutely threaten the constitutional legitimacy of all constitutional privacy rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Berman said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt goes against the institutional obligation to respect precedent. And it also goes against, as Chief Justice Roberts pointed out in his opinion, the principle that you don\u8217\'20\'19t decide in a given case, more than you have to resolve in that case.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Berman expressed concern that the Dobbs decision could weaken public trust in the supreme court, which has already been waning in recent years. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/394103/confidence-supreme-court-sinks-historic-low.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Gallup poll}}}\uc2  taken this month, only 25% of US adults say they have \u8220\'20\'1Ca great deal\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cquite a lot\u8221\'20\'1D of confidence in the supreme court. That is the lowest reading in Gallup\u8217\'20\'19s nearly 50-year history of polling public perception of the court.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think this opinion reflects the fact that a radical faction of the supreme court is moving in a maximalist direction, despite the fact that the American people as a whole are becoming increasingly progressive on this issue,\u8221\'20\'1D Berman said.\par \par \uc2 For the millions of Americans dismayed by the reversal of Roe, they have few options to change the composition of the court in the near future. Justices are appointed to lifelong terms, and the three conservative judges confirmed during \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett \u8211\'20\'13 are all under 60.\par \par \uc2 Democratic lawmakers are instead looking at legislative ways to protect Americans\u8217\'20\'19 fundamental rights, and demands for action will probably only intensify now that Roe has been overturned.\par \par \uc2 The House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , issued an urgent demand for Americans to support Democrats in the midterm elections this November, thus giving them an opportunity to codify the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into federal law and protect other crucial freedoms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTermination of pregnancy is just the opening act,\u8221\'20\'1D Pelosi said on Friday. \u8220\'20\'1CA woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose, reproductive freedom is on the ballot in November. We cannot allow [Republicans] to take charge so that they can institute their goal, which is to criminalize reproductive freedom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As we fight to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal at the federal level, I continue to reject the legitimacy of such an undemocratic institution.\par \par \uc2 Expand the court.\u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  (@IlhanMN) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/IlhanMN/status/1540354357664808960?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 24, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 But some progressives are looking beyond legislation to significant reform of the court itself. Immediately after the decision in Dobbs was announced, a number of progressives reiterated their calls to expand the court, which would allow Democrats to confirm more liberal justices.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs we fight to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal at the federal level, I continue to reject the legitimacy of such an undemocratic institution,\u8221\'20\'1D the progressive congresswoman \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2  said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CExpand the court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As of now, Democrats do not have the votes in the Senate needed to expand the court. That could change after November, if the American people decide to give Democrats the chance to do so.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220624ei6o002jv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
