\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I was worried I had been permanently mutilated\u8217\'20\'19: what  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  was like before Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 As told to Emine Saner \par \uc2 1,462 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 15:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In 1968, Ann Hill discovered she was pregnant while a law student. With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal, she was forced to have a backstreet operation. She explains how it inspired her to become a women\u8217\'20\'19s rights campaigner\par \par \uc2 When the doctor told me I was pregnant, he said most women were \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely happy\u8221\'20\'1D with this news. I said: \u8220\'20\'1CWell, I\u8217\'20\'19m not, and I would like to end this pregnancy.\u8221\'20\'1D He told me I would have to go before a committee at the hospital to determine whether a termination was necessary to save my life, including a psychiatrist\u8217\'20\'19s report, and that it would take several weeks. And what would be the chance they would agree? \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t help you,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 It was 1968 and I was 22, living in New Haven, Connecticut, and in my first month at Yale Law School. I knew straight away that I was not going to carry a pregnancy to term, that I was very early on in the pregnancy and that it should not be a major procedure.\par \par \uc2 Somehow, I got the name and number of one of the most well-known doctors in the area who performed abortions illegally, Nathan Rappaport. A man with a deep gravelly voice told me that it would be $500 upfront, and scheduled the procedure for a Saturday morning, so I didn\u8217\'20\'19t miss any law school classes. A friend lent me the money and I went with another friend, Steve, to Rappaport\u8217\'20\'19s apartment in a brownstone on New York\u8217\'20\'19s Upper West Side.\par \par \uc2 Growing up knowing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was illegal meant terrible fear about pregnancy. In high school, one of our most popular classmates was immediately deleted from the class when she got pregnant by one of the school\u8217\'20\'19s leading athletes (of course, it didn\u8217\'20\'19t affect him at all). She missed most of her senior year, and then the child was taken away and given up for adoption.\par \par \uc2 I tried to be careful not to get pregnant. When I found out I was, it was just this sinking feeling: what am I going to do? I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t believe it was happening. My whole life, my whole future was in jeopardy at that moment. I thought that the doctor would understand that, but he didn\u8217\'20\'19t, or he pretended that he didn\u8217\'20\'19t. It was just an awful, nauseating feeling.\par \par \uc2 I was nervous \u8211\'20\'13 you do not go for medical procedures at an apartment\par \par \uc2 It looked like a home: you first walked into a living area, where Steve had to stay, then I was taken into a room that looked like a dentist\u8217\'20\'19s office, which had a chair with stirrups. I was nervous \u8211\'20\'13 you do not go for medical procedures at an apartment \u8211\'20\'13 but it was just a matter of immediacy. Get it done, and then I\u8217\'20\'19ll breathe again. I was frightened about the procedure, but I knew I was doing the right thing. Fortunately, I hadn\u8217\'20\'19t known beforehand that he had been imprisoned for manslaughter, after a woman whom he had given \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to had died.\par \par \uc2 When I came round from the anaesthesia \u8211\'20\'13 I think he had given me ether \u8211\'20\'13 still groggy and trying to deal with everything, he foisted legal papers on me about his case. He knew I was a law student and wanted me to help him overturn his conviction and get his medical licence back.\par \par \uc2 I took the documents and left. I felt relief, but I was also in a lot of pain. Back in my dorm room at Yale, I was bleeding. I was worried about what would happen \u8211\'20\'13 was I going to bleed to death? I hadn\u8217\'20\'19t given much thought to the safety of it before because I knew I was going to do it. It may have been denial on my part. But that weekend, dealing with the pain and bleeding, I worried I had been permanently mutilated or would die.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, I went back to the original doctor I had seen, and told him I\u8217\'20\'19d had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He told me that he could admit me to the hospital to be \u8220\'20\'1Ccleaned out\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 that it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t illegal. Fortunately, the pain and bleeding stopped after a few days; I think he gave me antibiotics, concerned about infection.\par \par \uc2 I became very angry, realising that this was happening to me because I was a woman, so didn\u8217\'20\'19t have rights in this society\par \par \uc2 After I spent that weekend worried that I might die, I became very angry, realising that this was happening to me because I was a woman, so didn\u8217\'20\'19t have rights in this society. I focused immediately on doing something about it, turning pain and anger into action. I signed up with the Connecticut branch of \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2  to write a brief on the unconstitutionality of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and did a survey of obstetrician-gynecologists in Connecticut about their opinions on abortions. We found that, even in the most Catholic state in the country, the majority favoured reform of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, if not a repeal of it.\par \par \uc2 My experience led me to get involved with women\u8217\'20\'19s liberations groups, and we started talking about our abortions. Other than with my roommate, and the friend who had accompanied me to Rappaport, I hadn\u8217\'20\'19t talked about it. I believed that it was not something that I should be sharing because I was in law school, and I had done something illegal, and could be kicked out. Also, I didn\u8217\'20\'19t know at that point what anyone\u8217\'20\'19s reaction would be, because nobody ever talked about it. Everybody knew somebody who knew somebody, who could find the number of someone who could do an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but no one talked about it otherwise. You never talked about how awful it was that you would be forced to produce a baby.\par \par \uc2 We formed a group, Women vs Connecticut. We enlisted thousands of women, including health professionals, to sue Connecticut in federal court and get our rights to do what we wanted with our bodies. We were successful in the case, Abele v Markle, that preceded Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 In 1974, not long after Roe, I had a now legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the difference was night and day. It was performed by Dr Virginia Stuermer, one of my heroes and a pioneer in women\u8217\'20\'19s health. By then, I was married, and I was starting to set up the Connecticut Women\u8217\'20\'19s Education and Legal Fund, the first public interest law firm for women\u8217\'20\'19s rights in Connecticut, which demanded commitment from me 24/7.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s never been easy for poor women in this country to assert their rights fully. It\u8217\'20\'19s brutal\par \par \uc2 This time, I had a friend holding my hand and it was in a sterile setting. It didn\u8217\'20\'19t require hospitalisation; it was a short procedure, and not one that caused any great pain or bleeding afterwards. It was incredible that, in those short years between the two abortions, it could be so different. Now, with medication, women can be full members of society, knowing that if we get pregnant we have humane, inexpensive and safe ways of terminating a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 I am obviously very upset by the Roe v Wade decision. What has always bothered me is that it is going to disproportionately affect poor women \u8211\'20\'13 those who can\u8217\'20\'19t travel, or buy the pills that will become illegal. It\u8217\'20\'19s why we brought the lawsuit Maher v Roe, against the Connecticut department of social services. Once we stymied them from preventing women from getting abortions, they made it virtually impossible for poor women to get abortions in Connecticut by cutting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  payments out of Medicaid. So we sued them for that.\par \par \uc2 At that point, I was a legal services attorney and I represented one poor woman after another. We made it to the supreme court, but were told that a state could decide what it is going to do with its welfare resources. Then {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/may/28/joe-biden-abortion-hyde-amendment-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Hyde amendment}}}\uc2  came in, prohibiting the use of federal funds for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , so it\u8217\'20\'19s never been easy for poor women in this country to assert their rights fully. It\u8217\'20\'19s brutal.\par \par \uc2 I am so happy when I read about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/feb/22/colombia-legalises-abortion-in-move-celebrated-as-historic-victory-by-campaigners"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 women in Colombia}}}\uc2 , and other countries fighting hard to get restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  eliminated, and winning. I console myself with the notion that this is the last gasp of the white male trying to exercise control over women of all colours, races and classes. Why do they not understand that women are not vessels? Women are human beings.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220624ei6o002ec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex