\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 It isn\u8217\'20\'19t just the US:  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  barriers in Britain are forcing women to travel miles for treatment\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Connolly \par \uc2 1,220 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 June 2022\par 09:17 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In Scotland and Northern Ireland, access to second-trimester abortions still depends on a damaging postcode lottery\par \par \uc2 When a draft {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court decision}}}\uc2  was leaked in May showing that judges intended to overturn Roe v Wade, many in the UK reacted with outrage. Rightly so: such a law change would leave \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/03/upshot/abortion-united-states-roe-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 individual states}}}\uc2 . Rights groups estimate that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could become illegal in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/03/upshot/abortion-united-states-roe-wade.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about half the states}}}\uc2  if this is successful. Americans would be forced to travel to states where it was still legal, or order costly \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medicine online, risking severe legal consequences by doing so. Overturning Roe v Wade would probably also inspire anti-choice campaign groups to pursue legal action elsewhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 It can be easy, in the UK, to look to the US with horror, while overlooking the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access issues closer to home. We have a habit of importing American discourse into the UK. It\u8217\'20\'19s understandable: the states are so huge and culturally influential that everything that happens there can feel weighted with great significance and the potential for a kind of butterfly effect. The issues with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the UK are different, and less drastic than a full legal overhaul. But they are nonetheless significant. Practical difficulties with access have a major impact on the lives and choices of people in Scotland and Northern Ireland.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month Lucy Grieve, the co-founder of Back Off Scotland, wrote an article for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scotsman.com/health/the-scottish-government-is-failing-women-when-it-comes-to-abortion-lucy-grieve-3720095"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Scotsman}}}\uc2  outlining the need to travel from Scotland to England for second-trimester abortions, since no health board in Scotland provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care up to the longstanding legal limit of 24 weeks. This is not widely publicised (Grieve only found out by interviewing people for her work on buffer zones, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/uk-61624480"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legally protected spaces}}}\uc2  implemented around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics to deter protesters from harassing clients). She was surprised at how many described journeying to England for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Grieve found that people were having to travel as far as Bournemouth (around eight hours from Glasgow by train), and that 170 Scottish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clients had been referred by their doctors to travel to England for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 2019. The real number may be higher, since some women may organise their abortions by contacting English services directly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was very surprised to find that in Scotland there is no health board that provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care up to the legal limit of 24 weeks,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me. In some health board areas she found the service stopped significantly short of this too. In Fife, for example, it is 15 weeks and five days. Travelling a substantial distance for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  adds an unnecessary layer of practical complication to the procedure, from organising time off work to recovering away from home or while on the return trip. It can also create a psychological barrier, making the procedure seem more drastic than it otherwise would.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSomething that echoes across the experiences I\u8217\'20\'19ve heard is that this care requires a real support network,\u8221\'20\'1D Grieve said. \u8220\'20\'1CEven having somebody that can travel with you and support you through the procedure.\u8221\'20\'1D The \uc2 NHS\uc2  pays for people to travel for abortions, although Grieve spoke to some who had not been aware of this at the time and so paid themselves. Any accompanying friends or family members must fund themselves. During the pandemic, which made travelling across the country or staying in a hotel far more difficult, the number of people travelling to England for abortions fell by about a half, she said.\par \par \uc2 In Northern Ireland \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was decriminalised in October 2019. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services have still not been commissioned, as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsletter.co.uk/health/abortion-northern-ireland-pro-choice-groups-demand-health-minister-robin-swann-commissions-abortion-services-now-3701631"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 health minister, Robin Swann}}}\uc2 , has refused to comply. Last month the Northern Ireland secretary, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/article/britain-require-abortion-northern-ireland-dup-opposition/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brandon Lewis}}}\uc2 , announced he would intervene if this continued. One DUP MP, Carla Lockhart, responded by saying Lewis wanted to \u8220\'20\'1Cmake Northern Ireland one of the most dangerous places in Europe to be a child in the womb, especially if that little baby has a disability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Naomi Connor of Alliance for Choice, a group that campaigns for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in Northern Ireland for women, trans men and non-binary people, explained that early medical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 10 weeks was the only thing available within Northern Ireland in the meantime. Even this service is provided on a patchy basis, dependent on healthcare districts and reliant on the dedication of medical professionals. The majority of those who need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later than 10 weeks are still travelling to England. Again, their transport is paid for, but that of any accompanying person is not.\par \par \uc2 Connor said the Department of Health\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to provide a central website with information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in Northern Ireland had left a void filled by anti-choice groups. The top \uc2 Google\uc2  result for \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  NI\u8221\'20\'1D is an anti-choice group presenting itself as a medical advice provider. \u8220\'20\'1CRoutinely we are seeing women who have been in contact with Stanton (an anti-choice group), for the first few weeks of their pregnancy, without realising they are an anti-choice group,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 An \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 20 weeks is more medically complex than one at five, and recovery times are likely to be longer, making travel more arduous. Some fetal anomalies are only detected at the 20-week scan; Connor pointed out the irony that some of these emotive cases helped in the campaign for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Northern Ireland, but second-trimester \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still not available.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  hierarchy\u8221\'20\'1D, which deems only certain abortions (say for medical or financial reasons) morally acceptable is pervasive, even among liberals, but can be used to campaign for access. People who don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that everyone has the right to bodily autonomy may be persuaded by cases where a medical condition effectively forces someone\u8217\'20\'19s decision. \u8220\'20\'1CWe do not believe in an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  hierarchy, but later-stage abortions can be more complex,\u8221\'20\'1D Connor said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe more complex pregnancies should really be travelling the least.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Travel can have a huge psychological impact on people seeking abortions. A medical procedure becomes a multi-day, often clandestine and lonely event in an unfamiliar setting, and therefore becomes all the more memorable. The outcry in the UK over both of these access issues has been muted. Perhaps because practical difficulties such as these are not as headline-grabbing as a plan to overturn Roe v Wade. Perhaps because there is some latent discomfort with, or judgment of, those who seek second-trimester abortions. Even people who identify as pro-choice seem to frequently qualify this stance by, say, treating \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as something primarily necessitated by dire financial circumstances or focusing on fringe cases involving extreme health risks. These are valid reasons, of course, but it is equally valid for a pregnant person to simply not want to have a baby. Access to medical care should not depend on a postcode lottery.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rachel Connolly is a London-based journalist from Belfast\par \par \uc2 