\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Republican states trying to ban  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  expand health benefits for new mothers\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,009 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 June 2022\par 21:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bills with benefits for low-income mothers don\u8217\'20\'19t counteract bans that would lead to higher risks for maternal mortality, say experts\par \par \uc2 A number of Republican-led states that are moving to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are, at the same time, extending health insurance benefits to new mothers, professing to support \u8220\'20\'1Cwomen in crisis\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  prepares to rule on national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, many Republican states are seeking severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that would force many women to carry pregnancies to term, likely worsening the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/feb/23/us-maternal-deaths-mortality-black-mothers-covid#:~:text=Overall%20in%202020%2C%20there%20were,the%20rate%20for%20white%20people."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US maternal mortality crisis}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some of those same lawmakers are now passing bills that extend Medicaid benefits to low-income mothers, typically for one year after they give birth rather than the current two months.\par \par \uc2 Arizona, Florida, Tennessee and Texas have all extended health benefits for low-income mothers in recent months, and Alabama and Georgia have both moved to implement such extensions, according to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/medicaid/issue-brief/medicaid-postpartum-coverage-extension-tracker/#note-0-4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kaiser Family Foundation}}}\uc2 . All have also sought to impose severe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions or bans.\par \par \uc2 Although expanding pregnancy-related health coverage is \u8220\'20\'1Ca win in many ways\u8221\'20\'1D, it does not counteract the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8220\'20\'1Cwould certainly lead to higher risks for maternal morbidity or mortality,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sarah Blake, an associate professor of health policy at \uc2 Emory University\uc2  in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Blake said Georgia lawmakers are in \u8220\'20\'1CJekyll and Hyde mode\u8221\'20\'1D. Even as advocates for maternal health, herself included, are \u8220\'20\'1Cvery happy\u8221\'20\'1D for the extension, she said, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe know the state is very against women and their access to full scope reproductive health services\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Changes to postpartum benefits come as the supreme court is expected to rule in the coming days on a critical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2 . A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/biden-democrats-reaction-codifying-roe-v-wade-reform-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft opinion}}}\uc2  showed conservative justices are on the verge of ending federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections. If that happened, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/05/abortion-deserts-clinics-access-closed-map"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states}}}\uc2  would be certain or likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt shows you how [Republicans] are not operating in good faith,\u8221\'20\'1D said Loretta Ross, an associate professor at \uc2 Smith College\uc2  in Georgia and a reproductive justice activist. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they really cared about maternal mortality they\u8217\'20\'19d reduce the causes of maternal mortality \u8211\'20\'13 and it goes way beyond Medicaid expansion.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 State legislators in both Democrat- and Republican-led states have made changes to Medicaid, a federal and state partnership that provides health insurance for the poor and disabled, to take advantage of a provision of federal pandemic aid which streamlined postpartum benefit changes.\par \par \uc2 However, the most dramatic effects would be in Republican-led states, where lawmakers have long refused to expand the program to more low-income people.\par \par \uc2 The refusal is a legacy of Obamacare debates. Around the time the Affordable Care Act, better known as Obamacare, Republicans sued to stop the expansion of Medicaid. They succeeded in the supreme court, and each state was forced to adopt Medicaid expansion individually.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/medicaid/issue-brief/status-of-state-medicaid-expansion-decisions-interactive-map/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dozen states}}}\uc2 , mostly in the US south, still refuse to do so, even though the federal government would pay 90% of the costs of expanding the program.\par \par \uc2 Expanding Medicaid only to postpartum women appears to be a way for Republican-led states to champion their aid to a sympathetic group, even as they oppose broader Medicaid expansion and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Ross.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, for example \u8211\'20\'13 a state where one-quarter of women of reproductive age {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2022/05/09/texas-abortion-law-medicaid-contraception/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lack health insurance}}}\uc2 , the highest rate in the nation, and which is among the 10 worst states for maternal mortality \u8211\'20\'13 lawmakers recently expanded Medicaid to pregnant patients for six months after giving birth, instead of two.\par \par \uc2 A single adult supporting two children cannot earn more than $2,760 a year and qualify for Medicaid \u8211\'20\'13 unless they are pregnant, in which case they can earn up to $45,600 a year and qualify. However, the exemption previously lasted just 60 days after birth \u8211\'20\'13 the federal minimum \u8211\'20\'13 after which most people become uninsured once again.\par \par \uc2 The expansion to six months is welcome but insufficient, said Erika Ramirez, director of policy and advocacy for the Texas Women\u8217\'20\'19s Healthcare Coalition: \u8220\'20\'1CWe got six, which is better than nothing \u8230\'20\'26 [But] over and over, [the] top recommendation [from maternal health experts] has been for women to get postpartum care for a full 12 months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When the legislation was signed, Texas state senator Lois Kolkhorst, who sponsored it, described the Medicaid extension as a \u8220\'20\'1Cbipartisan effort to help keep Texas moms healthy and provide the care needed to avoid preventable deaths and illnesses\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yet just two days before the bill passed in the Senate, Kolkhorst and her Republican colleagues passed a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which is expected to go into effect immediately if the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ends federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections. It is one of 26 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions Kolkhorst voted for in her 11-year career as a legislator.\par \par \uc2 A similar effort to simultaneously extend Medicaid to pregnant women and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is under way in Alabama. There, one of the most {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aptv.org/watch/capitol-journal/march-9-2022-i5maip/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vocal proponents}}}\uc2  of expansion is Republican state representative Debbie Wood, who also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.valleytimes-news.com/2022/04/the-race-is-on-rep-debbie-wood-and-micah-messer-discuss-stances-on-issues/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposes \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  in nearly all circumstances, including rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 Kolkhorst could not be reached for comment and Wood did not respond to requests for an interview.\par \par \uc2 In Tennessee, the Republican governor, Bill Lee, directly connected the state\u8217\'20\'19s postpartum Medicaid expansion and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.actionnews5.com/2022/05/05/gov-lee-responds-question-tennessees-roe-v-wade-trigger-law/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a press conference in May}}}\uc2 , he spoke about Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D ban, a law that will allow the state to immediately ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the supreme court ends federal protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe lives of unborn children \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s very important that we protect the lives of them,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s also important that we recognize that women in crisis need support and assistance through this process. For example, that\u8217\'20\'19s why we\u8217\'20\'19ve expanded our postpartum coverage for women in TennCare.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis kind of strategy pits needy people against each other,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ross. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re supposed to advocate for postpartum women to get Medicaid, and screw everybody else who needs it? It\u8217\'20\'19s a classic divide and conquer strategy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220620ei6k000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
