\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 Will Black churches throw their weight behind reproductive rights?\b0\par\par\uc2 Carlisa N Johnson \par \uc2 1,277 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2022\par 19:25 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The disproportionate impact of impending bans may dislodge a longstanding ambivalence over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Ebenezer Baptist Church, once home to Reverend Dr \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 , once dedicated a portion of its Mother\u8217\'20\'19s Day service in mid-May to addressing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US. During the service in Atlanta, senior pastor and US senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  focused on a biblical story about a mother who found herself in debt after the death of her husband.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo be anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not make you pro-life. Especially if you will not stand up for the lives of our children once they are here,\u8221\'20\'1D said Warnock.\par \par \uc2 The Black church has long been a place for organizing and collective political power for Black communities in the US, from abolition in the 19th century to the civil rights movement of the 1960s. After the leak of the draft supreme court opinion that could overturn Roe v Wade, opinions are split about whether this longstanding symbol of Black political power extends to the fight for reproductive justice.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, advocates and allies have been pointing to the devastating impact the ruling, which would strip the protective right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , could have on the community, especially in the south. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/70/ss/ss7009a1.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Black women in the US receive abortions at the highest rate in the nation}}}\uc2  and in Georgia and Alabama alone, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/state-indicator/abortions-by-race/?currentTimeframe=0&sortModel=%7B%22colId%22:%22Location%22,%22sort%22:%22asc%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Black women receive more than }}}\uc2  60% of each state\u8217\'20\'19s total legal abortions. Balancing the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access with strong religious influences in the deep south, however, can prove delicate: while {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/fact-sheet/public-opinion-on-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 views on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  access are favorable}}}\uc2  throughout the country, sentiments among the Black religious communities remain varied.\par \par \uc2 While Warnock had used the story to discuss the material conditions for equitable social services like education, healthcare and public safety, he also took time to name the debate and its implications explicitly.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the supreme court seems poised to force its will upon women and a whole range of circumstances, perhaps we ought to focus our energy and our public policy on mothers who are just trying to make it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Toni Bond, an ethics and theology scholar who was one of 12 Black women who coined the phrase \u8220\'20\'1Creproductive justice\u8221\'20\'1D in 1994, said Black faith leaders need to make their voices heard in the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe definitely need to heighten our work at the intersections of religion, faith and reproductive justice,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bond in the recent virtual roundtable held by non-profit SisterReach. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been afraid to talk about that, so we have basically allowed anti-choice conservative fundamentalist folks [to lead the conversation].\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to start talking about this as an issue of systemic racism, and white supremacy, and neoliberalism and capitalism,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not about wanting to save the unborn lives of Bipoc folk, but wanting to use this as a way to increase the birthrates of white people who are afraid that Bipoc folks are going to take over this country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But while some churches are pointing towards a more transparent stance on the subject, others aren\u8217\'20\'19t discussing it at all.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been mentioned at my church,\u8221\'20\'1D says Deborah McKinney, a member of Abyssinia Baptist Church in Norfolk, Virginia. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t know if the church should be taking a hard stance, but what I do know is that things like voter\u8217\'20\'19s rights and civil rights were birthed in the Black church. So, had we continued these conversations in the church, people would have a clearer understanding of what is going on and then make better decisions when it\u8217\'20\'19s time to head to the polls.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This lack of political engagement makes some view progress on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as a far-off reality. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that part of it is the issue of what reproductive justice has always been associated with,\u8221\'20\'1D said Candice Benbow, a theologian raised in the Black Baptist church. \u8220\'20\'1CThis sexuality that has been demonized. So, while you have some Black faith spaces that believe reproductive justice matters, we still have a very long way to go when it comes to educating people in the church on all the ways Roe v. Wade is necessary for women\u8217\'20\'19s health.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 According to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2022/05/06/americas-abortion-quandary/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study by the Pew Research Center}}}\uc2 , Black protestants believe abortions should be legal at rates nearly double that of White Christians. Still, Black Christians like Benbow point to a culture supported by strict interpretations of the Bible within their churches to explain the lack of support for expanded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s this mindset that if you\u8217\'20\'19re following the word [of God], then you don\u8217\'20\'19t need Roe v Wade,\u8221\'20\'1D said Benbow.\par \par \uc2 If the conversations within Black churches cease to move forward, many Black women could shoulder the weight of the impact.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m really concerned about the deep south because a large majority of African Americans live in the deep south, and too large a majority of them are low income,\u8221\'20\'1D said Loretta Ross, a pioneer in the reproductive justice movement. \u8220\'20\'1CI am really concerned with what they need to do to access [\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] services and make sure that they ultimately don\u8217\'20\'19t die because we have this trifecta of harm with lack of birth control, quality sex education, and now this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Ross remains optimistic about the power of Black coalition building throughout the south yielding networks of advocates and practitioners creating pathways to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Organizations like SisterSong, SisterReach and ARC Southeast that have fought to streamline reproductive care for Black women in the south say they have been preparing for this moment for decades.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have an opportunity to be in, what is, an absolute reformation time. And what we are going to have to do is change and shift this atmosphere, so religion does not fall on the wrong side of everything that comes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bishop Yvette Flunder, senior pastor of the City of Refuge United Church of Christ in Oakland, California, at the virtual roundtable hosted by SisterReach.\par \par \uc2 To fully address reproductive rights at the intersection of race and religion, reproductive justice advocates believe that the material conditions shaping the experiences of Black communities must be addressed. Black women are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/healthequity/features/maternal-mortality/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three times more likely}}}\uc2  to die from pregnancy-related complications than their white counterparts within the US. According to the CDC, disparities in standard of care, structural racism and implicit bias are leading contributors. And these statistics extend beyond maternal care to broader inequities within healthcare systems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBasic needs, practical needs, eating, clothing, housing, employment, being impacted by violence is just as important,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reverend Cherisse Scott, CEO and Founder of SisterReach. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to think of how we seize this moment as a way forward to not abandon people but to continue to center the full breadth and depth of their lived experiences.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The culture within Black churches of the south preach a liberation gospel that calls out inequities surrounding class, accessibility, and most notably race, which makes the Black church\u8217\'20\'19s stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access all the more complicated. \u8220\'20\'1CI have heard in the last few weeks about how different conversations are happening in the Black church now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ross. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause of the different intersections of Roe with racism, I think [the church] is being urged to speak on it not because of the gendered implications, but instead because of the racial implications.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve dislodged a boulder, and now if you don\u8217\'20\'19t look out, you might get crushed by the avalanche,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ross. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a chance for the Black church to provide more support and less judgment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220614ei6d002y0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
