\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Jan 6 updates: Garland says he\u8217\'20\'19s watching hearings as pressure mounts to charge Trump \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Stein in Washington \par \uc2 7,346 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 June 2022\par 20:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 DoJ faces pressure to bring charges against Donald TrumpPanel wraps up after hearing testimony on Capitol attackInebriated Giuliani urged Trump to claim win, panel hearsSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee\u8217\'20\'19s second public hearing was today\u8217\'20\'19s main story, as it aired testimony from several of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s top advisors, all of whom said they told the former president there was no fraud in the 2020 election that would change the result of his loss to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Trump pressed on with making the claims, which the committee said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62a76a678f083046f4fbd1b8#block-62a76a678f083046f4fbd1b8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fueled the violence at the Capitol}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 * Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62a790f18f0841aa78d9aebf#block-62a790f18f0841aa78d9aebf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the chamber will vote on a bipartisan gun control bill as soon as it\u8217\'20\'19s written}}}\uc2 . The compromise measure doesn\u8217\'20\'19t go as far as Democrats would like, but represents the best chance to pass legislation at the federal level in response to the mass shootings in Buffalo, New York and Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 * The supreme court released five opinions that dealt with a number of aspects of federal law, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?page=with:block-62a774188f0841aa78d9ada1#block-62a774188f0841aa78d9ada1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 though none of the verdicts were in any of the major cases touching on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , gun rights or other hot-button issues.\par \par \uc2 * Attorney General Merrick Garland{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62a78d8d8f0841aa78d9ae91#block-62a78d8d8f0841aa78d9ae91"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said he is watching the hearings of the January 6 committee}}}\uc2 , as the justice department comes under pressure to bring charges against Trump.\par \par \uc2 * Separately, Capitol Police Officer Eugene Goodman, who was hailed for leading rioters away from the senate chamber, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/man-carried-confederate-flag-capitol-heads-trial-85352192"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 testified in the criminal trial}}}\uc2  of two men facing charges in the attack.\par \par \uc2 The blog is wrapping up for the day and will return on Tuesday morning around 9am ET. For updates on Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine, please tune into our global live blog on the war, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/jun/13/russia-ukraine-war-russia-committed-war-crimes-in-kharkiv-amnesty-alleges-evacuation-routes-destroyed-in-sievierodonetsk-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 At the White House daily media briefing, press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  has reiterated in response to a question that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to leave the topic of whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will be prosecuted over the January 6 hearing \u8220\'20\'1Cup to the Department of Justice\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The White House wants \u8220\'20\'1CAmericans to watch\u8221\'20\'1D the January 6 hearings, the second of which occurred this morning, \u8220\'20\'1Cand remember the horrors of one of the darkest days in our history\u8221\'20\'1D but the US president will stay away from commenting on related prosecutions.\par \par \uc2 He chose US attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause of his loyalty to the law\u8221\'20\'1D, Jean-Pierre said, and also \u8220\'20\'1Cto restore the independence and integrity of the Department of Justice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a dig at how the DoJ was regarded by Democrats as an extension of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s White House and under his sway instead of staying independent.\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  calls on a reporter during a press briefing at the White House today. Photograph: Patrick Semansky/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in New York, an ongoing sell off on Wall Street has pushed the S&P 500 into a bear market, meaning a loss of 20 percent from its most recent high.\par \par \uc2 The stock market\u8217\'20\'19s health and wider economy\u8217\'20\'19s health are generally regarded as two different things, but the S&P 500\u8217\'20\'19s nearly four percent loss in today\u8217\'20\'19s trading is fueled in part by concerns that the United State\u8217\'20\'19s decades-high inflation rate will cause a recession. It\u8217\'20\'19s also more bad news for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his economic policies, overshadowing more positive developments such as the drop in unemployment on his watch.\par \par \uc2 From the Associated Press:\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 dropped 3.8% in the first chance for investors to trade after getting the weekend to reflect on the stunning news that inflation is getting worse, not better. The Dow Jones was down 879 points, or 2.8%, at 30,513, as of 11.08am ET, and the Nasdaq composite was 4.5% lower.\par \par \uc2 The center of Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s focus was again on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which is scrambling to get inflation under control. Its main method is to raise interest rates in order to slow the economy, a blunt tool that risks a recession if used too aggressively.\par \par \uc2 Some traders are even speculating the Fed on Wednesday may raise its key short-term interest rate by three-quarters of a percentage point. That\u8217\'20\'19s triple the usual amount and something the Fed hasn\u8217\'20\'19t done since 1994. Traders now see a 34% probability of such a mega-hike, up from just 3% a week ago, according to \uc2 CME Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 No one thinks the Fed will stop there, with markets bracing for a continued series of bigger-than-usual hikes. Those would come on top of some already discouraging signals about the economy and corporate profits, including a record-low preliminary reading on consumer sentiment that was soured by high gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/13/wall-street-stock-markets-bear-market"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 S&P 500 sinks into bear-market territory as recession fears pound US stocks}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Schumer vows gun violence bill vote 'as soon as possible'\par \par \uc2 Senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said he\u8217\'20\'19ll bring {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/12/us-senators-bipartisan-gun-control-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a recent bipartisan gun control bill to a vote on the chamber\u8217\'20\'19s floor as soon as it\u8217\'20\'19s written}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will put this bill on the floor as soon as possible, once the text of the final agreement is finalized so the Senate can act quickly to make gun safety reform a reality,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer said in a speech in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYesterday\u8217\'20\'19s agreement does not have everything Democrats wanted but it nevertheless represents the most significant reform to gun safety laws that we have seen in decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democratic and and Republican lawmakers have been trying to find a common ground on the highly controversial topic of gun control following a recent spate of mass shootings in Uvalde, Texas and Buffalo, New York.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 Attorney General Garland says he's watching January 6 hearings\par \par \uc2 Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said he and his prosecutors are watching the hearings of the January 6 committee as the justice department faces pressure to bring charges against former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrNEW: AG \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  says he\u8217\'20\'19s watching the Jan. 6 committee hearings, adding \u8220\'20\'1CI can assure you the January 6 prosecutors are watching the hearings as well\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Hugo Lowell (@hugolowell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hugolowell/status/1536417865217323008?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 13, 2022}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/12/capitol-attack-panel-urge-doj-consider-criminal-charges-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Some of the lawmakers on the committee have called for Garland to levy criminal charges against Trump}}}\uc2 . The former president is at the center of an array of investigations, including an inquiry into his business practices in New York. He will testify under oath in that probe on 15 July, along with his daughter \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  and son \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/08/donald-trump-testify-investigation-children-new-york-ag"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump to testify in New York investigation into his business practices}}}\par \par \uc2 Garland answered reporters questions during a DoJ press conference about gun trafficking.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , the former New York mayor who was one of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s top attorneys near the end of his term, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/13/giuliani-inebriated-trump-falsely-claim-victory-election-night"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has denied he was drunk on election night in 2020}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltrGiuliani's attorney says Giuliani was not drunk on election night. \u8220\'20\'1CGiuliani denies all falsehoods by the angry and misguided Ms Cheney," Robert Costello tells CNN. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lsOdoaOgvv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/lsOdoaOgvv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kara Scannell (@KaraScannell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KaraScannell/status/1536406281975738374?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 While the latest report of Giuliani being drunk in public came from today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing of the January 6 committee, such claims are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/rudy-giuliani-alcoholic-911-interview-b1909838.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/story/2018/05/08/giuliani-trump-lawyer-insulting-576749"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  will soon start {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9NV4YVFTmuo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 her daily briefing to reporters}}}\uc2 , and there\u8217\'20\'19s a chance she\u8217\'20\'19ll be asked about {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/11/us/politics/biden-2024-election-democrats.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 this story}}}\uc2  from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The piece asks a provocative question: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://projects.fivethirtyeight.com/biden-approval-rating/?cid=rrpromo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 given his low approval ratings}}}\uc2 , among other issues, should Biden not run in 2024? The president says he will stand again, but the article features a trickle of Democratic voices questioning the wisdom of that idea, or even outright telling him not to.\par \par \uc2 As the Times reported:\par \par \uc2 As the challenges facing the nation mount and fatigued base voters show low enthusiasm, Democrats in union meetings, the back rooms of Capitol Hill and party gatherings from coast to coast are quietly worrying about Mr. Biden\u8217\'20\'19s leadership, his age and his capability to take the fight to former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  a second time.\par \par \uc2 Interviews with nearly 50 Democratic officials, from county leaders to members of Congress, as well as with disappointed voters who backed Mr. Biden in 2020, reveal a party alarmed about Republicans\u8217\'20\'19 rising strength and extraordinarily pessimistic about an immediate path forward.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo say our country was on the right track would flagrantly depart from reality,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Simeonidis, a \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  member from Miami. Mr. Biden, he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cshould announce his intent not to seek re-election in \u8217\'20\'1924 right after the midterms.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democratic stalwart Howard Dean has perhaps the sharpest criticism in the piece, though it\u8217\'20\'19s not aimed at Biden alone:\par \par \uc2 Howard Dean, the 73-year-old former Vermont governor and \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  chairman who ran for president in 2004, has long called for a younger generation of leaders in their 30s and 40s to rise in the party. He said he had voted for \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 , 40, in the 2020 primary after trying to talk Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , 48, of Connecticut into running.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe generation after me is just a complete trash heap,\u8221\'20\'1D Mr. Dean said.\par \par \uc2 The United States is indeed led by elderly people these days, as Axios reports in a closer look at the subject that\u8217\'20\'19s fittingly titled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/06/13/american-gerontocracy-biden-trump-age"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmerican gerontocracy\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Diversity and technology are making the workplace, home life and culture unrecognizable for many older leaders. That can leave geriatric leadership of government out of step with everyday life in America \u8212\'20\'14 and disconnected from the voters who give them power.\par \par \uc2 Washington is run by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/politics-policy/joe-biden"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden}}}\uc2 , 79 \u8230\'20\'26 House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , 82 \u8230\'20\'26 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , a comparatively youthful 71 \u8230\'20\'26 and Senate Republican leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , age 80.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 , running the U.S. pandemic response, is 81.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Separate from the January 6 committee hearing, Capitol Police Officer Eugene Goodman was in a federal courtroom describing how one of two defendants facing charges over the attacked jabbed him with a Confederate battle flag.\par \par \uc2 Goodman is one of the most prominent defenders of the Capitol that day, credited with diverting the mob away from the Senate chamber and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/capitol-police-officer-eugene-goodman-1st-interview-jan/story?id=82465552"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 appearing in a well-known photo}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He was testifying at the trial of Kevin Seefried and his adult son Hunter Seefried, whom the Associated Press reported face charges including a felony count of obstruction of an official proceeding.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/capitol-siege-riots-presidential-elections-new-mexico-election-2020-fcb3a9ba822a570f360ce03011ee83dc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to the AP}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Goodman recalled seeing Kevin Seefried standing alone in an archway and telling him to leave. Instead, Seefried cursed at him and jabbed at the officer with the base end of the flagpole three or four times, Goodman said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHe was very angry. Screaming. Talking loudly,\u8221\'20\'1D Goodman said. \u8220\'20\'1CComplete opposite of pleasant.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 U.S. District Judge \uc2 Trevor McFadden\uc2  is hearing testimony without a jury for the Seefrieds\u8217\'20\'19 bench trial, which started Monday. The Seefrieds waived their right to a jury trial, which means McFadden will decide their cases.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 Midday summary\par \par \uc2 Today has been dominated by the latest revelations from the January 6 Committee, which aired testimony from a number of former officials in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s campaign and White House, all of whom told the president the same thing: the 2020 election was not stolen. Nonetheless, Trump pressed on with making the claims, which the committee said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62a76a678f083046f4fbd1b8#block-62a76a678f083046f4fbd1b8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fueled the violence at the Capitol}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what else happened today:\par \par \uc2 * The supreme court released five opinions that dealt with a number of aspects of federal law, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?page=with:block-62a774188f0841aa78d9ada1#block-62a774188f0841aa78d9ada1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 though none of the verdicts were in any of the major cases touching on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , gun rights or other hot-button issues.\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?page=with:block-62a730a28f0841aa78d9aa01#block-62a730a28f0841aa78d9aa01"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The senate reached a compromise on gun rights legislation}}}\uc2  that can hopefully win enough support from both Democrats and Republicans to pass the evenly divided chamber. Further negotiations on the bill are expected in the days to come.\par \par \uc2 * Lawmakers on the January 6 committee {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?page=with:block-62a72f2e8f08f7f9ebefe352#block-62a72f2e8f08f7f9ebefe352"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continued their calls}}}\uc2  for the justice department to bring criminal charges against Trump, saying the evidence they uncovered justifies the move.\par \par \uc2 * Separately, Capitol Police Officer Eugene Goodman, who was hailed for leading rioters away from the senate chamber, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/wireStory/man-carried-confederate-flag-capitol-heads-trial-85352192"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 testified in the criminal trial}}}\uc2  of two men facing charges in the attack.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  has ruled against immigrants who are seeking their release from long periods of detention while they fight deportation orders, the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 In two cases decided on Monday morning, the court said that the immigrants, who fear persecution if sent back to their native countries, have no right under a federal law to a bond hearing at which they could argue for their freedom no matter how long they are held.\par \par \uc2 Protesters gathered outside the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  in Washington, DC, earlier today, awaiting outcome of key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case. In fact, the justices ruled on other cases today. More decisions coming Wednesday and all month. Photograph: Bonnie Cash/UPI/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 The nine justices also ruled 6-3 to limit the immigrants ability to band together in court, an outcome that Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  wrote:\par \par \uc2 Will leave many vulnerable non-citizens unable to protect their rights.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In recent years, the high court has taken an increasingly limited view of immigrants\u8217\'20\'19 access to the federal court system under immigration measures enacted in the 1990s and 2000s.\par \par \uc2 For a while, it seemed like the court was going to push back a bit. In extreme cases, it would interpret a statute to allow for as much judicial review as possible. Clearly now, the court is no longer willing to do that,\u8221\'20\'1Dsaid Nicole Hallet, director of the immigrants rights clinic at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  law school.\par \par \uc2 The immigrants who sued for a bond hearing are facing being detained for many months, even years, before their cases are resolved.\par \par \uc2 The court ruled in the cases of people from Mexico and El Salvador who persuaded Homeland Security officials that their fears are credible, entitling them to further review.\par \par \uc2 Their lawyers argued that they should have a hearing before an immigration judge to determine if they should be released. The main factors are whether people would pose a danger or are likely to flee if set free.\par \par \uc2 Sotomayor wrote the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion in one case involving Antonio Arteaga - Martinez, who had previously been deported to Mexico. He was taken into custody four years ago, and won release while his case wound through the federal courts. His hearing on whether he can remain in the United States is scheduled for 2023.\par \par \uc2 But Sotomayor wrote that the provision of immigration law that applies to people like Arteaga-Martinez simply doesn\u8217\'20\'19t require the government to hold a bond hearing.\par \par \uc2 The court, however, left open the issue of the immigrants\u8217\'20\'19 ability to argue that the Constitution does not permit such indefinite detention without a hearing.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  wrote the court\u8217\'20\'19s opinion holding that federal judges can only rule in the case of the immigrants before them, not a class of similarly situated people.\par \par \uc2 Sotomayor dissented from that decision, joined by Justices \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and Elena Kagan.\par \par \uc2 She wrote that the ability to join together in a class was especially important for people who have no right to a lawyer and \u8220\'20\'1Care disproportionately unlikely to be familiar with the U.S. legal system or fluent in the English language.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The cases are Johnson v Arteaga-Martinez, 19-896, and Garland v Aleman Gonzalez, 20-322.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Supreme Court pose for a group photo at the Supreme Court in Washington, April 23, 2021. Seated from left are \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 . Standing from left are \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , Elena Kagan, \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  and Amy Coney Barrett. Photograph: Erin Schaff/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  issued five opinions this morning, just around the time the January 6 hearing was getting underway. None of them was one of the four big cases being mostly closely watched, on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gun rights, rules on emissions affecting climate change and an immigration issue affecting undocumented people crossing the US-Mexico border in order to claim asylum in the United States, known as Remain in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 In one of the most significant opinions of the day, the nine-judge court ruled that Native Americans prosecuted in certain tribal courts can also be prosecuted based on the same incident in federal court, which can result in longer sentences, the Associated Press writes.\par \par \uc2 The 6-3 ruling is in keeping with an earlier ruling from the 1970s that said the same about a more widely used type of tribal court.\par \par \uc2 The case before the justices involved a Navajo Nation member, Merle Denezpi, accused of rape. He served nearly five months in jail after being charged with assault and battery in what is called a Court of Indian Offenses, a court that deals exclusively with alleged Native American offenders.\par \par \uc2 Under federal law Courts of Indian Offenses can only impose sentences of generally up to a year. Denezpi was later prosecuted in federal court and sentenced to 30 years in prison. He said the Constitution\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CDouble Jeopardy\u8221\'20\'1D clause should have barred the second prosecution.\par \par \uc2 But the justices disagreed.\par \par \uc2 Denezpi\u8217\'20\'19s single act led to separate prosecutions for violations of a tribal ordinance and a federal statute. Because the Tribe and the Federal Government are distinct sovereigns, those offenses are not the same. Denezpi\u8217\'20\'19s second prosecution therefore did not offend the Double Jeopardy Clause,\u8221\'20\'1D the court decided.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/amy-coney-barrett"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett}}}\uc2  , the ultra {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/06/people-of-praise-accused-child-abuse-amy-coney-barrett"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conservative leaning}}}\uc2  associated justice confirmed in the dying days of the Trump administration, wrote the opinion for the majority.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had argued for that result as had several states, which said barring federal prosecutions in similar cases could allow defendants to escape harsh sentences.\par \par \uc2 In a dissent, Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  wrote that the case involved the same \u8220\'20\'1Cdefendant, same crime, same prosecuting authority\u8221\'20\'1D and said the majority\u8217\'20\'19s reasoning was \u8220\'20\'1Cat odds with the text and original meaning of the Constitution.\u8221\'20\'1D The conservative Gorsuch was joined in dissent by two of the court\u8217\'20\'19s three liberal justices, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Justice Elena Kagan.\par \par \uc2 The case before the justices involves a tribal court system that has become increasingly rare over the last century.\par \par \uc2 Courts of Indian Offenses were created in the late 1800s during a period when the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s policy toward Native Americans was to encourage assimilation. Judges and generally prosecutors are appointed by federal officials.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Committee ends for day after Trump official reveals post-January 6 thoughts: 'Are you out of your effing mind?'\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee has ended the day\u8217\'20\'19s testimony by taking viewers back to the scene of the attack and showing how the people who broke in to the Capitol were believers in a conspiracy that many of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s top officials told him was bogus.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know exactly what\u8217\'20\'19s going on right now. Fake election!\u8221\'20\'1D a rioter said in video aired by the committee.\par \par \uc2 The hearing closed with the jarring words of Eric Herschmann, a White House lawyer, who recalled a phone call with John Eastman, another of the president\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/28/john-eastman-trump-lawyer-capitol-attack-emails"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 whom a judge has said conspired with Trump to overturn the election}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI said to him, Are you out of your effing mind?\u8221\'20\'1D Herschmann recalled. \u8220\'20\'1CI said I... only want to hear two words coming out of your mouth for now on: orderly transition.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Before the hearing ended, the committee\u8217\'20\'19s senior investigative counsel Amanda Wick outlined one possible motivation for why Trump stuck with the fraud claims: they were a money-making opportunity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the select committee has demonstrated, the Trump campaign knew these claims of voter fraud were false, yet they continue to barrage small dollar donors with emails encouraging them to donate to something called Official Election Defense Fund. The select committee discovered no such fund existed,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Wick goes on to say much of the $250 million raised for the supposed effort was funneled into a political action committee that made donations to pro-Trump organizations, as well as confidantes like his chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . The barrage of fundraising emails to supporters \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinued through January 6, even as President Trump spoke on the ellipse. Thirty minutes after the last fundraising email was sent, the Capitol was breached,\u8221\'20\'1D Wick said.\par \par \uc2 The committee said to expect more testimony from Herschmann in the future. It reconvenes on Wednesday at 10 am.\par \par \uc2 Committee members talk to the press after the hearing. Photograph: Ken Cedeno/UPI/Rex/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 The second panel of witnesses for the day has been dismissed, after Lofgren went through the many court rulings against Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claims of fraud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe rejection of \{resident Trump\u8217\'20\'19s litigation efforts was overwhelming. Twenty two federal judges appointed by Republican presidents, including 10 appointed by President Trump himself and at least 24 elected or appointed Republican state judges dismissed the president\u8217\'20\'19s claims,\u8221\'20\'1D Lofgren said, noting that 11 lawyers have been referred for disciplinary proceedings due to \u8220\'20\'1Cdue to bad faith and baseless efforts\u8221\'20\'1D to undermine the election.\par \par \uc2 Prior to their dismissal, the committee heard from Benjamin Ginsberg, whom Lofgren called, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most preeminent Republican election lawyer in recent history.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn no instance did a court find that the charges of fraud were real,\u8221\'20\'1D Ginsberg said. He also rejected arguments pushed by the Trump campaign that they didn\u8217\'20\'19t get a fair hearing, noting that of 62 lawsuits filed by the campaign, 61 were dismissed, and the one upheld didn\u8217\'20\'19t affect the outcome.\par \par \uc2 Ben Ginsberg, BJay Pak and Al Schmidt. Photograph: Ken Cedeno/UPI/Rex/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Former Philadelphia official Al Schmidt is talking about what happened after Trump began calling him out by name as involved in alleged election fraud in the city.\par \par \uc2 Philadelphia is a Democratic bastion in the swing state of Pennsylvania, and turnout there and in its suburbs was crucial to Biden winning the state. As the Democrat\u8217\'20\'19s victory became clear, Trump began questioning the election\u8217\'20\'19s integrity, and Schmidt personally.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what the president wrote, back when he was allowed on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 enltrLofgren just read out this tweet to Al Schmidt, who's talking now about how threats to him being "more specific" and "more graphic" and started to include his family too {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JUmSeR4z2z"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JUmSeR4z2z}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Nicholas Wu (@nicholaswu12) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/nicholaswu12/status/1536385676127641601?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels almost silly to talk about a tweet, but we can really see the impact they have because prior to that, the threats were pretty general in nature,\u8221\'20\'1D Schmidt said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter the president tweeted at me by name, calling me out the way that he did, the threats became much more specific, much more graphic and included not just me by name but included members of my family, my name, their ages, our address, pictures of our home, just every bit of detail that you can imagine. That was what changed with that tweet,\u8221\'20\'1D he continued.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Pak is focusing on his debunking of a specific claim of election fraud pushed by Trump supporters following the election, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.factcheck.org/2020/12/video-doesnt-show-suitcases-of-illegal-ballots-in-georgia/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 centered on a suitcase allegedly full of ballots used to sway the vote in Georgia for Biden, which was captured by a security camera in an Atlanta vote counting center}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe found that the suitcase full of ballots, the alleged black suitcase that was being seen pulled from under the table, was actually an official lockbox where ballots were kept safe,\u8221\'20\'1D Pak told the lawmakers, adding that \uc2 William Barr\uc2  requested the inquiry since he expected the White House would ask him about it.\par \par \uc2 Trump lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  however played the video before a Georgia Senate committee, which Pak addressed in his testimony.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 FBI\uc2  interviewed the individuals that are depicted in the videos. There\u8217\'20\'19s... double, triple counting of the ballots (which) determined that nothing irregular happened in the county and the allegations made by Mr. Giuliani were false,\u8221\'20\'1D Pak said.\par \par \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  on screen. Photograph: Michael Reynolds/\uc2 EPA\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 The committee has resumed its hearing, with \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  questioning three witnesses.\par \par \uc2 Appearing before the committee is election attorney Benjamin Ginsberg, BJay Pak, a former US Attorney for the northern district of Georgia, and former Philadelphia City Commissioner Al Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 The committee is now taking a break after showing the testimonies of several top officials in the Trump administration and his campaign, all of whom said they didn\u8217\'20\'19t find his fraud claims credible.\par \par \uc2 Just before taking its recess the committee showed a portion of recorded testimony from \uc2 Richard Donoghue\uc2 , a former acting deputy attorney general, who will testify live at a future hearing. He recalled his conversations with Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI tried to again put this into perspective and further put it in clear terms to the president, and I said something to the effect of, sir, we\u8217\'20\'19ve done dozens of investigations, hundreds of interviews. The major allegations are not supported by evidence,\u8221\'20\'1D Donoghue said.\par \par \uc2 But the president was undeterred, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThere were so many of these allegations that when you gave him a very direct answer on one of them, he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t fight us on it but he would move on to another allegation,\u8221\'20\'1D Donoghue recalled.\par \par \uc2 He said the justice department looked into claims a truck driver transported ballots from New York to Pennsylvania, which turned out to be false. The president claimed there was a suitcase of ballots used to sway the outcome in Georgia, but Donoghue found no evidence of that. Trump then went on to claim people were getting paid to vote on Native American reservations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMuch of the information he\u8217\'20\'19s getting is false and/or just not supported by the evidence,\u8221\'20\'1D Donoghue said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 William Barr\uc2  is now offering his thoughts on 2000 Mules, a movie produced by far-right film-maker and provocateur Dinesh D\u8217\'20\'19Souza that promotes Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election fraud claims.\par \par \uc2 For those who missed it, \uc2 The Guardian\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/29/donald-trump-big-lie-film-2000-mules-dinesh-dsouza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 David Smith}}}\uc2  went and saw the film last month, and described its core contention this way:\par \par \uc2 It purports to show that Democratic-aligned ballot \u8220\'20\'1Cmules\u8220\'20\'1C were paid to illegally collect and drop off ballots in Arizona, Georgia, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin, tipping swing states in favour of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  against Trump.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Cdocumentary\u8221\'20\'1D has been resoundingly debunked by factcheckers who point out that its supposed smoking gun \u8211\'20\'13 $2m worth of anonymised mobile phone geolocation data that allegedly tracks the \u8220\'20\'1Cmules\u8221\'20\'1D visiting drop boxes \u8211\'20\'13 is based on false assumptions about the accuracy of such technology.\par \par \uc2 Count Barr among the film\u8217\'20\'19s detractors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the photographic evidence of it was lacking,\u8221\'20\'1D Barr said of the movie. \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a little bit of it but it was lacking and it didn\u8217\'20\'19t establish widespread illegal ballot harvesting.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/29/donald-trump-big-lie-film-2000-mules-dinesh-dsouza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18big lie\u8217\'20\'19 hits cinemas: the film claiming to investigate voter fraud}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 The committee is now trying to establish that even Trump\u8217\'20\'19s closest advisors didn\u8217\'20\'19t believe in his fraud claims.\par \par \uc2 Attorney general \uc2 William Barr\uc2  has reappeared on video, outlining a conversation in the White House days after the election with chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 . According to Barr, Meadows said that when it comes to the fraud claims, \u8220\'20\'1CI think that (Trump is) becoming more realistic and knows that there\u8217\'20\'19s a limit to how far he can take this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As for Barr, he underscored that he viewed investigating fraud as not a problem for the justice department, telling Trump his agency \u8220\'20\'1Cdoesn\u8217\'20\'19t take sides in elections and the department is not an extension of of your legal team.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnd our role is to investigate fraud and look at something if it\u8217\'20\'19s if it\u8217\'20\'19s specific, credible and could have affected the outcome of the election and... we\u8217\'20\'19re doing that and it\u8217\'20\'19s just not,\u8221\'20\'1D Barr recalls saying.\par \par \uc2 In the words of \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 , the California Democrat currently leading the hearing, \u8220\'20\'1CEven after his attorney general told him his claims of election fraud were false. President Trump continued to promote these claims.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lofgren seems to be building the case that the professionals in the Trump campaign never believed there was fraud, and were instead very worried about his prospects following election night.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs the week wore on, as we paid attention to those numbers every single day, multiple times a day... I was feeling less confident for sure,\u8221\'20\'1D campaign manager Bill Stepien said, describing the state of the race as \u8220\'20\'1Cvery, very, very bleak\u8221\'20\'1D for Trump.\par \par \uc2 Stepien goes on to refute some of the arguments swirling over why Trump was losing Arizona. \u8220\'20\'1CSomeone had thrown out... the claim that there were 1000s of illegal citizens who are not eligible to vote and cast their ballots Arizona,\u8221\'20\'1D Stepien said. He looked into it and discovered \u8220\'20\'1Coverseas voters voting in the election. I saw obviously, people who are eligible to vote.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Stepien eventually came to believe that what was happening with the campaign was not \u8220\'20\'1Cnecessarily honest or professional,\u8221\'20\'1D and opted to leave.\par \par \uc2 The former manager was supposed to testify in person today, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62a738cd8f08f7f9ebefe3c0#block-62a738cd8f08f7f9ebefe3c0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 but had a family emergency}}}\uc2 . The committee is instead showing excerpts from his video interviews.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Stirewalt is now discussing Fox News\u8217\'20\'19s decision to call key state Arizona for Biden early on election night \u8211\'20\'13 which enraged the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 He begins by talking up the strength of Fox\u8217\'20\'19s decision desk, which he described as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe best in the business.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe knew it would be a consequential call because it was one of five states that really mattered,\u8221\'20\'1D along with Wisconsin, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Georgia,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBy the time we found out how much everybody was freaking out and losing their minds over this call, we were already trying to call the next state, we had already moved on,\u8221\'20\'1D Stirewalt said.\par \par \uc2 Lofgren asks him what chance he thought Trump had of winning the election, based on what he saw on election night. \u8220\'20\'1CNone,\u8221\'20\'1D Stirewalt replied.\par \par \uc2 Former Fox News politics editor Chris Stirewalt. Photograph: Jonathan Ernst/AP\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Trump claimed that there was \u8220\'20\'1Cmajor fraud\u8221\'20\'1D on election night, his former attorney general \uc2 William Barr\uc2  told the January 6 committee, according to video the committee aired.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRight out of the box on election night, the president claimed that there was major fraud underway,\u8221\'20\'1D Barr said.\par \par \uc2 The commission is discussing the \u8220\'20\'1Cred mirage\u8221\'20\'1D that often occurs on presidential election nights, when Republicans who vote on election day have their votes counted first but Democrats, who often vote early or by mail, sometimes have their votes counted later, creating the impression that Republicans are leading early in the night only to have their share eroded as more Democrats have their votes counted.\par \par \uc2 Barr testifies that though this dynamic was familiar and Trump had been warned about it, the president seized on it to allege fraud.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat seemed to be the basis for this broad claim that there was major fraud. And I didn\u8217\'20\'19t think much of that because people had been talking for weeks and everyone understood for weeks that that was going to be what happened on election night,\u8221\'20\'1D Barr said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 The committee\u8217\'20\'19s first witness of the day is Chris Stirewalt, a former politics editor for Fox News, has been sworn in, and the hearing is now showing a montage of clips from interviews with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lawyers and other officials.\par \par \uc2 These include \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , the former New York mayor who became one of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s most notable attorneys. \uc2 Jason Miller\uc2 , another former Trump attorney, described Giuliani as being \u8220\'20\'1Cintoxicated\u8221\'20\'1D on election night.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s campaign manager Bill Stepien testified by video that he did not think the president should declare victory on election night, but said the president disagreed with him.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.04pm BST\par \par \uc2 It looks like \uc2 William Barr\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s final attorney general during the time of the 2020 election, will be playing a major role in the today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing.\par \par \uc2 The committee last Thursday aired video in which he said he thought Trump\u8217\'20\'19s claims of election fraud were \u8220\'20\'1Cbullshit,\u8221\'20\'1D and committee members say he will reappear today to elaborate on his views.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19ll hear detailed testimony from attorney general Barr describing the various election fraud claims the department of justice investigated. He\u8217\'20\'19ll tell you how he told Mr. Trump repeatedly that there was no merit to those claims. Mr. Barr will tell us that Mr. Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election night claims of fraud were made without regard to the truth, and before it was even possible to look for evidence of fraud,\u8221\'20\'1D Democratic representative \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  said as the hearing began.\par \par \uc2 The hearing on Monday. Photograph: Ken Cedeno/UPI/Rex/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , the committee\u8217\'20\'19s vice chair, is showing videos from lawyers who worked for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s campaign that are testifying they never saw evidence that the 2020 election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Trump campaign legal team knew there was no legitimate argument, fraud, irregularities or anything to overturn the election. And yet, President Trump went ahead with his plans for January 6 anyway,\u8221\'20\'1D Cheney said.\par \par \uc2 The Wyoming representative accused Trump of using this evidence to deceive his supporters into attacking the Capitol. \u8220\'20\'1CAs one conservative editorial board put it recently, \u8216\'20\'18Mr. Trump betrayed his supporters by conning them on January 6, and he is still doing it,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 January 6 committee begins second hearing\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee is beginning its second hearing into \u8220\'20\'1Cthe conspiracy overseen and directed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to overturn the results of the 2020 presidential election and block the transfer of power, a scheme unprecedented in American history,\u8221\'20\'1D as committee chair \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  put it in his opening statement.\par \par \uc2 The Mississippi Democrat is making clear today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing will deal specifically with the former president\u8217\'20\'19s actions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis morning, we will tell the story of how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost an election and knew he lost an election and as a result of his loss, decided to wage an attack on our democracy and attack on American people, trying to rob you of your voice in our democracy, and in doing so lit the fuse that led to the horrific violence of January 6,\u8221\'20\'1D Thompson said.\par \par \uc2 The Capitol attack committee members. Photograph: Mandel Ngan/AFP/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.31pm BST\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile in the Capitol, we may have more developments today on the gun control compromise reached over the weekend, which could attract enough Republican support to pass. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/richardluscombe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Richard Luscombe}}}\uc2  has this look at what exactly the measure would do.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has urged US lawmakers to get a deal on gun reforms to his desk quickly as a group of senators announced a limited bipartisan framework on Sunday responding to last month\u8217\'20\'19s mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 The proposed deal is a modest breakthrough offering measured gun curbs while bolstering efforts to improve school safety and mental health programs.\par \par \uc2 It falls far short of tougher steps long sought by Biden, many Democrats, gun reform advocates and America citizens. For example, there is no proposal to ban assault weapons, as activists had wanted, or to increase from 18 to 21 the age required to buy them.\par \par \uc2 Even so, if the accord leads to the enactment of legislation, it would signal a turn from years of gun massacres that have yielded little but stalemate in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/12/us-senators-bipartisan-gun-control-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US senators reach bipartisan gun control deal after recent mass shootings }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Could Trump face criminal charges over January 6? As my colleague {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/richardluscombe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Richard Luscombe}}}\uc2  reports, some members of the committee investigating the assault believe the evidence is there.\par \par \uc2 Members of the House committee investigating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s efforts to overturn his 2020 election defeat called on Sunday for the US justice department to consider a criminal indictment for the former president and warned that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe danger is still out there\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Their comments on the eve of the second of the panel\u8217\'20\'19s televised hearings into the January 6 2021 insurrection and deadly Capitol attack will add further pressure on the attorney general, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who has angered some Democrats by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/10/merrick-garland-charge-donald-trump-january-6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 so far taking no action}}}\uc2  despite growing evidence of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s culpability.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are certain actions, parts of these different lines of effort to overturn the election, that I don\u8217\'20\'19t see evidence the justice department is investigating,\u8221\'20\'1D committee member Adam Schiff, Democratic congressman for California, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RepAdamSchiff/status/1535994230254059520"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told ABC\u8217\'20\'19s This Week}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/12/capitol-attack-panel-urge-doj-consider-criminal-charges-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Capitol attack panel members urge DoJ to consider criminal charges for Trump}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee will soon continue building its case against former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , with today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing looking at the motivations behind the attack on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 However, a wrench has already been thrown into their plans: the ex-president\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2022/jun/13/jan-6-hearing-trump-insurrection-live-updates?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62a738cd8f08f7f9ebefe3c0#block-62a738cd8f08f7f9ebefe3c0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 former campaign manager has a family emergency, and won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to testify}}}\uc2  as planned, and the hearing has been pushed back to 10:30 am eastern time.\par \par \uc2 The second hearing of the committee will have some important differences from the first, held last Thursday. First of all, it\u8217\'20\'19s taking place during work hours, not during the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/09/january-6-hearings-sketch-liz-cheney"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 primetime TV hour, as in the case of last week\u8217\'20\'19s hearing}}}\uc2 . Committee member \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  is also set to question witnesses, rather than the body\u8217\'20\'19s counsel.\par \par \uc2 As for the goal of these hearings, my colleague Joan E Greve {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/13/jan-6-house-committee-second-hearing-conspiracy-theories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 describes it in the words of committee chair Bennie Thompson}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 If the committee is successful in building its case against Trump, the hearings could deliver a devastating blow to the former president\u8217\'20\'19s hopes of making a political comeback in the 2024 presidential election. But if Americans are unmoved by the committee\u8217\'20\'19s findings, the country faces the specter of another attempted coup, Thompson warned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur democracy remains in danger. The conspiracy to thwart the will of the people is not over,\u8221\'20\'1D Thompson said on Thursday. \u8220\'20\'1CJanuary 6 and the lies that led to insurrection have put two and a half centuries of constitutional democracy at risk. The world is watching what we do here.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Protesters are gathering outside the supreme court, with the justices less than a half hour away from releasing rulings in which the conservative majority could make major changes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, gun rights and environmental regulation.\par \par \uc2 enltrOpposing protestors face to face right now. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/epObAVwJnp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/epObAVwJnp}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Whitney Wild (@WhitneyWReports) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhitneyWReports/status/1536334266619113472?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrScene outside the Supreme Court this morning. Two small groups of protesters have gathered with a group of police on bicycles separating the two groups. T-minutes 40 minutes until opinions. ?I\u8217\'20\'19m standing by with ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/fox5dc?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @fox5dc}}}\uc2  ?. Join us live on ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SCOTUSblog?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @SCOTUSblog}}}\uc2  ? TikTok. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/PNPQifGuD2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/PNPQifGuD2}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Katie Barlow (@katieleebarlow) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/katieleebarlow/status/1536338024962867201?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Last month, the court was rocked by the unprecedented leak of a draft opinion showing conservatives {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/06/abortion-midterms-elections-roe"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 were poised to strike down Roe v Wade and end \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights nationwide}}}\uc2 . Those same justices may also opt {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/may/28/us-supreme-court-gun-rights-second-amendment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to expand the ability to carry concealed weapons}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/feb/25/supreme-court-west-virgina-epa-nondelegation-doctrine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 curb the government\u8217\'20\'19s regulatory powers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Former Trump campaign manager to miss January 6 hearing\par \par \uc2 Bill Stepien, the former campaign manager for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who was to be a main witness in today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing of the January 6 committee, will not attend due to an emergency.\par \par \uc2 The hearing is now delayed by 30 minutes to 10.30am, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/hugo-lowell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hugo Lowell}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 enltrJust in: Former Trump campaign manager Bill Stepien is no longer appearing at the second Jan. 6 committee hearing this morning due to a family emergency \u8212\'20\'14 and hearing has been delayed to around 10:30a ET\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Hugo Lowell (@hugolowell) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hugolowell/status/1536334574984232961?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 13, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The development throws a wrench into the plans for the committee\u8217\'20\'19s second hearing, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/13/jan-6-house-committee-second-hearing-conspiracy-theories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 which was to look deeper into the conspiracy theories that fueled the attack on the Capitol}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lies are going to be the subject of this morning\u8217\'20\'19s January 6 committee hearing, specifically those that motivated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supporters to attack the Capitol, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/joan-greve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Joan E Greve}}}\uc2  reports:\par \par \uc2 The House select committee investigating the January 6 insurrection in 2021 will reconvene Monday to scrutinize the conspiracy theories that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/10/capitol-attack-hearing-analysis-trump-pence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 led a group}}}\uc2  of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s supporters to attack the US Capitol.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic chair of the committee, Mississippi congressman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 , has said the second hearing will focus on \u8220\'20\'1Cthe lies that convinced those men and others to storm the Capitol to try to stop the transfer of power\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re going to take a close look at the first part of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attack on the rule of law, when he lit the fuse that ultimately resulted in the violence of January 6,\u8221\'20\'1D Thompson said on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/13/jan-6-house-committee-second-hearing-conspiracy-theories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 House panel to scrutinize conspiracy theories that led to Capitol attack}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senate mulls bipartisan gun control proposal as January 6 committee meets again\par \par \uc2 Good morning, everybody. Today could be a very big day in Washington, with the inquiry into the January 6 insurrection continuing, the supreme court releasing opinions and the Senate considering a proposal to restrict gun access following a spate of mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a rundown of what to expect:\par \par \uc2 * Senators have reached a deal on a framework {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/12/us-senators-bipartisan-gun-control-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for gun control legislation}}}\uc2  meant to respond to recent mass shootings in Buffalo, New York and Uvalde, Texas, which looks like it could get the support of enough Republicans and Democrats to pass the chamber.\par \par \uc2 * The supreme court{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will release another batch of decisions}}}\uc2  at 10 am eastern time. There\u8217\'20\'19s no telling what the court will opt to release, but major rulings on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/biden-democrats-reaction-codifying-roe-v-wade-reform-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rights}}}\uc2 , gun control and environmental regulation are expected before the term is out.\par \par \uc2 * At the same time, the January 6 committee will begin its second hearing following last Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s blockbuster look into what happened at the Capitol that day. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jun/13/jan-6-house-committee-second-hearing-conspiracy-theories"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing will look deeper at the conspiracy theories that motivated the attack}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 * Democratic senator Bernie Sanders and Republican senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  will take part in a one-hour debate {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bipartisanpolicy.org/press-release/the-senate-project-sen-lindsey-graham-and-sen-bernie-sanders-debate-live-on-june-13/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 organized by The Senate Project}}}\uc2 , intended to build bridges between the two parties while also allowing the lawmakers to air their (very different) perspectives on politics. The event begins at 12 pm eastern time, and will be streamed on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nation.foxnews.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Fox Nation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220613ei6d0020d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
