\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Pro-choice militants are targeting \u8216\'20\'18pregnancy crisis centers\u8217\'20\'19 across US\b0\par\par\uc2 Poppy Noor \par \uc2 1,121 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2022\par 06:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Experts say that no matter how righteous, extremist protests are risky: they may turn people against the cause\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CJane was here\u8221\'20\'1D: those were the words graffitied on the walls of a \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnancy crisis center\u8221\'20\'1D in Amherst, New York, this week, as part of a targeted arson attack.\par \par \uc2 In Charlotte, North Carolina, the message was more explicit: \u8220\'20\'1CIf abortions aren\u8217\'20\'19t safe then you aren\u8217\'20\'19t either,\u8221\'20\'1D read the words scrawled in red paint across another center, which also had its windows broken.\par \par \uc2 The same message was found on the walls of \u8220\'20\'1Cpregnancy crisis centers\u8221\'20\'1D in Texas and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/10/abortion-arson-attack-wisconsin-pro-choice-janes-revenge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wisconsin}}}\uc2  in May, and another attack took place on a similar institution in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.king5.com/article/news/crime/lynnwoods-next-step-pregnancy-services-vandalized/281-eac20492-8fa9-4f37-828a-cc846decb902"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington}}}\uc2 , also in May. The attacks on these centers \u8211\'20\'13 which try to dissuade women from seeking abortions \u8211\'20\'13 are believed to be linked to Jane\u8217\'20\'19s Revenge, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.influencewatch.org/organization/janes-revenge/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an extremist, militant pro-choice group}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 No one has been injured during these attacks, but the methods have been extreme: vandals have thrown molotov cocktails, committed arson, damaged property and made threats. At a time of rapidly increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in the US \u8211\'20\'13 and an upcoming supreme court decision this summer that is expected to result in total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in as many as 26 states \u8211\'20\'13 can more violence be expected?\par \par \uc2 After the attack in Wisconsin, a letter signed by \u8220\'20\'1CJane\u8217\'20\'19s Revenge\u8221\'20\'1D was sent to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://journalstar.com/news/national/group-claims-responsibility-for-attack-on-wisconsin-anti-abortion-organization-promises-more-violence/article_90f1677b-fa62-52e6-8a79-3b0c480dfdd7.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bellingcat journalist}}}\uc2  laying out a kind of mission statement, and threatening further action. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://madison.com/news/local/crime-and-courts/group-claims-responsibility-for-attack-on-anti-abortion-organization-in-madison-warns-of-more-violence/article_c56741e2-05fe-551f-ada9-078bf58db076.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  mere \u8216\'20\'18difference of opinion\u8217\'20\'19}}}\uc2  as some have framed it,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are literally fighting for our lives. We will not sit still while we are killed and forced into servitude.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The letter also demanded that \u8220\'20\'1Canti-choice establishments\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 institutions that have a reputation for trying to lure women into unwanted pregnancies and spreading misinformation about the impacts of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 to disband within 30 days, or else face more violence.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWisconsin is the first flashpoint,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter said. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we are all over the US, and we will issue no further warnings \u8230\'20\'26 We will not stop until \u8230\'20\'26 the inalienable right to manage our own health is returned to us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at \uc2 UC Davis\uc2  with an expertise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, believes the attacks can be read as an expression of distrust in the government, and a loss of faith in democratic institutions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people \u8211\'20\'13 conservatives and progressives \u8211\'20\'13 have lost faith in a lot of democratic institutions. A lot of people believe the supreme court is partisan and illegitimate,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She notes some of the public discourse around the supreme court in recent years: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  referred to supreme court justices as \u8220\'20\'1Cmy judges\u8221\'20\'1D and was specific about the ways they would serve his aims. \u8220\'20\'1CHe would just come out [and say] \u8216\'20\'18my\u8217\'20\'19 judges will give you specific outcomes on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , guns and a variety of other things,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ziegler.\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s impending rulings in a number of high-profile cases, from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/regulation/court-battles/3503408-supreme-court-may-soon-expand-gun-rights-amid-roiling-debate/#:~:text=In%20the%20court%27s%202008%20decision,the%20home%20for%20self%2Ddefense."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 gun rights}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 in quick succession since Trump\u8217\'20\'19s appointments of justices Amy Coney Barrett, \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have only deepened the public\u8217\'20\'19s impression of partisanship, Ziegler said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not something that\u8217\'20\'19s unfolding over a very long period of time. And [the supreme court] is rendering decisions that are all helping conservatives,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the court just does things that help conservatives, people will ask \u8216\'20\'18why is there a partisan body that isn\u8217\'20\'19t elected? Why is there a partisan body with lifetime appointments?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What is surprising about these recent attacks, however, is that the vast majority of violent protest tactics \u8211\'20\'13 including around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 have come, historically, from the right of American politics. Rightwing protests over lockdown, racial justice and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have seen armed militias taking justice into their own hands, even storming the US Capitol. The tactics of the right on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been similarly extreme: 11 people were murdered by anti-choice protesters between 1993 and 2016, and another 26 murders were attempted. The tactics of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have included bombings, arson, death threats and hate mail.\par \par \uc2 Are violent protests successful?\par \par \uc2 Ziegler believes that some people on both the left and the right might feel they are enacting some sort of vigilante justice when they resort to violent protest tactics around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . At present, pro-choice advocates may be seeing an apparent mismatch between public opinion and legislation: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ablink.editorial.theguardian.com/ss/c/TBl-lE0k4WbTlFRn6v-lQQ6a3wWzoxh1pfcZxh0_cpjGjDRKamO53OigFMyOm74Jjs4YS-D3MDpFJGUKSTZ6AjUOa-7fe8SFmLCt7o1O1YojL2qEGcX5E5pFuMlTVvPHb9PzvDXigYlYQP7o_9YHzFsOEPDxLGJUfWC71IhZ24lb2pGM6deGzgXpLJeJBsEJy6iFXH1_ZFMs0FsWGc89iQUR_-p8L6WkwGG86asJL1fbp5KrV_gywlCCzDq1YSJW9ajXi_CYUHFV0qa4MHzx8KRXmPiC6zLo8NjyFnfE0oI/3mr/yxf51V_MRL20JtqzI0dBow/h18/FTyQlJDXjdSFUC3aLiPW0Uroo40smv5RVLLrtKS3JGY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 61% of Americans}}}\uc2  believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, yet states are enforcing increasingly extreme restrictions.\par \par \uc2 These tactics, however, are not commonly successful, academics say \u8211\'20\'13 even if protesters feel their grievances are valid. Take the example of American civil rights protests in the 1950s and 1960s.\par \par \uc2 Omar Wasow studied the political consequences of different kinds of protests during that period: violent and non-violent. He found violent protest seemed to have the unintended effect of chilling support for the movement, leading to more votes for conservatives, while peaceful protest apparently had the effect of liberalising white voters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if we think violence might be justified in response to state repression under segregation, or Jim Crow, it may produce outcomes entirely contrary to what activists were fighting for,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wasow. \u8220\'20\'1CNorm-violating tactics can be repulsive to put to potential moderate allies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ziegler said the same has been true for the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, whose own violence has rarely been effective.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think that violence was particularly effective for conservatives,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not wrong to say that progressives need to be willing to use the strategies that conservatives have used. But committing illegal acts of violence has not historically been why the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has succeeded.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Instead, she said, the movement has succeeded as well as it has due to big, long-term, structural changes: essentially changing the rules of the game. \u8220\'20\'1CThey have gotten involved in changing the rules of campaign finance, they have changed access to the vote, and changed how the supreme court confirmation process works.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople often focus on a narrow period of time and say, \u8216\'20\'18Oh, look at how all of this success was made [in a small period],\u8217\'20\'19 but there\u8217\'20\'19s really decades of activism that\u8217\'20\'19s slowly building the kind of social and political capital to make change happen,\u8221\'20\'1D Wasow said.\par \par \uc2 With popular opinion seemingly on the side of keeping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal at least in some instances, such tactics could simply sully the aims of the cause, Ziegler warned.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you have majority support for what you stand for, and the message you send is, \u8216\'20\'18Who cares, we\u8217\'20\'19re gonna go out and commit acts of violence,\u8217\'20\'19 that will probably also be counterproductive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220611ei6b000gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
