\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 How Mexican feminists are helping Americans get abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Cecilia Nowell \par \uc2 1,563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 June 2022\par 09:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Latin American groups are sharing their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access models with US activists in as Roe v Wade stands to be overturned\par \par \uc2 In late January, nearly 70 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists from across Mexico gathered in a city along the US-Mexico border. For three days, they huddled in hotel conference rooms, video chatting with activists in the US, who had been unable to travel due to Covid-19 and an Arctic cold front. Together, they strategized how to support Americans as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions proliferated across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was three days of very, very, very, very cold outside, but very, very warm inside,\u8221\'20\'1D Ver\u243\'00\'F3nica Cruz S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, director of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://laslibres.org.mx/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Las Libres}}}\uc2 , a feminist organization based in Guanajuato, Mexico, said.\par \par \uc2 Over the course of the long weekend, members of 30 different \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, from across Mexico and the United States, formed what they call the Red Transfronteriza, or Cross-Border Network. Following a model that Mexican and other Latin American feminists had developed over the past two decades, the Red Transfronteriza would \u8220\'20\'1Caccompany\u8221\'20\'1D Americans through their abortions, guiding them through the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s protocol for safely using \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills without the supervision of a doctor. They would also supply \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to Americans for nothing, mailing donated medications to the United States.\par \par \uc2 On the last day of their January gathering, the network held a press conference to announce their plan. The next day, Cruz said, 10 women had already written asking for help.\par \par \uc2 The gathering was fueled by a pair of developments just a few months prior. Just days after Texas\u8217\'20\'19s six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, known as SB 8, went into effect last September, the Mexican supreme court decriminalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That same month, Cruz began reaching out to other activists in Mexico to extend the infrastructure they had built while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been criminalized into the United States. The aim was to not only guide Americans through self-managing their own abortions with pills but also to teach US activists what they had learned in supporting abortions outside of the law.\par \par \uc2 Since January, they have mailed thousands of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to the United States and guided hundreds of Americans, not only Texans, through their questions and concerns. With the days probably numbered for the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, they expect the need for their work to only grow.\par \par \uc2 At their January meeting, the Red Transfronteriza settled on two forms of support. If they could travel, Americans were welcome to cross the border and abort with the in-person support of Mexican networks. But if they couldn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8211\'20\'13 for reasons of cost, logistics or immigration status \u8211\'20\'13 then the network would mail them \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, free of charge, and then virtually walk them through their abortions. Groups based in the United States, which have chosen to remain anonymous for security reasons, would distribute the pills sent by their Mexican colleagues.\par \par \uc2 They send them \u8220\'20\'1Ceverything they are going to need \u8211\'20\'13 sanitary napkins, chewing gum for nausea, pills for pain\u8221\'20\'1D, said Sandra Cardona, a member of the Monterrey-based \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  network Red Necesito Abortar. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything so that you can have your \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  safely at home and without going out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Although many members of the Red Transfronteriza had experience working with Americans, the number of requests for help that they received from the US soared after SB 8 went into effect. Where Red Necesito Abortar used to receive four or five requests every month from the US, Cardona says they are now getting hundreds. Crystal P\u233\'00\'E9rez Lira, a member of the Tijuana-based Colectiva Bloodys, estimates that the collective receives about 300 messages monthly on their social media accounts. And Cruz says that, since January, Las Libres has directly accompanied 200 women and mailed a thousand packages of pills to people in the US. Many Americans are reaching them through social media: the groups maintain active presences on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , WhatsApp, Telegram, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , Reddit and even TikTok.\par \par \uc2 The groups are also building deeper alliances with US groups, which have long supported people seeking abortions by helping them fund the costs of the procedure, or travel to clinics in neighboring states. As more than half of US states appear poised to limit or ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care if Roe is overturned this month, the Red Transfronteriza hopes to show Americans how they have successfully supported Mexicans in aborting safely at home, outside of the medical establishment.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accompaniment networks have a long history in Latin America, dating back to 2000, when the state of Guanajuato \u8211\'20\'13 where Cruz and Las Libres are based \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2000/08/29/world/a-victory-of-sorts-for-abortion-rights-in-a-mexican-state.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tried to pass a law}}}\uc2  that would have made it illegal to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in cases of rape, the only situation in which the procedure had been allowed in the conservative region. Cruz and her colleagues began organizing demonstrations, building relationships with friendly gynecologists and learning how pregnant people could safely take the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills mifepristone and misoprostol at home to end their pregnancies no matter the reason. Mexico was in a unique position to begin this work because misoprostol is available over-the-counter at most pharmacies to treat ulcers, and by itself has an effectiveness rate of 85% when used in early pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe began to develop accompaniment networks as a way of providing security,\u8221\'20\'1D Cruz said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo that women would have that guarantee that, despite the fact that it was illegal, despite what the world said negatively about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , we had a group of people that were in favor of it, that were going to accompany them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not a crime to self-manage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in almost any state in the United States\par \par \uc2 Sara Ainsworth\par \par \uc2 In 2009, when the Latin American Feminist Conference came to Mexico City, Cruz realized that Las Libres were not alone. She learned of \uc2 Women on Waves\uc2 , a healthcare initiative that first began providing people in countries with restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws care on boats docked in international waters, and then began mailing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to those same countries under the name Women on Web. (In 2018, Women on Web {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.insider.com/women-us-abortion-pill-mail-aid-access-2018-10"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 launched a program}}}\uc2  called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aidaccess.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Aid Access}}}\uc2  to mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to the United States.) She also learned about the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  hotline that Women on Web had helped organize in Ecuador, which people could call for guidance on how to take \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 But at the time, Cruz said, Las Libres was the only group that she knew of accompanying women in their own countries. Soon, however, their model would spread across Latin America. Today, similar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Caccompaniment\u8221\'20\'1D networks operate in Chile, Argentina, Colombia, Bolivia, Peru and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Cardona said she was surprised at the number of people reaching out from states other than Texas, like Oklahoma, Georgia and even California. Then she realized that even in a liberal state like California, a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could cost $600. Mexican \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accompaniment networks could help people abort for free, with a combination of donated pills and cheap, over-the-counter medications.\par \par \uc2 Although Americans have, justifiably, been fearful of the legal consequences of self-managing their abortions \u8211\'20\'13 especially after the case of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/10/texas-woman-murder-charges-dropped-self-induced-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lizelle Herrera}}}\uc2 , a Texas woman wrongly arrested for self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , made headlines in April \u8211\'20\'13 the Red Transfronteriza believes that accompaniment minimizes legal risks.\par \par \uc2 When Las Libres was founded \u8220\'20\'1Cthere were women in jails for having abortions in Guanajuato\u8221\'20\'1D, said Cruz. \u8220\'20\'1CIn fact, Guanajuato was worse than Texas, in terms of restrictions.\u8221\'20\'1D But what they found, she says, is that \u8220\'20\'1Ca good accompaniment eliminates criminalization\u8221\'20\'1D. She claims that not a single woman who was supported through their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a trained accompaniment network has ended up in jail in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Cruz notes that women who go to the hospital after taking the pills often do so out of fear of the amount of bleeding they cause. An experienced accompanier can advise them on what is normal, and what to say if they really should see a doctor (that they are suffering a miscarriage, which is indistinguishable from a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 Cardona says the network is planning to lead workshops across northern Mexico to train \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists on supporting Americans. \u8220\'20\'1CThe majority who come from the United States come with a lot of fear of being imprisoned, a lot of fear of bleeding to death,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a huge lack of information.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a crime to self-manage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in almost any state in the United States,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sara Ainsworth, senior legal & policy director at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ifwhenhow.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If/When/How: Lawyering for Reproductive Justice}}}\uc2 , though prosecutors have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/may/18/abortion-prosecution-fetal-homicide-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 misused other laws}}}\uc2  to go after people who end their pregnancies. She recommends that anyone seeking to self-manage their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  contact If/When/How\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reprolegalhelpline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ReproLegal Helpline}}}\uc2 , where their lawyers can provide free, confidential legal advice.\par \par \uc2 Despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thelily.com/in-texas-anyone-who-mails-abortion-pills-can-now-be-sent-to-jail/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 efforts by some US states to punish}}}\uc2  groups who mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, Cardona says the Red Transfronteriza will not stop its work. Ainsworth also notes that she thinks it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely states will go after international groups \u8211\'20\'13 partially because other international organizations like Aid Access have managed to continue operating in the US and also because the mail is controlled by federal laws.\par \par \uc2 If they take away anything from the Mexican model, P\u233\'00\'E9rez Lira says, she hopes that Americans will learn that they do not have to depend on the law. Instead, they can rely \u8220\'20\'1Con their own resources\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220610ei6a0018i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
