\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Republicans keep passing extreme anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans without popular support. Here\u8217\'20\'19s why\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine in New York \par \uc2 749 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 June 2022\par 14:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Most Americans don\u8217\'20\'19t want \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans but gerrymandering allows politicians to face little accountability\par \par \uc2 Hello, and happy Thursday,\par \par \uc2 As states have passed a wave of increasingly extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions in recent years, a sort of puzzling contradiction has emerged. The American public broadly supports the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , public polling has shown, yet politicians who pass these controversial restrictions are consistently re-elected. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/07/gerrymandering-abortion-roe-v-wade-ohio"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 we published a story }}}\uc2  that seeks to answer that question. A big part of why politicians face little accountability is gerrymandering. State lawmakers, who have the power to draw the boundaries of their own districts in most places, can pick which voters they represent and virtually guarantee their re-election.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s more important than ever to understand this dynamic. In his draft opinion overturning Roe v Wade, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/read-justice-alito-initial-abortion-opinion-overturn-roe-v-wade-pdf-00029504"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wrote that \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  is an issue}}}\uc2  that should be resolved by the political process, not the courts. By insulating politicians from accountability, extreme gerrymandering prevents the political process from doing that. In 2019, Alito joined four of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative justices in saying there was nothing federal courts could do to police even the most extreme gerrymandering.\par \par \uc2 Few places better capture the link between partisan gerrymandering and extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures than Ohio.\par \par \uc2 In 2010, Republicans won control of the Ohio legislature and drew new maps that allowed them to hold a veto-proof majority for the next decade. In 2011, the legislature began to pass a series of restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Republicans enacted a new law that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a fetus was viable and required viability testing after 20 weeks. They passed another measure that prohibited taxpayer-funded hospitals from entering into patient transfer agreements with clinics, making it harder for the clinics to operate. In 2019, the state had banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks, one of the most restrictive laws in the country. (The Ohio Policy Evaluation Network, which tracks \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Ohio has a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/22054878-ohio-legislative-timeline_january-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 good timeline}}}\uc2  of these bills).\par \par \uc2 When Ohio lawmakers were passing these measures, there wasn\u8217\'20\'19t overwhelming public support for them. Ohio voters are closely divided on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/how-the-public-views-ohios-abortion-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a majority did not support }}}\uc2  the six week ban (one poll after it passed in 2019 showed that a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courthousenews.com/poll-most-ohio-voters-oppose-states-heartbeat-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 majority of people opposed it}}}\uc2  ). Even so, Ohio Republicans have maintained their majorities in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat mismatch between what we see in public opinion and what we see at the statehouse, really suggests that what citizens are thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access really is not reflected in their statehouse,\u8221\'20\'1D Danielle Bessett, a sociology professor at the \uc2 University of Cincinnati\uc2 , who closely studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in Ohio, told me. \u8220\'20\'1CThat suggests that there isn\u8217\'20\'19t a concern about this being sort of something that they\u8217\'20\'19re going to get held accountable for at the polls.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s an imbalance that exists across the country. Nationally, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/fact-sheet/public-opinion-on-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 61% of Americans}}}\uc2  say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases, but states are enacting a blitz of increasingly extreme restrictions, including several that are considering outright bans. Republicans continue to control more state legislative chambers than Democrats do, and very few are expected to flip partisan control ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/24/gerrymandering-wisconsin-purple-state-rightwing-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fewer than 1 in 5 state legislative districts}}}\uc2  are estimated to be competitive this year).\par \par \uc2 In Ohio, Republicans have once again engineered maps that preserve their advantage. After the state supreme court struck down five proposals for a new legislative map because they were too gerrymandered, lawmakers ran out the clock. They convinced a federal court to impose a map for the 2022 elections that will allow them to maintain, at minimum, 54% of the seats in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s frustrating. In some ways it\u8217\'20\'19s hopeful that people do think that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be a right and should exist for people in Ohio,\u8221\'20\'1D Sri Thakkilapati, the interim executive director of PreTerm, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Cleveland told me. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s helpful to know that there are more of us. But in some ways it\u8217\'20\'19s very disheartening\u8230\'20\'26It feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s not gonna make a difference.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Also worth watching\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jim Marchant, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/feb/11/qanon-donald-trump-big-lie-elections-swing-states"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a QAnon linked candidate}}}\uc2  running to be Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s chief election official, is seeking his party\u8217\'20\'19s nomination in the GOP primary on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Voters with disabilities {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/sites/default/files/001_-_complaint_against_john_h_merrill_filed_by_the_national_federation_of_the_blind_of_alabama_gail_smith_eric_peebles_jill_rossiter.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are suing Alabama}}}\uc2  for offering inadequate access to absentee ballots for people who are blind\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220609ei690028o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
