\par\par \uc2 Film\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There\u8217\'20\'19s always something to be done\u8217\'20\'19: how the Janes provided illegal abortions before Roe\b0\par\par\uc2 Adrian Horton \par \uc2 1,386 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2022\par 12:28 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A HBO documentary looks to the past \u8211\'20\'13 a secret, women-run network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the late 1960s \u8211\'20\'13 as a window into the future of restricted reproductive rights in the US\par \par \uc2 Regardless of whether the supreme court fully overturns Roe v Wade later this month, as indicated by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the majority draft opinion leaked in May}}}\uc2 , or merely allows it to be gutted at the state level, the US will continue its years-long march backwards on reproductive rights. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the US in 2022 mirrors 1972, the year before the supreme court ensured a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with Roe v Wade, and a time when a sparse patchwork of legalization in a few states forced many women to seek care from dubious illegal providers or dangerous at-home methods.\par \par \uc2 The Janes, a new HBO documentary on an underground network of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in Chicago in the years just before legalization, looks to this past as a window into America\u8217\'20\'19s likely post-Roe future \u8211\'20\'13 a US without a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose that is less dystopia than memory. The 101-minute film, directed by Tia Lessin and Emma Pildes, traces the personal political evolutions of several members as well as the development of the group from collection of activists who connected women with safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers \u8211\'20\'13 male doctors, or men who claimed to be doctors \u8211\'20\'13 to performing the procedure themselves. The group eventually provided over 11,000 abortions, many to poor women who could not afford another option. The same women \u8211\'20\'13 poor, rural, people of color, often young \u8211\'20\'13 who are left behind now by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/14/oklahoma-abortion-post-roe-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 extreme measures}}}\uc2  passed by Republican state legislatures banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/26/oklahoma-strictest-abortion-ban-law-us-governor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as early as six weeks into pregnancy}}}\uc2 , before most women know they\u8217\'20\'19re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis film is instructive about what this country looks like when women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 basic healthcare \u8211\'20\'13 is criminalized,\u8221\'20\'1D Lessin told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause what we know to be true is that when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean that women stop seeking abortions. It just means that they don\u8217\'20\'19t get access to safe abortions.\u8221\'20\'1D As a former Chicago ER doctor notes in the film, his hospital\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2003-jan-21-me-patt21-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 septic \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ward}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/abortion-rulings-history-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 standard feature before Roe}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 admitted 15-20 women a day with injuries or infections, sometimes lethal, from attempted abortions.\par \par \uc2 That danger prompted Heather Booth, interviewed in the film, to find a doctor to provide a friend\u8217\'20\'19s sister with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a college student in 1965. As word of mouth spread, Booth gathered several friends from the women\u8217\'20\'19s movement to share the workload, which informally became the Janes. The network eventually included numerous safe houses, clandestine drivers, phone staff, pseudonymous flyers and, once they learned one of their top doctors did not have medical license (he also appears in the film and speaks frankly about his motivation: under the table money), training to do the procedures themselves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are many ways in which this film gives us an example of what it looks like to be of help and of use. And unfortunately, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to need more and more of that in the years to come,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lessin. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s sort of a kick in the ass and maybe a call to action. We shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be sitting on our hands and sighing about the state of this country. There\u8217\'20\'19s always something to be done. And in this case, they decided to create this network.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The film, which includes interviews with several former Janes and archival footage of late 60s protests in Chicago, is part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2022/may/04/how-film-tv-depictions-abortion-change"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 collection of recent films}}}\uc2  revisiting the pre-legalization past as access eclipses in the US. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/apr/20/happening-review-cleverly-structured-and-impeccably-produced-abortion-drama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Happening}}}\uc2 , a film from the French director Audrey Diwan, turns novelist Annie Ernaux\u8217\'20\'19s memoir of searching for an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the 1960s into a thriller. The feature film {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/jan/24/call-jane-review-abortion-drama-is-sensitive-if-not-revelatory"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Call Jane}}}\uc2 , starring Elizabeth Banks as a fictional Jane client-turned-member-turned-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider and Sigourney Weaver as a dauntless organizer modeled in part on real-life leader Jody Parsons, premiered at Sundance earlier this year and will get a wide release this fall.\par \par \uc2 Call Jane, directed by Phyllis Nagy, enlisted Jane member and documentary participant Judith Arcana as a historical consultant. Arcana was among the seven Janes arrested by Chicago police in 1972 in a sting operation and charged with conspiracy to commit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the threat of 110 years in prison. \u8220\'20\'1CWe were ordinary women trying to save people\u8217\'20\'19s lives, but we were criminals,\u8221\'20\'1D Arcana says in the film. (The group\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer, Jo-Anne Wolfson, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chicagomag.com/Chicago-Magazine/April-2019/Chicagos-Forgotten-Pro-Choice-Warriors-the-Janes/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stalled the trial}}}\uc2  until the Roe verdict dismissed their charges.) Arcana\u8217\'20\'19s son, Daniel, began production on what would become the documentary in 2016, when Trump was elected and \u8220\'20\'1Cthe writing was on the wall,\u8221\'20\'1D said Emma Pildes, his sister and the film\u8217\'20\'19s co-director.\par \par \uc2 The context in the late 1960s is different than now, but the reasons people seek abortions \u8211\'20\'13 in fear, confidence or, often, desperation \u8211\'20\'13 remain the same. She wasn\u8217\'20\'19t ready. Rape. Lack of finances. Struggling to stay afloat with the kids she has. Medical risks. She simply doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want to have a child. In one of the most poignant sequences of the film, several Janes hold up the index cards used to keep track of the calls, which were read aloud and passed around to members for follow-up. There are stacks of them, each an individual case with notes such as \u8220\'20\'1Cafraid of pain\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Cterrified\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1C21, has one kid, $0\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cbe cautious father is a cop.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey weren\u8217\'20\'19t just cards, you know? They were people, and I knew they were women like me,\u8221\'20\'1D says Diane Stevens.\par \par \uc2 Each Jane also remembers the ones who got away or could not be reached, such as a 19-year-old Black woman who entered their safe house with an infection from a botched \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and, when told she needed to go to the hospital, fled. The Janes later learned she died of septicemia. \u8220\'20\'1CWe felt an enormous amount of guilt connected to this,\u8221\'20\'1D says Martha Scott, a former Jane, in the film. \u8220\'20\'1CNot that this was our doing, but she came through our hands, and we didn\u8217\'20\'19t do enough.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe show really clearly in the film what happens when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in one state and not legal in another,\u8221\'20\'1D Lessin said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe saw who\u8217\'20\'19s able to make the trip and who gets left behind. It\u8217\'20\'19s very cut and dried. It\u8217\'20\'19s along racial lines, it\u8217\'20\'19s along economic lines \u8211\'20\'13 we saw it then and we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing it right now, and we will see it on steroids in the coming years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The threat of anti-choice legislation is clear on the horizon; {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/09/republican-lawmakers-abortion-ban-roe-v-wade-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 13 states have so-called \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D bans}}}\uc2  ready to go into effect the day Roe is overturned, passed by conservative legislatures deeply out of step with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2022/05/06/americas-abortion-quandary/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more nuanced public opinion}}}\uc2 , which favors \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. Reproductive rights epitomizes the hardening of minority rule in the US; the issue is not changing public opinion but having a democracy which reflects it.\par \par \uc2 Asked, along those lines, if they hoped to reach anti-choice audiences, Pildes answered: \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t need to,\u8221\'20\'1D as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/where-americans-stand-on-abortion-in-5-charts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most polls find between 85% to 90%}}}\uc2  of Americans think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in at least some circumstances. \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be nice and certainly one of the things that we want to promote is dialogue. We want people to be having conversations about it for various reasons, not just to change hearts and minds but to de-stigmatize it and make people who have had abortions not feel like pariahs, shamed or that they\u8217\'20\'19ve done something wrong.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe can\u8217\'20\'19t control what people do with what we\u8217\'20\'19ve done,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lessin. \u8220\'20\'1CMostly I hope that, after the credits roll, they can feel the emotions around what it looks like when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is criminalized. That they can feel the anger that we felt, they can feel the commitment to prevent us from going back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The Janes premieres on HBO and HBO Max on 8 June, Foxtel and Binge in Australia on 9 June, and will air in the UK at a later date\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220608ei68000mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
