\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 How Republicans pass  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  bans most Americans don\u8217\'20\'19t want\b0\par\par\uc2 Sam Levine in New York \par \uc2 1,482 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2022\par 06:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some form is widely supported, but gerrymandered districts allow politicians to push extreme measures through\par \par \uc2 On 10 April 2019, the Ohio legislature easily {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.legislature.ohio.gov/legislation/legislation-summary?id=GA133-SB-23"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed SB 23}}}\uc2 , a bill that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after a fetal heartbeat is detected, as early as six weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 It was a move that should have carried considerable political risk in Ohio, a state closely divided between Democrats and Republicans. There wasn\u8217\'20\'19t widespread support for the bill \u8211\'20\'13 polling showed public opinion was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/datacentral/2019/03/ohio-sharply-divided-on-bill-to-ban-abortion-as-early-as-6-weeks-poll-shows.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly evenly split}}}\uc2  over the bill (a poll after the bill was passed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.courthousenews.com/poll-most-ohio-voters-oppose-states-heartbeat-abortion-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 showed a majority opposed it}}}\uc2  ), John Kasich, a previous Republican governor, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/ed425a404fe646fe8343ae7e1b948e9f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had twice vetoed}}}\uc2  the bill, saying it was unconstitutional, and it had stalled in the legislature for years.\par \par \uc2 But Ohio\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2 , a Republican, nonetheless signed the bill into law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/apr/12/ohio-governor-abortion-signs-heartbeat-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the next day}}}\uc2 . And the following fall, when the politicians who passed the measure came up for re-election, Republicans didn\u8217\'20\'19t lose any seats in the state legislature. In fact, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/politics/elections/2020/11/04/2020-election-ohio-legislative-results/6050134002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  they expanded}}}\uc2  their majority.\par \par \uc2 Ohio offers a case study of how US politicians enact extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures that don\u8217\'20\'19t align with voters\u8217\'20\'19 views but face little accountability at the polls \u8211\'20\'13 an issue even more at stake this month as the supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/biden-democrats-reaction-codifying-roe-v-wade-reform-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 is on the verge of issuing}}}\uc2  a decision that will probably overturn Roe v Wade, the landmark 1973 decision establishing a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Ohio and elsewhere, politicians are protected by their ability to draw their own political districts every 10 years, distorting them in such a way as to virtually guarantee their re-election. Republicans drew the lines in Ohio in 2011 and have held a supermajority in the state legislature ever since. \u8220\'20\'1CWe can kind of do what we want,\u8221\'20\'1D Matt Huffman, the top Republican in the Ohio senate, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dispatch.com/story/news/2022/05/19/meet-matt-huffman-the-lima-republican-who-runs-ohio/7269099001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  told the Columbus Dispatch recently}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/read-justice-alito-initial-abortion-opinion-overturn-roe-v-wade-pdf-00029504"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft opinion}}}\uc2  overturning Roe, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  wrote that disputes over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be resolved through the political process. \u8220\'20\'1CThe permissibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the limitations, upon it, are to be resolved like most important questions in our democracy: by citizens trying to persuade one another and then voting,\u8221\'20\'1D Alito wrote, quoting the late Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But as it urges returning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the political sphere, the supreme court has sanctioned a manipulation of the political process that makes it nearly impossible for Ohioans and voters in other states to make their voices heard on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In 2019, Alito and four of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative justices said federal courts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/18pdf/18-422_9ol1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 could not do anything}}}\uc2  to police partisan gerrymandering, giving lawmakers in Ohio and elsewhere {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/18pdf/18-422_9ol1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more freedom to gerrymander their districts}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That kind of gerrymandering will probably serve as an invisible, virtually impenetrable fortress that will allow lawmakers across the US to continue to push extreme \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures that are unsupported by the public. Although public attitudes about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/where-americans-stand-on-abortion-in-5-charts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can be complex}}}\uc2 , the vast majority do not support overturning Roe v Wade and a majority supports legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in some form. State lawmakers who have pushed measures criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and outlawing it entirely have ignored those attitudes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are different strands of the same braid. We don\u8217\'20\'19t have those restrictions without the gerrymandering,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kellie Copeland, the executive director of Pro-Choice Ohio, a group that works to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state.\par \par \uc2 When Ohio was considering the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, Copeland said, her organization facilitated a \u8220\'20\'1Cparade of witnesses\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 medical professionals, women who had abortions, religious leaders \u8211\'20\'13 to give emotional testimony to the legislature. Many lawmakers didn\u8217\'20\'19t stick around to listen. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t care. And they don\u8217\'20\'19t care because they know they\u8217\'20\'19re untouchable because of gerrymandering,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a problem that exists beyond Ohio. The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/where-americans-stand-on-abortion-in-5-charts/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vast majority}}}\uc2  of Americans believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in at least some circumstances, but state lawmakers continue to offer a blitz of increasingly extreme restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Republicans control far more state legislative chambers than Democrats do and only about 17.5% of state legislative districts are expected to be competitive over the next decade. Very {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/24/gerrymandering-wisconsin-purple-state-rightwing-measures"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  few chambers}}}\uc2  are expected to flip partisan control. In Ohio, Republicans have once again come up with a state legislative map distorted to their advantage and have openly defied the state supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s orders to come up with a fairer map.\par \par \uc2 Extreme restrictions with extreme consequences\par \par \uc2 In 2010, Kasich {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/politics/2010/11/republican_john_kasich_headed.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 had ousted}}}\uc2  an incumbent Democrat and Republicans flipped control of the Ohio house as part of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.redistrictingmajorityproject.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nationwide Republican effort}}}\uc2  aimed at winning state legislative chambers to control the redistricting process. Armed with complete redistricting power, Ohio Republicans drew new districts that allowed them to win a supermajority in the state legislature throughout the last decade, even as Barack Obama carried the state in 2008 and 2012.\par \par \uc2 A wave of new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/22054878-ohio-legislative-timeline_january-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 began to flow}}}\uc2 . In 2012, the state enacted a new law banning abortions after a fetus was viable, except in cases of medical emergency, and requiring viability testing at 20 weeks ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/health/2021/12/01/what-is-viability/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three to four weeks before}}}\uc2  the accepted medical definition of viability). The next year, Ohio lawmakers tucked a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/us-usa-ohio-budget/ohio-governor-signs-budget-including-tax-cut-anti-abortion-provision-idUSBRE96003A20130701"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 number of restrictions}}}\uc2  into a budget bill, including a hugely consequential measure that prevented \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics from entering into required patient-transfer agreements with taxpayer-funded hospitals. The state went on to prohibit certain government money from going to Planned Parenthood, ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 20 weeks post-fertilization outright and, in 2019, passed the six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThroughout the \u8217\'20\'1980s, \u8217\'20\'1990s, early 2000s, there were occasional laws that would tinker with the informed consent requirement for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or tinker around the edges with minors access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and things like that,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessie Hill, a law professor at Case Western Univerity. \u8220\'20\'1CWe really started to see an uptick in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions after 2010, or 2011, the last time the redistricting took effect in Ohio. It\u8217\'20\'19s been since then, just sort of increasingly extreme restrictions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Those restrictions produced extreme consequences. Between 2011 and 2015, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/369c2391530e41a8b351961edcdadf48"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 seven of the state\u8217\'20\'19s 16}}}\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics either closed or curtailed their operations (six full-service clinics {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prochoiceohio.org/clinics/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 remain open today }}}\uc2  with three additional clinics providing medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services). A complete ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state could increase the average distance a woman has to travel in Ohio to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic from an average of 26 miles to as much as 269 miles in a worst-case scenario, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1363/psrh.12191"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to one recent study}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a burden that significantly harms those in rural areas, who have seen clinics in their counties close and who will have to take more time off work to travel.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s kind of death by a thousand cuts,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sri Thakkilapati, the interim executive director of PreTerm, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Cleveland. \u8220\'20\'1CMaybe any one or two of these things you could overcome, but all together it becomes a really burdensome process,\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As the supreme court weighs overturning Roe v Wade, Ohio is now considering a virtually {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ohiocapitaljournal.com/2022/04/29/ohio-bill-would-ban-abortion-without-rape-exemption/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 complete ban on \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 . Such a ban would mean that those seeking an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to pay \u8220\'20\'1Cexorbitant amounts of income\u8221\'20\'1D to travel to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of state, said Danielle Bessett, a professor at the \uc2 University of Cincinnati\uc2  who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who are not gonna be able to afford that travel \u8230\'20\'26 are then going to try and self-source \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care at home. And we\u8217\'20\'19ll probably see lots of inequality in how people are prosecuted and arrested for that,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, she said, there will be those who don\u8217\'20\'19t try either of those and are forced to carry their pregnancy to term. \u8220\'20\'1CThere are equity issues there, too, with Black women having the highest rate of maternal mortality,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 As lawmakers have pushed these severe restrictions, they have consistently remained out of line with what most Ohioans believe. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cleveland.com/open/2020/10/despite-ohio-laws-limiting-abortion-majority-of-voters-think-abortion-should-remain-legal-poll-shows.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Polls}}}\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.suffolk.edu/news-features/news/2022/06/06/15/41/suffolk-poll-shows-ohio-voters-want-abortion-rights-protected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 consistently shown}}}\uc2  that a majority of Ohio voters support some form of legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while a minority believes it should be illegal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose who are anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and claim a faith tradition, they don\u8217\'20\'19t speak for me. They don\u8217\'20\'19t represent the countless folks who are faithfully pro-choice. Same thing with our elected leaders. They don\u8217\'20\'19t represent who we are and what we believe in our communities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Elaina Ramsey, the executive director of Faith Choice Ohio, which works to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Thakkilapati agrees. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s frustrating. In some ways it\u8217\'20\'19s hopeful that people do think that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be a right and should exist for people in Ohio. It\u8217\'20\'19s helpful to know that there are more of us. But in some ways it\u8217\'20\'19s very disheartening,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt feels like it\u8217\'20\'19s not gonna make a difference.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220608ei68000rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
