\par\par \uc2 Global development\par \b \uc2 Too many have suffered under Ghana\u8217\'20\'19s  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  laws. Ending Roe v Wade risks our hope for change\b0\par\par\uc2 Bisi Adjapon \par \uc2 946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2022\par 13:32 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a country where stigma, poverty and religious beliefs compound anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, a rollback on US rights will only embolden extremists\par \par \uc2 No one knew she\u8217\'20\'19d had multiple abortions. Not even me, a lifelong friend. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t even count how many times I had to do it. He wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t let me use contraception and he wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t wear anything.\u8221\'20\'1D Sitting opposite her at a restaurant, I felt deep anguish. This was a prominent Ghanaian official.\par \par \uc2 She told me how my novel, The Teller of Secrets, had stirred memories she had tried to suppress. It was not the only shame-filled confession triggered by the book. An award-winning Ghanaian film-maker messaged me to share harrowing accounts of a Muslim woman\u8217\'20\'19s abortions she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t make public. Literary friends of mine have confided similar pain. Almost every African woman I know, mid-30s and above, has had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or two, or more.\par \par \uc2 A male relative confessed to me, with minimal repentance, how he subjected women to abortions rather than permit contraception, even threatening to leave them if they didn\u8217\'20\'19t comply. In a culture where marriage validates women, they are often powerless to refuse. But nothing compares to the horror a child must feel when she finds herself pregnant, like when my 13-year-old niece was raped. Her mother, a staunch Christian, forced my niece to drop out of school and give birth, setting off a cycle of poverty, more children and a disastrous marriage that finally culminated in her tragic death. If her mother had allowed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I would have wept but refused to judge. I was in no position to help financially or adopt the baby. My niece is merely one illustration of child mothers.\par \par \uc2 Poor people send their daughters into servitude in wealthy homes where they\u8217\'20\'19re often forced to sleep with the master or older offspring. When a child is forced to give birth it can lead to a torn womb, or the wretched condition of fistula where the bladder is eroded.\par \par \uc2 It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have to happen like this. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal in Ghana, except in cases of incest, rape, foetal abnormalities or when a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk. But ignorance, poverty, religious belief and stigma prevent girls and women from seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even when it would be legal.\par \par \uc2 Where it is illegal, money makes it possible. Many doctors willingly perform abortions for those with cash. Those who can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to pay subject themselves to dubious procedures under unsanitary conditions. Women guzzle whole bottles of gin or ingest harmful chemicals. Coat hangers have breached cervixes to provoke contractions. Women have had their wombs scraped without anaesthesia, have bled to death or contracted infections that have scarred their innards and left them infertile. When such a woman is caught, she is shamed and punished. It is only when she dies that society\u8217\'20\'19s wrath turns to the impregnator. Someone has to pay, after all.\par \par \uc2 In a land where so-called churches sprout up seemingly daily, where pastors have flogged pregnant teens or stomped on stomachs, fear of God\u8217\'20\'19s wrath paralyses. Lately, there have been calls to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC1790853/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  legalise \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , pointing to its lawfulness in western countries, especially in the US whose influence prompted Ghana to overhaul its educational system in 1988. So now there is unease at the potential overturning of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  zealots in the US consider some forms of contraception as equivalent to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The draft supreme court opinion does not refer to it, but some legal experts fear it could mean reduced access to contraception. The Republican governor of Mississippi has refused to rule out {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/09/mississippi-governor-contraception-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 banning some forms of contraception}}}\uc2 . Bill Gothard, whose curriculum is used by many home schoolers, exhorts women to abstain from destroying babies through contraception, but to rather procreate to raise an army for Christ. At a seminar I once attended in Virginia, he quoted from the Bible that women will be preserved through childbirth, concluding that having multiple births is a fountain of youth for women.\par \par \uc2 Anti-abortionists have called for defunding Planned Parenthood and so international family planning programmes, whose largest donor is \uc2 USAid\uc2 , are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2017/jan/24/how-the-us-trump-global-gag-rule-threatens-health-clinics-across-kenya-and-uganda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 already under threat}}}\uc2 . These family planning clinics provide nutrition, prenatal care, counselling and baby-wellness to the poor. They reduce abortions, and maternal and infant mortality.\par \par \uc2 So here\u8217\'20\'19s the question: since women are the ones who get pregnant, shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t they have unfettered access to contraception? Shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t they exercise the right to pull out any unwanted seeds planted by those who value them as little more than soil to be cultivated at will? Shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t any female be the decider of what happens inside her body, of what affects her future? Overturning Roe v Wade will rip agency from all women and embolden extremists worldwide.\par \par \uc2 We must raise girls who feel valued, who don\u8217\'20\'19t view marriage as aspirational, who are financially independent. Health workers must be better educated to stop shaming girls for seeking contraception. Most of all, Ghana\u8217\'20\'19s national insurance scheme must cover reproductive health, so that women\u8217\'20\'19s fate is less reliant on foreign policies. A woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose is a right that must be defended.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bisi Adjapon is a Ghanaian American author who lives in Ghana. Her first novel {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://harpercollins.co.uk/products/the-teller-of-secrets-a-novel-bisi-adjapon?variant=39414017654862"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Teller of Secrets}}}\uc2  is published by \uc2 HarperCollins\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Sign up for Her Stage to hear directly from incredible women in the developing world on the issues that matter to them, delivered to your inbox monthly:\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220607ei67002pa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
