\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Will anti-abortionists use \u8216\'20\'18uterus surveillance\u8217\'20\'19 against women in the US?\b0\par\par\uc2 Arwa Mahdawi \par \uc2 719 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2022\par 16:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 If, as is expected, Roe v Wade is overturned by the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , 26 states are certain or likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and data tracking could mean there\u8217\'20\'19s nowhere for women to hide\par \par \uc2 If you are looking for a cheerful column that will make you giggle and distract you from everything that is wrong with the world, click away now. This week I have nothing but doom, gloom and data trackers for you. If you are hoping to sink into a well of existential despair, maybe let out a few screams into the void, then you\u8217\'20\'19ve come to the right place.\par \par \uc2 Here goes: the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , as you are no doubt aware, is expected to overturn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2  and the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  very soon. At least 13 Republican-led states have \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/03/us/state-abortion-trigger-laws-roe-v-wade-overturned/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trigger laws}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D in place, which means that the moment Roe is overruled, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be fully or partly banned. Other states will follow suit. According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a pro-choice research organisation, 26 states are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2021/10/26-states-are-certain-or-likely-ban-abortion-without-roe-heres-which-ones-and-why"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 certain or likely to ban \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  when Roe falls.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps you are the glass half-full sort. Perhaps you are thinking: \u8220\'20\'1CWell, at least people can travel to a state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal.\u8221\'20\'1D Unfortunately, it\u8217\'20\'19s not that simple. There are the obvious logistical and financial constraints, for one thing. Then there\u8217\'20\'19s the fact that we live in a world of mass surveillance: pretty much everything we do these days leaves a digital footprint \u8211\'20\'13 one that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  extremists will not hesitate to weaponise. One Democratic senator has described the potential of new technology to track down and punish anyone who might even be thinking of having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/20/uterus-surveillance-the-scramble-to-keep-abortion-data-from-prying-eyes-00033775"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 uterus surveillance}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. Expect to see a big rise in this, not least because some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  states are providing financial incentives to snitch on your fellow citizens. Texas, for example, has passed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/02/sonia-sotomayor-dissent-texas-abortion-ban-law-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cbounty hunter\u8221\'20\'1D laws}}}\uc2  promising at least $10,000 to individuals who help enforce the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban by successfully suing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider.\par \par \uc2 To be fair, there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing new about uterus surveillance. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists may be stuck in the past when it comes to reproductive rights, but they have always been adept at using modern technology to further their goals. One tactic they\u8217\'20\'19ve used for decades is standing outside clinics and recording the licence plates of anyone who enters. As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://buffalonews.com/news/bill-seeks-to-halt-tracing-of-licenses-reports-show-abortion-foes-have-used-method-to/article_047dcbaa-200a-58dc-8143-3a79481b75ec.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 far back as 1993}}}\uc2 , extremists were tracing the people connected to those licence plates, obtaining their phone numbers, then calling up to harass them. Years ago tracing someone took a bit of time and effort. Nowadays, you can look up someone\u8217\'20\'19s personal information with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/mar/23/data-brokers-lawsuit-security-transparency"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 click of a button}}}\uc2  and a small fee.\par \par \uc2 The wonders of the modern world mean there are a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/apr/04/us-law-enforcement-agencies-access-your-data-apple-meta"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mind-boggling number of ways}}}\uc2  in which you can now identify anyone who might be thinking about an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . To begin with, there\u8217\'20\'19s location data. Vice media recently reported that a data location company is selling information related to Planned Parenthood facilities (many of which provide abortions). The data shows where groups of people visiting the locations came from, how long they stayed and where they went afterwards. That data is aggregated so it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t provide the names of individuals; however, de-anonymising this sort of information is not very difficult. There is plenty of evidence that location data is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2021/07/catholic-priest-quits-after-anonymized-data-revealed-alleged-use-of-grindr/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost never anonymous}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Period-tracking apps, which are used by millions of people, are also a worrying source of potentially incriminating information in a post-Roe world. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/06/02/period-tracking-apps-gather-intimate-data-new-bill-aims-curb-mass-collection/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Experts have warned}}}\uc2  that rightwing organisations could buy data from these apps and use it to prove that someone was pregnant then had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Your text messages could also be used against you, as could your browser history. Indeed, authorities in Mississippi {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/05/tech/abortion-laws-phone-privacy/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have already used}}}\uc2  a woman\u8217\'20\'19s online search for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to indict her for second-degree murder after she miscarried. That happened in 2018; imagine what is going to happen in a post-Roe world. Speaking of which, I\u8217\'20\'19ve just realised I Googled the word \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D 100 times while researching this. I\u8217\'20\'19m off to scrub my search history.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Arwa Mahdawi is a Guardian columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220607ei67001xi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
