\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  and guns may awaken a slumbering giant for Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 844 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 June 2022\par 13:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A mobilisation such as America has rarely seen could propel Democrats to larger majorities this November\par \par \uc2 Two of the most basic human aspirations are making one\u8217\'20\'19s own decisions about whether or when to have a child, free from government interference, and keeping any child one does have out of harm\u8217\'20\'19s way, secure against random violence.\par \par \uc2 Yet both aspirations have been fiercely resisted in the United States \u8211\'20\'13 the first by many evangelical Christians, the second by the gun lobby.\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers are in the pockets of both. Democratic lawmakers are on the side of reproductive freedom and gun control.\par \par \uc2 It has become the sharpest divide in contemporary American politics.\par \par \uc2 The American people are not evenly divided on these issues. A large majority wants to maintain access to abortions during the first trimester of pregnancy, which has been the rule since the supreme court decided Roe v Wade in 1973.\par \par \uc2 An even larger majority (including many Republican voters) support requiring universal background checks for would-be gun buyers, and most favor banning high-capacity magazines and the sale of assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 Do the opinions of the majority matter on these two issues, where politically potent minorities have demanded the opposite? At first glance, it seems not.\par \par \uc2 After the 2 May {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/magazine/2022/05/16/how-alitos-draft-opinion-on-abortion-rights-would-change-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leak of a draft opinion}}}\uc2  in the case of Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization, written by \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and evidently joined by four other Republican-appointed Justices \u8211\'20\'13 which argues that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/news/daily-comment/why-there-are-no-women-in-the-constitution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 no right to \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  can be found in the constitution}}}\uc2  and that, therefore, no such right exists \u8211\'20\'13 Senate Democrats tried to codify a national right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But on 11 May, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.congress.gov/bill/117th-congress/senate-bill/4132/text"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act}}}\uc2  failed in the Senate, by a vote of 49 to 51. That was short not only of a simple majority but, more importantly, of the super-majority of 60 votes required to overcome the inevitable filibuster. (Only the West Virginia Democrat Joe Manchin crossed party lines.)\par \par \uc2 Now, in the wake of last week\u8217\'20\'19s massacre of 19 children and two teachers in Uvalde, Texas, Congress is about to vote on regulating guns. Almost no one believes there are 10 Republican senators who will support any form of gun control, even after last week\u8217\'20\'19s horror.\par \par \uc2 While steadfastly refusing to maintain access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services and refusing all recent attempts to control guns, Republican lawmakers at the federal and state levels also remain opposed to government funding for childcare, parental leave, sex education and contraception, and for reproductive, maternal, neo-natal and pediatric health services.\par \par \uc2 It takes a great deal to awaken the slumbering giant of American voters. Most do not belong to either major political party. Many are turned off by politics. In the typical midterm election, only about half of those who are eligible to vote do so.\par \par \uc2 Yet every so often the slumbering giant awakens \u8211\'20\'13 and with a swoop of its huge arm at the ballot box remedies the growing disconnect between what voters want and what politicians do (or fail to do).\par \par \uc2 In the 2014 midterms, only 20% of young people between the ages of 18 and 29 went to the polls.\par \par \uc2 But in the 2018 midterms, after two years of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and congressional Republicans trampling on issues young people cared about \u8211\'20\'13 such as the environment, education and protection for undocumented immigrants who came to America as children \u8211\'20\'13 young voters were stirred to action: 36% of them voted. That was enough to switch control of the House to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Most pundits are convinced that the Democrats are doomed to lose the House and Senate in the upcoming midterms. They point to the fact that after 15 months in office, Biden is polling badly, at about 40%.\par \par \uc2 But the punditocracy is ignoring the disconnect between what most Americans want on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and guns and what Republican lawmakers are doing.\par \par \uc2 The two issues of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and guns may have a larger impact on Americans together than they have had separately because of the moral relationship between them \u8211\'20\'13 being free to decide whether and when to have children and keeping children safe from gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (The pundits also forget that at the same point in his presidency, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was polling at about 40%. But as inflation declined, Reagan ran for re-election against \uc2 Walter Mondale\uc2  and won 49 states.)\par \par \uc2 If the slumbering giant does awaken, a mobilization such as America has rarely seen could propel Democrats to larger majorities in the House and Senate this November \u8211\'20\'13 giving them enough votes in the Senate to eliminate the filibuster and consigning Republicans to a near permanent minority.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich, a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California at Berkeley\uc2  and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His new book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It,}}}\uc2  is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220601ei61001jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
