\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 DC wants to be a sanctuary for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2 . Could Congress stop it?\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,082 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 May 2022\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Congress holds power over the district\u8217\'20\'19s laws and finances. A Republican majority could overrule local pro-choice legislation\par \par \uc2 The patient came from Texas. Her pregnancy was too far along to end it in that state, where abortions are banned after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 She was a bundle of nerves: terrified a bounty hunter might report her for seeking the procedure, even out of state; nervous about the logistics of the trip and meeting a new provider; worried the procedure might take longer than expected, or, worse, not happen at all.\par \par \uc2 Family and friends had pulled together the resources for travel, for the procedure itself, and for childcare, but the patient needed to get back to her kids the same day.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was this sense of pressure because it\u8217\'20\'19s like, all right, no matter how busy the schedule is, we\u8217\'20\'19ve got to get this person out so that this person can get their flight to be able to get home,\u8221\'20\'1D said Serina Floyd, the patient\u8217\'20\'19s physician in Washington DC, more than a thousand miles away.\par \par \uc2 In this case, everything went fine \u8211\'20\'13 but it underscored the complications of seeking and providing health care in a country increasingly hostile to reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 After the explosive leak of the draft of the supreme court decision that would reverse the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US, providers like Floyd are gearing up for surges of patients from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be or is already heavily restricted or banned.\par \par \uc2 But in this fraught moment, Washington is in a particularly unusual spot. Non-residents frequently seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care here, and local leaders are weighing legislation to designate it an official sanctuary city for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The new law would protect patients and providers from out-of-state investigations or \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/05/06/dc-sanctuary-city-abortion-brianne-nadeau/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Texas-style bounty claims}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, where ordinary citizens may sue anyone suspected of aiding or performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet Congress has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://statehood.dc.gov/page/congressional-intervention"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 outsized control}}}\uc2  of the city\u8217\'20\'19s laws and finances, with the ability to block any local legislation before it becomes law, to set new laws for the district independent of voters, and to restrict the use of local taxpayer dollars and federal funding to the city \u8211\'20\'13 giving rise to worries that Congress could block future laws protecting reproductive health or implement new ones against voters\u8217\'20\'19 will.\par \par \uc2 While DC residents are US citizens who pay local and federal taxes, they have no representatives to Congress to control these outcomes. \u8220\'20\'1CShadow\u8221\'20\'1D representatives to Congress from DC are not allowed to vote on federal laws. The local refrain is that Washingtonians have \u8220\'20\'1Ctaxation without representation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Because Congress also has final approval of the district\u8217\'20\'19s funds, federal lawmakers can overrule laws passed locally on how to use DC taxpayer dollars. Congress has blocked previous laws passed by the district that would grant health insurance for same-sex domestic partners, legalize cannabis, and set up needle-exchange programs, for instance.\par \par \uc2 Using federal funds, including Medicaid, for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prohibited under the Hyde Amendment. But in 1988, Congress went a step further in DC with the Dornan Amendment, also banning the use of local taxpayer funds for abortions \u8211\'20\'13 a decision that all 50 states are permitted to make on their own.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, DC passed legislation protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and access, a move that \u8220\'20\'1Cvery clearly put forth the will of the people in the district,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Laura Meyers\uc2 , president and CEO of Planned Parenthood of Metropolitan Washington.\par \par \uc2 But local leaders are concerned that a more conservative Congress could overrule these rights and ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the district outright, or further limit the city\u8217\'20\'19s ability to provide resources to patients seeking reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am very concerned that if Republicans take control, we could even have a Texas-style \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bounty system here, and I\u8217\'20\'19m also concerned if Roe is overturned that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be banned in DC,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Eleanor Holmes Norton\uc2 , DC\u8217\'20\'19s nonvoting delegate to the \uc2 US House of Representatives\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDC already considers itself a sanctuary city,\u8221\'20\'1D Norton said. Yet \u8220\'20\'1Cwe are vulnerable in a way no other jurisdiction is.\u8221\'20\'1D She is continuing to push for DC to become the nation\u8217\'20\'19s 51st state, which would grant it the same autonomy that other US states have.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUntil DC has full statehood, of course, we stay in this limbo,\u8221\'20\'1D Meyers said. \u8220\'20\'1CCongress has at various points in time treated DC like a political football. It has experimented, in terms of policy, in ways that a congressman from, let\u8217\'20\'19s say, Oklahoma, would never dream of doing to Nebraska.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 DC has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://worldpopulationreview.com/state-rankings/abortion-rates-by-state"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  rate}}}\uc2  in the US, but the majority of abortions \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/womens-health-policy/state-indicator/abortions-by-out-of-state-residents/?currentTimeframe=0&selectedRows=%7B%22states%22:%7B%22district-of-columbia%22:%7B%7D%7D%7D&sortModel=%7B%22colId%22:%22Location%22,%22sort%22:%22asc%22%7D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 69%}}}\uc2  in 2019 \u8211\'20\'13 are for patients from elsewhere. Many come from neighboring Virginia and Maryland, as well as from Pennsylvania, Florida, Georgia, Ohio, and Texas.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are already seeing an increase in patients from other states,\u8221\'20\'1D Meyers said. She expects to see a greater rise as more states pass restrictions and in light of the expected fall of Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 Providers like Floyd are already planning for a surge \u8211\'20\'13 hiring patient navigators to help people access care, changing staffing and workflow plans to accommodate as many patients as possible, and increasing telehealth services to provide medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , among other services.\par \par \uc2 Patients from states restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to travel long distances to access health care. It\u8217\'20\'19s an expensive and time-consuming process \u8211\'20\'13 finding a place to stay, arranging transportation, taking off work, finding childcare, and paying for all of it \u8211\'20\'13 that will exacerbate inequality and add more pressure to already marginalized patients, experts said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis will fall disproportionately on poor women, especially poor women of color, and we will continue to see ever-widening disparities and inequities,\u8221\'20\'1D Meyers said.\par \par \uc2 The US has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest rate}}}\uc2  of maternal mortality in the developed world, and black women in particular are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/hestat/maternal-mortality/2020/maternal-mortality-rates-2020.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 three times}}}\uc2  more likely than white women to die from pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Such inequalities are sharply felt in DC, where the maternal mortality rate is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ocme.dc.gov/sites/default/files/dc/sites/ocme/agency_content/Maternal%20Mortality%20Review%20Committee%20Annual%20Report_Finalv2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 twice}}}\uc2  that of the rest of America, Floyd said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat inequity is extreme.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Floyd also worries about legislation that could prohibit travel for out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and open providers up to legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very important question and a very real potential risk,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re facing is very scary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But, she added, \u8220\'20\'1CI do this work for a reason.\u8221\'20\'1D And she has no intention of stopping.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220529ei5t000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
