\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Arkansas Republican admits  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  trigger law would cause \u8216\'20\'18heartbreak\u8217\'20\'19 if Roe is reversed\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed Pilkington in New York \par \uc2 658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2022\par 12:46 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Governor Asa Hutchinson signed near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban bill, even though he disagreed with the lack of exceptions for incest and rape\par \par \uc2 The Republican governor of Arkansas, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , has admitted that an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  trigger law that he signed on to the books would lead to \u8220\'20\'1Cheartbreaking circumstances\u8221\'20\'1D if Roe v Wade is overturned, in which girls as young as 11 who became pregnant through rape or incest would be forced to give birth.\par \par \uc2 Hutchinson\u8217\'20\'19s remarks give a revealing insight into the twisted human and political quandaries that are certain to arise should the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 , as expected, destroy the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  enshrined in Roe v Wade when it issues its ruling next month. The governor told CNN\u8217\'20\'19s State of the Union on Sunday that in 2019 he had signed the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.arkleg.state.ar.us/Bills/Detail?id=SB6&ddBienniumSession=2021%2F2021R"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Arkansas trigger law, Senate Bill 6}}}\uc2 , which would ban almost all abortions the instant Roe were reversed, even though he disagreed with its lack of exceptions for incest and rape.\par \par \uc2 Asked why he had put his signature on the law, despite the fact that it would prohibit all abortions other than in cases where a pregnant woman\u8217\'20\'19s life were in imminent danger, he said: \u8220\'20\'1CI support the exceptions of rape and incest \u8230\'20\'26 I believe that should have been added; it did not have the support of the assembly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Under intense questioning from the CNN host Dana Bash, the governor was asked why an \u8220\'20\'1C11- or 12-year-old girl who is impregnated by her father, or uncle or another family member be forced to carry that child to term?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He replied: \u8220\'20\'1CI agree with you. I\u8217\'20\'19ve had to deal with that particular circumstance even as governor. While it\u8217\'20\'19s still life in the womb, life of the unborn, the conception was in criminal circumstances \u8211\'20\'13 either incest or rape \u8211\'20\'13 and so those are two exceptions I think are very appropriate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added that if the supreme court does throw out the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , then \u8220\'20\'1Cthese are going to become very real circumstances. The debate and discussion will continue, and that could very well be revisited.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Bash pressed Hutchinson on what would happen if the absence of rape and incest exceptions cannot be revisited in the law that he had personally approved, pointing out that his term as governor comes to an end in January. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you can\u8217\'20\'19t change [the trigger law], that means girls who are still children, 11- and 12-year-olds, might be in that situation in a very real way in just a couple of months,\u8221\'20\'1D Bash said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose are heartbreaking circumstances,\u8221\'20\'1D Hutchinson replied. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we passed these trigger laws we were trying \u8230\'20\'26 to reduce abortions, but whenever you see that real-life circumstances like that the debate is going to continue and the will of the people may or may not change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A report by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/06/no-exception-rape-incest-anti-abortion-laws-republicans-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Guardian this month}}}\uc2  found that at least 11 US states have passed laws that ban abortions without any exceptions for rape or incest. Such trigger laws are legally written in such a way that they would come into effect the second that the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  embodied in Roe were overturned.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a draft majority opinion}}}\uc2  of the supreme court written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  was leaked to Politico. With the apparent backing of five of the six conservative justices on the nine-member court, it would eradicate federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the most aggressive terms.\par \par \uc2 The court has insisted that the draft is not final and that changes to its wording or outcome are still possible. But the country on both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  divide is bracing now for Roe to be undone and power over women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive choices to be handed to individual states like Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220522ei5m001xw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
