\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US Senate\uc2  passes $40bn aid package for Ukraine \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Luscombe \par \uc2 5,919 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par 20:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 All Democrats and all but 11 Republicans supported the billBiden backs Sweden and Finland as members of \u8216\'20\'18revived Nato\u8217\'20\'19Congress members led \u8216\'20\'18reconnaissance tours\u8217\'20\'19 of Capitol, panel suggestsSign up to receive First Thing \u8211\'20\'13 our daily briefing by email\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing summary\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a wrap for Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s US politics blog, but our {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/may/19/russia-ukraine-war-latest-russian-use-of-laser-weapons-shows-invasion-a-failure-says-zelenskiy-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 global live blog of the Ukraine conflict continues here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s what we followed today:\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  has (finally) passed a $40bn package of military, economic and humanitarian aid for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cproud\u8221\'20\'1D to support applications by Finland and Sweden to join \uc2 Nato\uc2 , after meeting the countries\u8217\'20\'19 leaders at the White House, saying their addition will strengthen the defense alliance. But Turkey says it can\u8217\'20\'19t support the move.\par \par \uc2 * Oklahoma has passed the nation\u8217\'20\'19s most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, a total ban beginning at conception which allows citizens to sue anyone who \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D a woman in terminating a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 * The 6 January House panel wants to hear from Republican congressman \uc2 Barry Loudermilk\uc2  about a \u8220\'20\'1Ctour\u8221\'20\'1D he allegedly hosted at the Capitol building the day before it was overrun by a mob of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters.\par \par \uc2 * The homeland security department has suspended the government\u8217\'20\'19s troubled new disinformation board after the resignation of its director and a wave of Republican criticism.\par \par \uc2 * Biden is on his way to Alaska, the staging post for his onward journey on Air Force One to Seoul and Tokyo and meetings with the leaders of South Korea and Japan in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 * The alleged white supremacist behind Saturday\u8217\'20\'19s grocery store massacre in Buffalo, New York, made a brief court appearance this morning. House lawmakers sent the domestic terrorism bill to the Senate, where it faces an uncertain fate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 National security adviser Jake Sullivan has been giving a preview of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s trip to South Korea aboard Air Force One as the president and his entourage travel on the first leg towards their staging post in Alaska.\par \par \uc2 The audio feed from 38,000 feet is, how shall we say, patchy. But it seems Sullivan was asked about the likelihood of North Korea launching some kind of deliberate action or challenge to the US while Biden is in Asia to meet the leaders of South Korea and Japan:\par \par \uc2 Our analysis [is] that there is a genuine possibility, a real risk of some kind of provocation while we\u8217\'20\'19re in the region, whether in South Korea or in Japan, that could take the form of a nuclear test, the seventh nuclear test that North Korea has conducted.\par \par \uc2 It could take the form of a missile test. There have been a number of missile tests this year. And of course, North Korea has a long history going back decades of missile tests, both to advance their capabilities and to cause provocations.\par \par \uc2 We are prepared for those eventualities, we are coordinated closely with both the ROK [Republic of South Korea] and Japan, we know what we will do to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 One of the main messages we are sending on this trip is that the United States is here for our allies and partners. We are here to help provide deterrence and defense for the ROK and Japan. Our cooperation will only strengthen in the face of any further provocations by North Korea.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Chad to keep explaining things\u8221\'20\'1D to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when Trump was US president, the former White House aide \uc2 Fiona Hill\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPutin doesn\u8217\'20\'19t like to do that,\u8221\'20\'1D Hill told the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hb_spyqwVXY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Chicago Council on Global Affairs}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou could see that he got frustrated many times with President Trump. Even though he loves to be able to spin his own version of events, he wants to have predictability in the person that he\u8217\'20\'19s engaging with.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Under Trump, Hill was senior director for European and Russian affairs on the national security council. She is now a senior fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution in Washington\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The British-born adviser come to prominence when she testified in Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first impeachment for withholding military aid to Ukraine in an attempt to extract political dirt on opponents including \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Putin ordered the Russian invasion of Ukraine in February. Bloody fighting continues. Biden has committed to supporting Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 At the Chicago event, Hill said Putin \u8220\'20\'1Cthought that somebody like Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/people/biden/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ,}}}\uc2  who\u8217\'20\'19s a trans-Atlanticist, who knows all about \uc2 Nato\uc2 , who actually knows where Ukraine is, and actually knows something about the history, and is very steeped in international affairs, would be the right person to engage with as opposed to somebody that you have to explain everything to all the time, honestly\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Russian president, Hill said, might still be \u8220\'20\'1Cwaiting for us to sue for peace, [to] negotiate away Ukraine\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Trump and Republicans claim Putin would not have invaded if Trump was in power.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration was dogged by investigations of Russian election interference and links between Trump and Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/19/trump-putin-biden-ukraine-fiona-hill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Putin \u8216\'20\'18had to keep explaining things to Trump\u8217\'20\'19, ex-White House aide says}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 6 January panel seeks information on congressman's Capitol 'tour'\par \par \uc2 The 6 January House panel wants to hear from a Republican congressman about a \u8220\'20\'1Ctour\u8221\'20\'1D he allegedly hosted at the Capitol building the day before it was overrun by a mob of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters.\par \par \uc2 The committee has written to Georgia representative \uc2 Barry Loudermilk\uc2  asking for his voluntary cooperation.\par \par \uc2 Barry Loudermilk\uc2 . Photograph: Ron Harris/AP\par \par \uc2 According to a publicly released letter, panel chair and Mississippi Democrat \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  wrote:\par \par \uc2 Based on our review of evidence in the select committee\u8217\'20\'19s possession, we believe you have information regarding a tour you led through parts of the Capitol complex on January 5, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Public reporting and witness accounts indicate some individuals and groups engaged in efforts to gather information about the layout of the US Capitol, as well as the House and Senate office buildings, in advance of January 6, 2021.\par \par \uc2 Loudermilk did not immediately reply to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 The House committee is looking into Trump\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to overturn his election defeat by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-capitol-breach"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deadly riot at the Capitol}}}\uc2  by his supporters on the day Congress was certifying the result.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s move suggests the panel has evidence pointing to certain \u8220\'20\'1Creconnaissance tours\u8221\'20\'1D taking place in the days before 6 January, potentially providing some rioters with a layout of the complex, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Hugo Lowell reports.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/19/january-6-panel-congress-reconnaissance-tours-capitol"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 6 panel evidence suggests Congress members led \u8216\'20\'18reconnaissance tours\u8217\'20\'19 of the Capitol before attack}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 The words \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Crisk averse\u8221\'20\'1D rarely appear in the same sentence, but after the former president suffered mixed fortunes in some Republican primary race endorsements, and a bloodied nose in others, he\u8217\'20\'19s rethinking his strategy of prolifically backing candidates, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/19/politics/trump-endorsement-risk-pennsylvania/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to CNN}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The network says candidate in upcoming elections are likely to have a harder time winning Trump\u8217\'20\'19s coveted endorsements.\par \par \uc2 Mehmet Oz. Photograph: Hannah Beier/Reuters\par \par \uc2 An adviser tells CNN that Trump is \u8220\'20\'1Cagitated\u8221\'20\'1D by the inability of his pick Mehmet Oz, the celebrity TV doctor-turned-politician, to score a decisive victory over David McCormick in the Pennsylvania senate primary. The race is heading for an automatic recount with just a few hundred votes from Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s primary separating them.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not how he expected this to go,\u8221\'20\'1D the adviser tells CNN.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Oz loses, it puts [Trump] in an awkward spot because he absolutely trashed David McCormick at his rally and pissed off quite a few allies who never thought he should have endorsed Oz.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While Trump has scored some wins, for example a convincing victory by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/may/19/republicans-just-nominated-one-of-the-most-radical-governor-candidates-in-history"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Doug Mastriano in Pennsylvania\u8217\'20\'19s gubernatorial primary}}}\uc2 , other endorsements have fallen flat.\par \par \uc2 The scandal-plagued North Carolina congressman Madison Cawthorn {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/17/madison-cawthorn-chuck-edwards-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost his seat}}}\uc2  despite Trump\u8217\'20\'19s (admittedly late-in-the-game) backing, and former senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  looks headed for a thumping loss to incumbent Brian Kemp in Georgia\u8217\'20\'19s governor primary next Tuesday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/kemp-perdue-trump_n_6285676ae4b05c3afec44f34"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Huffpost}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A newly cautious approach to upcoming primaries by Trump would underscore his belief that in order to maintain influence inside the Republican party, his endorsement must remain powerful, CNN says.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s Republican-led legislature just passed the nation\u8217\'20\'19s strictest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban, which allows citizens to sue anyone who \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets\u8221\'20\'1D a woman in terminating a pregnancy. The ban begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 The law would take effect immediately if Republican governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  signs the bill, which he is expected to do. It would allow litigants to sue for $10,000 and \u8220\'20\'1Cemotional distress\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 . Photograph: Sue Ogrocki/AP\par \par \uc2 If the law goes into effect, it will make {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/05/oklahoma-lawmakers-bill-makes-abortion-illegal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oklahoma}}}\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cthe first state to successfully outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and eliminate access while Roe v Wade is still standing,\u8221\'20\'1D according to a spokesperson for Planned Parenthood, and have profound effects for women in Texas.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban is modeled after a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2021/oct/22/supreme-court-texas-abortion-ban-arguments"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 six-week \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  ban}}}\uc2  first passed by Texas. There, the state passed a law to allow anyone, anywhere to sue those who \u8220\'20\'1Caid and abet\u8221\'20\'1D an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at six weeks, which is before most women know they are pregnant. Consequently, thousands of Texan patients headed to Oklahoma seeking to terminate pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Oklahoma then passed a similar six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. The bill being considered by Stitt would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment an egg is fertilized, even before it implants in the uterus.\par \par \uc2 The right to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was established in the landmark 1973 supreme court decision Roe v Wade. The case provided a constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy up to the point a fetus can survive outside the womb.\par \par \uc2 In early May, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/audio/2022/may/05/us-supreme-court-ready-to-overturn-right-to-abortion-what-happens-next-podcast"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked draft opinion}}}\uc2  written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito Jr\uc2 , one of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives, showed a majority of justices considered reversing Roe v Wade outright. If they followed through with that draft decision, at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/05/abortion-deserts-clinics-access-closed-map"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 US states}}}\uc2  would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 certain or likely to ban \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thousands of patients in the Midwest and South would then be forced to seek care across state lines, sometimes traveling hundreds of miles. Perhaps as many as one-in-five, according to the estimates of one economist, would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/14/dr-tlaleng-mofokeng-roe-v-wade-reversal-un"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 forced to carry a pregnancy to term}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a heartwarming image that encapsulates the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s celebrated spirits of bipartisanship, camaraderie and mutual friendship: Democratic and Republican senators prepare to take lunch with Sweden\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  and Finland\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2  on Capitol Hill today .\par \par \uc2 The leaders held a closed-door meeting with the senators after their earlier summit with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the White House to discuss the Nordic nations\u8217\'20\'19 historic application to join \uc2 Nato\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressional leaders including Democratic senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and Republican senate minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and a bipartisan group of senators, meet with Sweden\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  and Finland\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Sauli Niinisto\uc2 . Photograph: Drew Angerer/\uc2 Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Interim summary\par \par \uc2 Time to take stock of developments so far today:\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  has (finally) passed a $40bn package of military, economic and humanitarian aid for Ukraine. \uc2 Andriy Yermak\uc2 , chief of staff to Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2 , says thank you, and predicts it will spur his country to victory.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cproud\u8221\'20\'1D to support applications by Finland and Sweden to join \uc2 Nato\uc2 , after meeting the countries\u8217\'20\'19 leaders at the White House, saying their addition will strengthen the defense alliance. But Turkey says it can\u8217\'20\'19t support the move.\par \par \uc2 * The homeland security department has suspended the government\u8217\'20\'19s troubled new disinformation board after the resignation of its director and a wave of Republican criticism.\par \par \uc2 * Biden is on his way to Alaska, the staging post for his onward journey on Air Force One to Seoul and meetings with the leaders of South Korea and Japan in the coming days. We\u8217\'20\'19ll hear from White House press secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  and national security adviser Jake Sullivan aboard the flight a little later.\par \par \uc2 * The alleged white supremacist behind Saturday\u8217\'20\'19s grocery store massacre in Buffalo, New York, made a brief court appearance this morning. House lawmakers have sent the domestic terrorism bill to the Senate, where it faces an uncertain fate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Ukraine thanks US for $40bn aid, predicts victory\par \par \uc2 A top aide to Ukrainian president \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskiy\uc2  has thanked the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  after it approved nearly $40 billion in aid, saying this would help ensure the defeat of Russia, Reuters writes.\par \par \uc2 Andriy Yermak\uc2 , chief of staff to Zelenskiy, posted praise online.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTogether we\u8217\'20\'19ll win,\u8221\'20\'1D he tweeted.\par \par \uc2 enltrThanks \uc2 US Senate\uc2  for the historic decision to provide $40 billion aid package to ????. Together, we'll win.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Andriy Yermak\uc2  (@AndriyYermak) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AndriyYermak/status/1527341427075973120?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 19, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 He also said:\par \par \uc2 We are moving towards victory confidently and strategically. We thank our allies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In addition, \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  (\uc2 G7\uc2 ) financial leaders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/may/19/russia-ukraine-war-latest-russian-use-of-laser-weapons-shows-invasion-a-failure-says-zelenskiy-live?page=with:block-62867dd38f08bebdc756037b#block-62867dd38f08bebdc756037b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have agreed}}}\uc2  on $18.4bn (\u163\'00\'A314.7bn) to help {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/ukraine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ukraine}}}\uc2  and said they were ready to stand by Kyiv and \u8220\'20\'1Cdo more as needed\u8221\'20\'1D , according to a draft communique seen by Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Finance ministers and central bank governors of the US, Japan, Canada, Britain, Germany, France and Italy are holding talks as Ukraine is running out of cash.\par \par \uc2 G7\uc2  countries have \u8220\'20\'1Cmobilised $18.4 billion of budget support, including $9.2 billion of recent commitments\u8221\'20\'1D in 2022, the draft said.\par \par \uc2 You can read more about this and all the news from the ground and Europe on the war in Ukraine in our global live blog on the crisis, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/may/19/russia-ukraine-war-latest-russian-use-of-laser-weapons-shows-invasion-a-failure-says-zelenskiy-live?page=with:block-62867dd38f08bebdc756037b#block-62867dd38f08bebdc756037b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Senate passing the $40bn aid bill for Ukraine with a richly bipartisan vote was a glaring exception to the partisan divisions that have hindered work on other issues under Biden.\par \par \uc2 They promise to become only less bridgeable as November\u8217\'20\'19s elections for control of Congress draw closer, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 That includes Republicans blocking Democrats from including billions to combat the relentless pandemic in the measure, leaving their efforts to battle Covid-19 in limbo.\par \par \uc2 Last week the House approved the Ukraine bill 368-57, with all of those opposed Republicans. Though support in both chambers was unmistakably bipartisan, the GOP defections were noteworthy after Trump, still a potent force in the party, complained that such sums should first be targeted at domestic problems.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority leader and New York Democrat \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said there could be yet more aid packages for Ukraine from the US.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re doing the fighting, they\u8217\'20\'19re the ones getting killed, they\u8217\'20\'19re the ones struggling and suffering. The least we can do is give them the weaponry they need,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  on Capitol Hill today. Photograph: Leah Millis/Reuters\par \par \uc2 Schumer called it \u8220\'20\'1Cbeyond troubling\u8221\'20\'1D that Republicans were opposing the Ukraine assistance.\par \par \uc2 It appears more and more that MAGA Republicans are on the same soft-on-Putin playbook that we saw used by former President Trump,\u8221\'20\'1D said Schumer, using the Make America Great Again acronym Democrats have been using to cast those Republicans as extremists.\par \par \uc2 Senate Minority leader and Kentucky Republican \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , a strong backer of the measure, addressed concerns by his GOP colleagues, saying Ukraine\u8217\'20\'19s defeat would jeopardize America\u8217\'20\'19s European trading partners, increase US security costs there and embolden autocrats in China and elsewhere to grab territory in their regions.\par \par \uc2 The most expensive and painful thing America could possibly do in the long run would be to stop investing in sovereignty, stability and deterrence before it\u8217\'20\'19s too late,\u8221\'20\'1D McConnell said.\par \par \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , center, with House Speaker and Democrat \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to his right and Kevin McCarthy, House minority leader, to his left, at an event on Capitol Hill yesterday. Photograph: Tom Brenner/Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 US Senate\uc2  approves $40bn in new aid to Ukraine\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  has approved \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s massive new military and humanitarian aid bill for Ukraine, with a huge bipartisan vote in favor of the package as allies boost the fight back against Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of its smaller neighbor.\par \par \uc2 The final vote moments ago was 86 in favor, 11 against. The vote had been expected last week until Kentucky rightwinger \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  blocked it.\par \par \uc2 All 50 Democrats in the Senate and all but 11 Republicans supported the bill, which was larger than the original $33bn one first requested by Biden last month.\par \par \uc2 The US president is expected to sign the bill into law as soon as possible. Russia invaded Ukraine three months ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHelp is on the way, really significant help. Help that could make sure that the Ukrainians are victorious,\u8221\'20\'1D Senate majority leader and New York Democrat \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Buffalo massacre will be \u8220\'20\'1Ca catalyst\u8221\'20\'1D for legislation to combat hate crime against Blacks, according to the prominent civil rights attorney {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/law/2022/apr/29/its-part-of-our-culture-to-marginalize-minorities-in-america-ben-crump-wants-to-change-that"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ben Crump}}}\uc2 , who is representing the families of several of the 10 victims.\par \par \uc2 Crump says he\u8217\'20\'19s also working with the Brady Center to achieve a reduction in gun violence in the US.\par \par \uc2 Ben Crump\uc2  (right) comforts family members of Buffalo victim Ruth Whitfield at a press briefing on Monday. Photograph: Brendan McDermid/Reuters\par \par \uc2 His office released a statement this morning announcing Crump will be representing the families of Buffalo victims Andre Mackneil and Geraldine Talley. He was already acting for the family of victim Ruth Whitfield.\par \par \uc2 Mackneil was killed as he was buying a cake for his son, whose third birthday was the day of the massacre. Talley was buying iced tea.\par \par \uc2 All three families will join Crump and veteran civil rights activist \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  at a press conference in Buffalo on Thursday afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Crump said:\par \par \uc2 I am honored to stand with these families in the face of such horror and hatred as we investigate and call for meaningful change to ensure no family has to feel such pain ever again.\par \par \uc2 I hope that one day soon these families will know their loved ones\u8217\'20\'19 deaths were a catalyst for long overdue Black anti-hate crime legislation \u8211\'20\'13 and for that we demand swift action from our elected officials.\par \par \uc2 Gun reform legislation has stalled in the Senate amid opposition from Republicans. On Wednesday, the House {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/19/us-house-domestic-terrorism-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed the domestic terrorism bill}}}\uc2  including some gun reforms and new categories of offenses for some hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 It, too, faces substantial headwinds in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 US suspends troubled 'disinformation' panel\par \par \uc2 Information is coming in that the government\u8217\'20\'19s new disinformation board is out.\par \par \uc2 The homeland security department has paused the work of the troubled panel and accepted the resignation of its director Nina Jankowicz.\par \par \uc2 Nina Jankowicz. Photograph: \uc2 United States Department of Homeland Security\par \par \uc2 She told the Associated Press hours after resigning on Wednesday that a wave of attacks and violent threats she has fielded since the board\u8217\'20\'19s launch will not stop her from speaking out about disinformation campaigns pulsing through the social media feeds of Americans:\par \par \uc2 We need to have a grownup conversation about how to deal with threats to our national security and that\u8217\'20\'19s not what happened here. I\u8217\'20\'19m not going to be silenced.\par \par \uc2 It remains to be seen how the board\u8217\'20\'19s disastrous rollout and ensuing criticism around it will damage ongoing US efforts to counter disinformation used as a weapon by Russia and other adversaries.\par \par \uc2 The homeland security secretary, Alejandro Mayorkas, acknowledged the board\u8217\'20\'19s controversy had become a distraction to the department\u8217\'20\'19s other work, which includes safeguarding US elections, two officials familiar with his decision said.\par \par \uc2 While the board has not formally been closed down, it will be reviewed by members of a DHS advisory council that is expected to make recommendations in 75 days. The \uc2 Washington Post{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2022/05/18/disinformation-board-dhs-nina-jankowicz/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first reported}}}\uc2  the board\u8217\'20\'19s pause.\par \par \uc2 The department announced the formation of the Disinformation Governance Board on 27 April with the stated goal to \u8220\'20\'1Ccoordinate countering misinformation related to homeland security.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/19/homeland-security-disinformation-board-nina-jankowicz-resigns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US homeland security pauses new disinformation board amid criticism}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Democratic lawmakers prepare to speak to the media about rising gun crime and domestic terrorism in the wake of the Buffalo massacre comes news of another multiple shooting: at a high school graduation in Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 According to the Associated Press, one person was killed and another injured in the incident Wednesday night at Middle Tennessee state university, where graduating seniors at Murfreesboro\u8217\'20\'19s Riverdale high school were being honored.\par \par \uc2 The injured victim is hospitalized in critical condition, the AP says. It was not immediately known if the victims were students.\par \par \uc2 Rutherford county sheriff Mike Fitzhugh told local media that the shooter or shooters remain at large. The incident occurred as attendees were leaving at the conclusion of the ceremony.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe shooting at \uc2 MTSU\uc2  is a tragedy for our community. We mourned with the family who lost a loved one.\par \par \uc2 At 8:51 p.m. MPD and MTSU Police responded to a shooting on campus and found two people suffering from gunshot wounds near the Murphy Center. 1 dead 1 injured. READ MORE BELOW {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3aBQlBb63P"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/3aBQlBb63P}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Murfreesboro TN Police Department (@MboroPoliceDept) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MboroPoliceDept/status/1527141839471517698?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 19, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 The 24 February Russian invasion of Ukraine was, Finland\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2  added, revelatory:\par \par \uc2 The masks have fallen and we see only the cold faces of war.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s war in Ukraine has changed Europe\u8217\'20\'19s and our security environment.\par \par \uc2 Finland takes the step of Nato membership in order to strengthen not only its own security, but to also to widen transatlantic security. This is not a threat to anybody.\par \par \uc2 Sweden\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  also took a swipe at Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion, which she said was \u8220\'20\'1Ca watershed moment\u8221\'20\'1D for her nation:\par \par \uc2 The situation in Ukraine reminds us of the darkest days in European history.\par \par \uc2 [But] over these past months we have shown transatlantic unity and strength at its best and have responded forcefully to Russia\u8217\'20\'19s aggression by providing unprecedent support for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 She acknowledged that her country\u8217\'20\'19s decision to seek \uc2 Nato\uc2  membership was a marked diversion from its centuries-long stance of neutrality:\par \par \uc2 After 200 years of military non-alignment, Sweden has chosen a new path.\par \par \uc2 Russia\u8217\'20\'19s full-scale aggression against a sovereign and democratic neighbor was a watershed moment for Sweden.\par \par \uc2 The security of the Swedish people will be best protected within the Nato alliance.\par \par \uc2 With Sweden and Finland as members \uc2 Nato\uc2  will also be stronger. We are champions of freedom, democracy and human rights.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Finland\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2  said his country had decided to seek \uc2 Nato\uc2  membership after a \u8220\'20\'1Crapid but very thorough process\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 The decision was made with an overwhelming parliamentary majority and huge, strong popular support.\par \par \uc2 Finland will be a strong Nato ally. We take our security very seriously. The Finnish armed forces are one of the strongest in Europe.\par \par \uc2 Turkey has said it will reject the Finnish and Swedish applications {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/18/erdogan-doubles-down-on-threat-to-veto-nordic-nato-membership"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 because of their supposed support for the Kurdish Workers\u8217\'20\'19 party}}}\uc2  (PKK), which the country regards as a terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 Addressing Turkey directly, Niinist\u246\'00\'F6 said:\par \par \uc2 Finland has always had broad and good bilateral relations with Turkey. As Nato allies we will commit to Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s security, just as Turkey will commit to our security.\par \par \uc2 We take terrorism seriously. We condemn terrorism in all its forms and we are actively engaged in combatting it.\par \par \uc2 We are open to discussing all the concerns Turkey may have concerning our membership in an open and productive manner.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said that the Russian invasion eliminated any doubt he said had arisen in recent decades over the purpose of the mutual defense alliance.\par \par \uc2 Was Nato still relevant? Was it still effective? Is it still needed in the 21st century world? Today there is no question. \uc2 Nato\uc2  is relevant. It is effective and is more needed now than ever.\par \par \uc2 The indispensable alliance of decades past is still the indispensable alliance for the world we face today. And I would argue tomorrow as well. And the decision of Sweden and Finland, the one they have made, is testament to that commitment.\par \par \uc2 This is about the future. It\u8217\'20\'19s about a revived Nato that has the tools and resources, the clarity and conviction, to defend our shared values and lead the world.\par \par \uc2 He said the two Nordic nations possessed all the attributes needed to make them valuable members of an alliance that would grow to 32 member states, if they are admitted:\par \par \uc2 Sweden and Finland have strong democratic institutions, strong militaries and strong and transparent economies, and a strong moral sense of what is right.\par \par \uc2 They meet every Nato requirement and then some. Having two new Nato members in the high north will enhance the security of our alliance and deepen our security cooperation across the board.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.00pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden: Sweden and Finland 'make Nato stronger'\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says he\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cproud\u8221\'20\'1D to support the admission of Sweden and Finland to the Nato defense alliance.\par \par \uc2 The president has been speaking at the White House in a trilateral press conference with Sweden\u8217\'20\'19s prime minister \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  and Finland\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2 , saying it\u8217\'20\'19s a \u8220\'20\'1Cmomentous day\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a very, very great day. I\u8217\'20\'19m proud to welcome and offer the strong support of the United States for the applications of two great democracies, and two close, highly capable partners, to join the strongest, most powerful defensive alliance in the history of the world.\par \par \uc2 Two proud independent countries exercising their sovereign right all states possess to decide their own security.\par \par \uc2 Biden said he and his fellow leaders had \u8220\'20\'1Ca good discussion\u8221\'20\'1D about Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine and the \u8220\'20\'1Cstrengthening of transatlantic security\u8221\'20\'1D but said talks with the two nations predated Russia\u8217\'20\'19s aggression, which he said had \u8220\'20\'1Cruptured\u8221\'20\'1D Europe\u8217\'20\'19s security.\par \par \uc2 We have consulted closely at every stage as Sweden and Finland made their determination and today I\u8217\'20\'19m proud to assure them that they have the full, total complete backing of the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 He said his administration was submitting reports on Nato accession to congress so the Seante can \u8220\'20\'1Cefficiently and quickly move on advising and consenting to the treaty\u8221\'20\'1D:\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is simple. Finland and Sweden make Nato stronger... a strong, united \uc2 Nato\uc2  is the foundation of America\u8217\'20\'19s security.\par \par \uc2 New members joining \uc2 Nato\uc2  is not a threat to any nation. It never has been. \uc2 Nato\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s purpose is to defend against aggression.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 The White House will lay out in further detail later today its plans to address the nationwide baby formula shortage, a day after \uc2 Joe Biden{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/18/baby-formula-shortage-biden-defense-production-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invoked the defense production act}}}\uc2  to force manufacturers to ramp up supply.\par \par \uc2 In another move on Wednesday, the House approved a $28m emergency funding bill to address the crisis, but its fate appears uncertain in a divided Senate.\par \par \uc2 The White House call to reporters around lunchtime is expected to address planned next steps in the Biden administration\u8217\'20\'19s battle to tackle the shortage, which is weighing on the president\u8217\'20\'19s approval ratings along with raging inflation and soaring gas prices.\par \par \uc2 My colleague Eric Berger has this helpful explainer about what\u8217\'20\'19s caused the baby formula shortage and what parents can do about it:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/18/baby-formula-shortage-why-is-there-none-what-to-do-causes-explained"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why is there a baby formula shortage in the US, and what can parents do?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Senate set to approve $40bn Ukraine aid package today\par \par \uc2 A $40bn package of military, economic and humanitarian aid for Ukraine and its allies was on the cusp of Senate passage Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The vote later today would give final congressional approval to the package, three weeks after President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  requested a smaller $33bn version.\par \par \uc2 The House approved it last week after beefing up defense and humanitarian expenditures, and Senate passage was not in doubt, though a small group of Republicans was expected to vote no, the Associated Press reports.\par \par \uc2 It includes the Kentucky senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , who used procedural tactics to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/12/us-ukraine-aid-rand-paul-senate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thwart quick passage of the bill}}}\uc2  last week, and who was branded \u8220\'20\'1Crepugnant\u8221\'20\'1D by Democratic senate majority leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 enltrSenate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  hits at Sen. \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  (R-KY) for delaying $40 billion Ukraine aid bill:\par \par \uc2 "It is repugnant... For Senator Paul to delay Ukraine funding for purely political motives is to only strengthen Putin's hand." {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/R8y5jCsfzz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/R8y5jCsfzz}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 The Recount (@therecount) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/therecount/status/1526948852087013377?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 18, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Notably, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Republican senate minority leader who visited Kyiv last weekend, and who has also urged swift approval of the package, did not leap to his colleague\u8217\'20\'19s defense.\par \par \uc2 Once passed, Biden will swiftly sign the measure, which the AP says brings to $54bn US spending on the conflict, in excess of the amount spent on all its foreign and military aid in 2019, according to the nonpartisan \uc2 Congressional Research Service\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll hear more later from \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and Democratic lawmakers about efforts to combat hate speech and the growth of \u8220\'20\'1Chomegrown violent extremism,\u8221\'20\'1D amplified by Saturday\u8217\'20\'19s racist massacre of 10 people by an alleged white supremacist gunman at a grocery store in Buffalo, New York.\par \par \uc2 The House {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/19/us-house-domestic-terrorism-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed the domestic terrorism bill late}}}\uc2  on Wednesday, which seeks to bolster federal resources to tackle extremist violence in the US and create new offenses specifically in that category.\par \par \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 . Photograph: Andrew Harnik/AP\par \par \uc2 But only one House Republican, Illinois congressman \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2 , voted in favor, and the bill is unlikely to find much Republican support in the equally divided Senate, where it would need 60 votes to pass. The party has routinely rejected any kind of measure involving gun restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi will host a press conference at lunchtime with other congressional leaders to discuss the Buffalo shooting and its aftermath.\par \par \uc2 Some states are not waiting for action in Washington DC. New York\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/18/buffalo-shooting-new-york-hochul-domestic-terrorism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unveiled}}}\uc2  what she called a \u8220\'20\'1Ccomprehensive plan to combat domestic terrorism and prevent gun violence\u8221\'20\'1D for the state.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the alleged Buffalo killer, Payton Gendron, 18, who is white, appeared briefly in court Thursday after a grand jury indicted him yesterday on a first-degree murder charge.\par \par \uc2 Gendron, wearing orange clothing and mask, was silent throughout the short proceeding and was sent back to jail. Somebody shouted \u8220\'20\'1CPayton you\u8217\'20\'19re a coward!\u8221\'20\'1D as he was led out, the Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/19/us-house-domestic-terrorism-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US House passes domestic terrorism bill in response to Buffalo shooting}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 Turkey says it rejects Nordic nations' Nato application\par \par \uc2 Will Nato get its two new members, Sweden and Finland? We\u8217\'20\'19ll get an update shortly from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Nordic nations\u8217\'20\'19 respective leaders, Prime Minister \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  and President \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2 , who\u8217\'20\'19ve been meeting Thursday morning at the White House.\par \par \uc2 The US is all in on expanding the western mutual defense alliance, Biden tweeting yesterday that he \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1526997841444585477"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strongly supported}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D the applications following Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 enltrI strongly support the historic applications from Finland and Sweden for membership in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . I look forward to welcoming President \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2  and Prime Minister \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  to Washington tomorrow so that we can further discuss their applications and European security.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 President Biden (@POTUS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1526997841444585477?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 18, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 But Turkey has this morning thrown a wrench into the works, with reports emerging that it\u8217\'20\'19s told allies it rejects Sweden and Finland\u8217\'20\'19s applications.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have told allies that we will say no,\u8221\'20\'1D Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s president \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  told students, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/turkey-has-told-allies-its-no-sweden-finlands-nato-bid-erdogan-2022-05-19/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according to Reuters}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s uncertain to what degree Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s stance could delay or even derail the process. We\u8217\'20\'19re hoping for more clarity when Biden and his fellow leaders speak in short order in the Rose Garden.\par \par \uc2 Turkey appears to be the only holdout. None of the other 30 Nato members have raised objections, publicly at least, with some appearing extremely enthusiastic. The Baltic state of Estonia, long under Moscow\u8217\'20\'19s shadow, can\u8217\'20\'19t seem to wait:\par \par \uc2 enltrNow it\u8217\'20\'19s official: Estonian Government moved fast and decided today to approve requests by Sweden and Finland to join {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NATO?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NATO}}}\uc2 . Important formal step to open up the doors of \uc2 NATO\uc2  by our side. Welcome, dear neighbours {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MarinSanna?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @marinsanna}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SwedishPM?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @SwedishPM}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/WeAreNATO?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #WeAreNATO}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nCdM5SALla"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nCdM5SALla}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Kaja Kallas\uc2  (@kajakallas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/kajakallas/status/1527226967107555328?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May 19, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, my colleague Patrick Wintour has taken this in-depth look at why Turkey is objecting, and the likely consequences of the move he says \u8220\'20\'1Ccould tangle {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/nato"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nato}}}\uc2  up in knots for months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/18/erdogan-doubles-down-on-threat-to-veto-nordic-nato-membership"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Why has Erdogan doubled down on threat to veto Nordic Nato bids?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to the Thursday edition of our US politics blog!\par \par \uc2 Nato\uc2  is on the cusp of adding two new members to the mutual defense alliance and Prime Minister Magdalena Andersso n of Sweden and President \uc2 Sauli Niinist\u246\'00\'F6\uc2  of Finland are meeting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  at the White House as we speak to discuss the security of Europe.\par \par \uc2 The two Nordic nations say they\u8217\'20\'19ve been forced into the decision by Russia \u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine, and Biden is backing the move, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/POTUS/status/1526997841444585477"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeting yesterday}}}\uc2  that: \u8220\'20\'1CI strongly support the historic application.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Turkey is saying it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t approve, and could yet derail or delay the process. We\u8217\'20\'19ll hear from the three leaders in the Rose Garden shortly, before Biden hops on Air Force One to Anchorage, Alaska, en route to Seoul and meetings in the coming days with leaders of South Korea and Japan.\par \par \uc2 The Ukraine conflict, North Korea\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear ambitions and China\u8217\'20\'19s growing influence, in the region will be on the president\u8217\'20\'19s agenda there. You can follow today\u8217\'20\'19s developments in Russia\u8217\'20\'19s war in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/may/19/russia-ukraine-war-latest-russian-use-of-laser-weapons-shows-invasion-a-failure-says-zelenskiy-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 our live blog here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s plenty going on in the US as Biden takes his leave:\par \par \uc2 * Lawmakers are grappling with the aftermath of the weekend\u8217\'20\'19s racist massacre at a Buffalo, New York, grocery store. The House yesterday passed a domestic terrorism bill, and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and other Democratic politicians will speak at lunchtime. The alleged killer, meanwhile, is in court this morning.\par \par \uc2 * The Biden administration has taken steps to address the nationwide baby formula shortage, invoking the 1950s-era defense production act to force manufacturers to ramp up supply. The House also passed a $28m emergency funding bill last night to address the crisis.\par \par \uc2 * Primary vote counting continues in Pennsylvania where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s approved candidate and TV doctor Mehmet Oz is locked in a tight race with David McCormick for the Republican senate nomination.\par \par \uc2 * We\u8217\'20\'19ll hear from the White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , and the national security adviser, Jake Sullivan, as they \u8220\'20\'1Cgaggle\u8221\'20\'1D with reporters on the flight to Alaska.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220519ei5j001xu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
