\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Indigenous and Alaska Native women could face escalated violence if Roe is repealed\b0\par\par\uc2 Hallie Golden \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par 19:38 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 32\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 They are also two to three times more likely to die as a result of pregnancy than white women, according to the CDC\par \par \uc2 The repeal of federally protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would result in an increase in violence experienced by Indigenous women, girls and all those who birth, predicted the director of one of the leading research institutes on Indigenous and Alaska Native people across the US .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe only option we have right now if this was to be overturned, is to provide the limited resources and support, but it will be limited, especially initially. As a direct result our people are going to suffer,\u8221\'20\'1D Abigail Echo-Hawk, director of the Urban Indian Health Institute, told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 Her remarks followed a leaked supreme court draft opinion authored by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  that showed the country\u8217\'20\'19s highest court ready to overturn Roe v Wade, the nearly 50-year-old ruling protecting the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until a fetus is viable outside the womb. Should that decision become official, 13 states with trigger laws would immediately in effect outlaw abortions. Many other Republican-controlled states are expected to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 A 2016 \uc2 National Institute of Justice{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncai.org/policy-research-center/research-data/prc-publications/TraffickingBrief.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  found that more than one in three Indigenous and Alaska Native women had {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ojp.gov/pdffiles1/nij/249822.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 experienced violence}}}\uc2  in the past year, including sexual violence and violence by an intimate partner. They were 1.7 times more likely than white women to be the victims of violence. They are also at an especially high risk for being trafficked, although comprehensive data is limited.\par \par \uc2 The high rates of these crimes \u8211\'20\'13 many of which are never reported, probably due to a mistrust in law enforcement \u8211\'20\'13 compounded with not being able to access legal abortions, can leave Indigenous people at an increased risk, explained Echo-Hawk.\par \par \uc2 For those in an abusive relationship, not having access to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could force the victim to stay in a bad situation and lead to more violence, she said. Those being trafficked, some of whom are not allowed to use birth control, may face additional violence from their perpetrators should they become pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Indigenous and Alaska Native women along with Black women are also two to three times {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/media/releases/2019/p0905-racial-ethnic-disparities-pregnancy-deaths.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more likely to die}}}\uc2  as a result of their pregnancy than white women, according to the \uc2 US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC)\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Echo-Hawk said having to access unsafe abortions or experiencing the mental anguish of being forced to go through a pregnancy could increase these numbers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat mental health and the mental violence which we\u8217\'20\'19re experiencing, that has a direct impact on a person\u8217\'20\'19s body when they are going through pregnancy, and it can directly result in pre-term birth and maternal mortality as a direct result,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo, we\u8217\'20\'19re actually battling mental violence that continues to be perpetuated against us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A lack of reproductive autonomy\par \par \uc2 For decades Indigenous people were subjected to forced sterilization in the US, with the practice peaking in the mid-20th century. Repealing Roe v Wade would shift the scales of their reproductive autonomy \u8211\'20\'13 or lack thereof \u8211\'20\'13 to a new and dire extreme, explained Kerri Colfer, senior Native affairs adviser for the National Indigenous Women\u8217\'20\'19s Resource Center.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNative people are still dealing with the trauma of colonization, historic violence against Native people, forced sterilizations, Indian child removal and boarding schools \u8230\'20\'26 being forced to carry pregnancy is a really traumatic thing, whether that is a pregnancy that resulted from assault or not. And now we\u8217\'20\'19ll have another generation with that trauma to carry on,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Indian Health Service{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ihs.gov/ihm/sgm/1996/sgm-9601/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 provides health }}}\uc2  care to about 2.6 million Indigenous and Alaska Natives. But the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4167108/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agency}}}\uc2 , which is within the US Department of Health and Human Services, restricts abortions to those in which a mother\u8217\'20\'19s life is at risk or in cases of rape or incest that have been reported within 60 days.\par \par \uc2 Echo-Hawk, who was a co-author of an amicus brief submitted to the supreme court on this issue last year, said as part of that effort, her organization looked at 33 IHS-funded facilities. They found that between 2002 and 2021, only seven Indigenous women were documented as visiting an IHS facility for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Instead, some Indigenous people seek abortions outside these facilities, she explained. Repealing Roe v Wade would mean that Indigenous people, some of whom live in extremely rural and poverty-stricken areas, might have to travel much further, to facilities outside their state.\par \par \uc2 She asked: \u8220\'20\'1CHow are we going to afford that travel?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since 2014, Indigenous Women Rising, an Indigenous-led and -centered reproductive justice organization, has helped hundreds through its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund by contributing funding for the procedure, as well as for gas, hotels and childcare.\par \par \uc2 Rachael Lorenzo, its executive director, said if Roe v Wade is overturned, they will need to keep close track of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in the states they serve the most, including North Dakota, South Dakota, Arizona and Oklahoma, and willprobably need more funds. The organization has several fundraising events scheduled, including a barbecue cookout for rural communities in southern New Mexico and a two-stepping fundraiser.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are scared, and they\u8217\'20\'19re angry, and they\u8217\'20\'19re frustrated. And so am I,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lorenzo, a descendant of Mescalero Apache, Laguna Pueblo and Xicana. \u8220\'20\'1CBut we also deserve joy. And if there\u8217\'20\'19s any way we can merge the two, by taking action and not letting ourselves get consumed with how scary this is \u8230\'20\'26 that\u8217\'20\'19s really where our energy is going to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220519ei5j001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
