\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 : El Salvador\u8217\'20\'19s jailed women offer US glimpse of post-Roe future\b0\par\par\uc2 Nina Lakhani \par \uc2 1,066 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par 17:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 32\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Don\u8217\'20\'19t let our reality become your reality,\u8217\'20\'19 campaigners warn after woman is freed after decade behind bars for medical emergency ruled attempted murder\par \par \uc2 A 33-year-old woman in El Salvador who suffered a medical emergency while pregnant has been freed after serving a decade in jail for attempted murder, the victim of a draconian \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban being replicated in the US.\par \par \uc2 The woman, named only as Jacqueline, sought medical help for an obstetric complication in 2011, and even though the baby survived, she was arrested on suspicion of attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . She was separated from her newborn daughter and eight-year-old son, and sentenced to 15 years for attempted murder.\par \par \uc2 Jacqueline, who was released on Wednesday, is the 65th woman to be freed after having been wrongly jailed on murder charges following a miscarriage or other obstetric emergency since the total ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  came into force in 1998.\par \par \uc2 The Salvadorian anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law, which was subsequently written into the constitution, has led to at least 182 women who suffered an obstetric emergency being prosecuted for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or aggravated homicide.\par \par \uc2 Poor, young women from rural areas with limited access to healthcare have been disproportionately persecuted, with most reported to the police by hospital workers. In many cases, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2015/dec/17/el-salvador-anti-abortion-law-premature-birth-miscarriage-attempted-murder"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prosecutors and judges have argued that the woman\u8217\'20\'19s failure to save the pregnancy amounted to murder.}}}\par \par \uc2 Salvadorian lawyers and activists have worked to free the convicted women, pursuing pardons, sentence reduction and access to education and work programs that can lead to an early release. Ten women have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/feb/09/el-salvador-woman-freed-abortion-law-10-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 freed}}}\uc2  since last December, leaving three still in prison.\par \par \uc2 Each case has taken a sustained campaign of protests, public health efforts and media pressure, according to Morena Herrera, a reproductive rights campaigner who recalls the moment when lawmakers, spurred on by the religious right, voted to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without exception in April 1997.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI warned them that this law would end the presumption of innocence for pregnant women, which is exactly what happened. It\u8217\'20\'19s this same risk that the US is now facing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Herrera.\par \par \uc2 A leaked supreme court decision suggests that Roe v Wade \u8211\'20\'13 the 1973 ruling that guarantees the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will soon be overturned}}}\uc2 , resulting in about half of US states banning or severely restricting access to safe, legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Texas and Oklahoma have already approved severe restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Herrera added: \u8220\'20\'1CDon\u8217\'20\'19t let our reality become your reality. If the law changes, doctors\u8217\'20\'19 hands will be tied and women will die. Do everything you can to stop this because the consequences of losing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are grave.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In El Salvador, a country of 6.5 million people, the consequences include death for women with ectopic pregnancies who have been denied timely medical care. Many others have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.safeabortionwomensright.org/news/el-salvador-beatriz-who-brought-el-salvadors-abortion-ban-to-the-world-stage-in-2013-has-died-following-a-motorcycle-accident/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  suffered complications }}}\uc2  to their physical and mental health. Suicide rates among young pregnant women have risen.\par \par \uc2 Last week advocates were dismayed when Esme, a 28-year-old from a poor rural community, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/10/el-salvador-woman-sentenced-prison-after-miscarriage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was sentenced to 30 years}}}\uc2  for aggravated homicide after suffering a miscarriage \u8211\'20\'13 the first woman convicted for an obstetric complication in seven years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are in shock, maybe we didn\u8217\'20\'19t put enough pressure. You can never take your foot off the pedal,\u8221\'20\'1D said Paula \u193\'00\'C1vila-Guill\u233\'00\'E9n, a human rights lawyer and executive director of the New York-based Women\u8217\'20\'19s Equality Centre. Esme spent two years in jail on remand, but is now at home while lawyers appeal the verdict.\par \par \uc2 The battle to free the three other women continues.\par \par \uc2 Mar\u237\'00\'EDa del Rosario was 23 years old when she started haemorrhaging at home alone. Instead of receiving medical help, she was detained by police and sentenced to 30 years for aggravated homicide (later reduced to 20 years). Mar\u237\'00\'EDa del Rosario\u8217\'20\'19s pregnancy was the result of rape; her alleged aggressor was acquitted. She has spent a decade in prison.\par \par \uc2 Liliana was a 21-year-old mother expecting her second baby in 2016 when she was sentenced to 30 years in jail for homicide after suffering a medical emergency. The sentence was later halved, but she has already spent seven years separated from her daughter.\par \par \uc2 The case of Bertha Arana is especially egregious. Arana, 28, was convicted of attempted murder in 2012 after suffering a medical emergency, even though her daughter survived. But unlike Jacqueline, every attempt to secure a reduction in her 30-year sentence has been blocked because Arana was born just over the border in Guatemala and does not have Salvadorian identity papers. She has not seen her daughter since she gave birth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBertha\u8217\'20\'19s case is not just unjust, it\u8217\'20\'19s cruel,\u8221\'20\'1D said Herrera. The \uc2 UN\uc2  has condemned such cases as arbitrary detentions.\par \par \uc2 As the US contemplates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/14/oklahoma-abortion-post-roe-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the reality of a post-Roe world}}}\uc2 , the Salvadorian experience demonstrates the long-term impact on women who are criminalised.\par \par \uc2 Cristina Quintanilla was 18 and excited about motherhood when she suffered a late miscarriage in 2005. She woke up handcuffed to a hospital bed and was given 30 years for aggravated murder, though she was released after serving four after a sentence review.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.com/news/magazine-24532694"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vilified as a \u8220\'20\'1Cbaby killer\u8221\'20\'1D in El Salvador,}}}\uc2  Quintanilla was unable to find work and in 2014 made it to the US, where she sought asylum. She\u8217\'20\'19s been in legal limbo ever since, waiting for an immigration judge to rule on the case, but denied a work visa because of her criminal record.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe injustice continues \u8211\'20\'13 I can\u8217\'20\'19t escape it. I\u8217\'20\'19m so tired of fighting to clear my name, to be given an opportunity to live my life,\u8221\'20\'1D said Quintanilla, 36. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very unfortunate what\u8217\'20\'19s happening here. Americans face a very uncertain future \u8211\'20\'13 it will be chaos.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The US backslide comes amid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/may/05/latin-america-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a wave of legal and political reproductive rights victories across Latin America}}}\uc2  improving access to safe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in countries such as Mexico, Colombia, Chile and Argentina.\par \par \uc2 \u193\'00\'C1vila-Guill\u233\'00\'E9n, who works with lawyers and activists across Latin America, said they had learned lessons from the erosion of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe realised that human rights standards and legal strategy would never be enough \u8211\'20\'13 we also needed mass mobilizations, activism, political work and a communication strategy, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what the US will need to do to win hearts and minds and get our rights back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220519ei5j001bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
