\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 Who will be prosecuted for  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  if fetuses are recognized as people?\b0\par\par\uc2 Noa Yachot \par \uc2 1,397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2022\par 09:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The expected reversal of Roe v Wade will lead to more women being charged over pregnancy outcomes\par \par \uc2 As America prepares for a future in which the federal protection of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is removed as the supreme court appears set to overturn the Roe v Wade ruling, experts are warning that it could pave the way for increased prosecutions of those who seek abortions.\par \par \uc2 Last week a Louisiana bill classifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as murder was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/louisiana-lawmakers-withdraw-bill-declaring-abortion-homicide-2022-05-13/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 withdrawn}}}\uc2  after causing a firestorm of controversy, mainly for seeking to subject people seeking to end their own pregnancies to criminal prosecution. For years, much of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has insisted that the criminalization of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should target providers but exempt pregnant people.\par \par \uc2 But that view stands in tension with the trajectory of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, which has successfully labored to enshrine the notion of \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal personhood\u8221\'20\'1D in law and rhetoric, leading to the growing criminalization of pregnancy outcomes in recent years.\par \par \uc2 The idea that human life begins at conception and must be protected by the state cannot restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  so long as the protections of Roe v Wade stand. But the same idea has been planted in dozens of state criminal codes that grant zygotes, embryos and fetuses legal status as victims in other contexts.\par \par \uc2 Even with Roe in place, the notion that fetuses have status separate from the people who carry them has helped fuel the criminalization of at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationaladvocatesforpregnantwomen.org/adora-perez-case-dismissed/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1,700 people}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 probably a dramatic undercount, advocates say \u8211\'20\'13 in connection with their pregnancies, for crimes like homicide and delivering drugs to a minor.\par \par \uc2 It has also paved the way for a wider dragnet should the supreme court overturn Roe, as the draft opinion leaked earlier this month signaled will happen in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 Most such laws exempt the pregnant person from criminal charges. But that didn\u8217\'20\'19t stop the arrest of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/09/texas-woman-26-charged-murder-self-induced-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lizelle Herrera}}}\uc2 , a Texas woman recently charged with murder for \u8220\'20\'1Cintentionally and knowingly causing the death of an individual by self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D. The charges were ultimately dropped, with the district attorney admitting they had no basis in law. Self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal in the vast majority of states. (South Carolina, Oklahoma and Nevada are exceptions.)\par \par \uc2 Similar incidents could become more common without Roe, if 26 states ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as expected. \u8220\'20\'1CWe could see prosecutors emboldened to step beyond what the law permits,\u8221\'20\'1D says Farah Diaz-Tello, senior counsel and legal director of If/When/How, a reproductive justice group.\par \par \uc2 Diaz-Tello\u8217\'20\'19s organization operates a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reprolegalhelpline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal helpline}}}\uc2  to help people understand {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reprolegalhelpline.org/sma-know-your-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their rights}}}\uc2  if they seek to manage their own abortions. She says the helpline has seen a dramatic uptick in calls since the draft decision overturning Roe was leaked.\par \par \uc2 How \u8216\'20\'18fetal personhood\u8217\'20\'19 took hold\par \par \uc2 Roe says pregnant women have the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that fetuses are not people entitled to the constitution\u8217\'20\'19s protections. But that finding in 1973 came with a stipulation. \u8220\'20\'1C??If this suggestion of personhood is established,\u8221\'20\'1D Justice Harry A Blackmun wrote in the decision, the \u8220\'20\'1Ccase, of course, collapses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Over the last half-century anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists saw Blackmun\u8217\'20\'19s point as an invitation. Within a week, Representative Lawrence Hogan of Maryland introduced the human life amendment to overturn Roe and declare human life to begin at conception. It has repeatedly been introduced, and failed, since.\par \par \uc2 With a national ban out of reach, an incremental, state-based approach took hold to normalize personhood. In 1989, the supreme court upheld a Missouri law that stated in its preamble that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe life of each human being begins at conception\u8221\'20\'1D, and \u8220\'20\'1Cunborn children have protectable interests in life, health, and wellbeing\u8221\'20\'1D. While that language came into direct conflict with Roe, the court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/492/490/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled}}}\uc2  the preamble could stand because it didn\u8217\'20\'19t actively restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a scathing dissent, Blackmun gave an ominous warning: ?For today, the women of this nation still retain the liberty to control their destinies. But the signs are evident and very ominous, and a chill wind blows.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The 1989 ruling encouraged \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents to seed fetal personhood as widely as possible, helping fuel the spread of criminal laws recognizing fetuses as potential victims. This idea has been particularly toxic in combination with the war on drugs that exploded in the 1980s, disproportionately targeting women of color.\par \par \uc2 In her book Policing the Womb: Invisible Women and the Criminalization of Motherhood, the legal scholar Michele Goodwin calls Black women the \u8220\'20\'1Ccanaries in the coal mine\u8221\'20\'1D for the government\u8217\'20\'19s policing of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://read.dukeupress.edu/jhppl/article/38/2/299/13533/Arrests-of-and-Forced-Interventions-on-Pregnant"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  by the National Advocates for Pregnant Women (NAPW) found that 52% of cases of women targeted for pregnancy outcomes between 1973 and 2005 were Black, despite Black women making up about 14% of people of reproductive age. In 2003, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/full/10.1002/hast.791"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Goodwin has written}}}\uc2 , Regina McKnight, a Black woman from South Carolina, became the first woman convicted for \u8220\'20\'1Chomicide by child abuse\u8221\'20\'1D for a stillbirth due to drug use. Her case \u8220\'20\'1Cinspired similar prosecutions of other poor black women and then of other women\u8221\'20\'1D. McKnight served seven years before her sentence was overturned.\par \par \uc2 According to the National \uc2 Association of Criminal Defense Lawyers\uc2 , there are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nacdl.org/getattachment/ce0899a0-3588-42d0-b351-23b9790f3bb8/abortion-in-america-how-legislative-overreach-is-turning-reproductive-rights-into-criminal-wrongs.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 thousands of crimes}}}\uc2  in the federal criminal code that may be used against pregnant people when Roe falls.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe really can\u8217\'20\'19t look to pre-1973 for any sort of help in understanding what we\u8217\'20\'19re facing, says Dana Sussman, acting executive director of NAPW. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in a completely different universe when it comes to our willingness to criminalize people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 To date, 39 states have \u8220\'20\'1Cfetal homicide\u8221\'20\'1D laws. Fetal personhood also lives in the Republican party platform and in a number of state laws that have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aclu.org/legal-document/order-summary-judgement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blocked}}}\uc2  by federal courts thanks to the protections that the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s draft opinion undoes. They have been the subject of ballot initiatives in Mississippi, North Dakota and Colorado \u8211\'20\'13 where they failed by a wide margin, in part because of their implications for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/12/ivf-treatment-us-anti-abortion-laws-bills"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in-vitro fertilization}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 along with Alabama, which approved a fetal personhood amendment to its constitution in 2018.\par \par \uc2 As with Lizelle Hererra, advocates have often been able to rally public outcry in support of people arrested for actions related to their pregnancies. But not every case attracts public attention. And dismissed cases do not erase the trauma and stigma of criminalization and incarceration.\par \par \uc2 In 2013, for example, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2016/jul/22/purvi-patel-abortion-sentence-reduced"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Purvi Patel}}}\uc2  was charged with feticide and felony child neglect in Indiana for a self-induced \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and sentenced to 20 years. Her conviction was overturned, but not before she spent three years in prison. In Oklahoma, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/oct/16/policing-pregnancy-miscarriage-corporate-drug-pushers-go-free"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brittney Poolaw}}}\uc2  is currently serving a four-year sentence for manslaughter after admitting to hospital staff when suffering a miscarriage that she had used drugs.\par \par \uc2 The word \u8220\'20\'1Cchild\u8221\'20\'1D in several states \u8211\'20\'13 South Carolina, Alabama and Oklahoma \u8211\'20\'13 has been interpreted by prosecutors and courts to apply to fetuses throughout the criminal code. \u8220\'20\'1CState prosecutors throughout those states can use any law that was intended to apply to the abuse or harm of children to fertilized eggs, embryos and fetuses,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sussman.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn those states, we\u8217\'20\'19ve already lost that argument. And we anticipate we will lose more of them without Roe and Casey.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The chaos is by design\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Diaz-Tello cautions against defeatism. If pregnant people remain exempt from criminalization under existing laws and future bans, legal advocates can win challenges to prosecutions that arise.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs long as the law is not permitting people to be criminally prosecuted, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to be here to hold them to it,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. Even if Roe is no longer the law of the land, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe still have the power to ensure that laws are not passed that criminalize people for seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Whether these exemptions hold remains to be seen. But the threat of criminalization is not about to recede given a patchwork of extreme restrictions and an emboldened anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement allying with prosecutors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe chaos and the confusion of all this is by design,\u8221\'20\'1D says Brigitte Amiri, deputy director of the reproductive freedom project at \uc2 the American Civil Liberties Union\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhen people are confused and scared, it makes it less likely that they will seek access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and it increases the stigma related to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And all of this falls most heavily on people who are in the most marginalized communities.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220518ei5i0015t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
