\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 Facebook\uc2  fails to stop Spanish language anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  disinformation, study says\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano in Los Angeles \par \uc2 668 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 May 2022\par 04:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A group examined nine profiles reaching more than 2.79 million people peddling inaccurate information without consequence\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 Facebook\uc2  pages with millions of followers are spreading disinformation about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Spanish speakers with little to no intervention from the social media platform, according to a new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prochoiceamerica.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Translating-Abortion-Disinformation-The-Spanish-Language-Anti-Choice-Landscape.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  from Naral Pro-Choice America.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights group examined nine \uc2 Facebook\uc2  pages that reach more than 2.79 million people for the first-of-its-kind report, part of its efforts to show how disinformation is disseminated by opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Researchers found that several of the pages \u8220\'20\'1Crepeatedly spread medically inaccurate information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without any action from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, highlighting what experts have described as a crisis of Spanish-language misinformation that\u8217\'20\'19s slipping through the cracks.\par \par \uc2 The report comes as reproductive rights are in peril in the US with the supreme court expected to overturn the landmark Roe v Wade ruling, which guaranteed the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt has never been more critical for people to have access to medically accurate information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 no matter the language they speak. What\u8217\'20\'19s clear here is that anti-choice extremists are capitalizing on social media companies\u8217\'20\'19 failure to moderate Spanish-language disinformation,\u8221\'20\'1D Mini Timmaraju, the Naral Pro-Choice America president, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Research from the group found that articles in Spanish focused on US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy that received the most engagement on social media \u8220\'20\'1Coverwhelmingly lean anti-choice\u8221\'20\'1D and come from outlets with religious affiliations.\par \par \uc2 Many of the Spanish-language \uc2 Facebook\uc2  pages examined by Naral researchers spread disinformation about the safety of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , claiming that it leads to increased risk of breast cancer, anxiety, infertility, depression and suicide. Some posts have been viewed thousands of times without intervention from \uc2 Facebook\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The content spreads the same disinformation about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as English-language anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, which also push medically inaccurate information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and demonizes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, the report says.\par \par \uc2 Catholicism plays a key role in much of the Spanish-language content, which suggests that support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care conflicts with religious identity. The Spanish-speaking anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement relies on stereotypes, said Gabriela Rico, the lead researcher for the report, such as that Latinx people widely oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are all Catholic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not only a false, racist assumption, but a way for the anti-choice movement to erase the majority of Latinx people who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://navigatorresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Navigator-Abortion-Rights-Update-05.12.2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 support reproductive freedom}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 often because of, not in spite of, their faith,\u8221\'20\'1D Rico, a Naral research manger, said.\par \par \uc2 A recent Navigator Research poll found that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://navigatorresearch.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/Navigator-Abortion-Rights-Update-05.12.2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 65% of Hispanic Americans}}}\uc2  support Congress passing a law to guarantee the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US.\par \par \uc2 The spread of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  disinformation is part of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s broader failure to moderate Spanish-language content, experts say. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/mar/03/facebook-spanish-language-misinformation-covid-19-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study last year}}}\uc2  found that only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://secure.avaaz.org/campaign/en/facebook_coronavirus_misinformation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 30% of misinformation in Spanish}}}\uc2  is flagged with warning labels, compared to 70% in English. Lawmakers have called on the company to do more to address what they deemed a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2021/mar/16/facebook-spanish-language-disinformation-congress"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disinformation crisis}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lujan.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2021/09/Facebook-Response-Lujan.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a response}}}\uc2  to lawmakers, \uc2 Facebook\uc2  focused on the resources the company has put into limiting misinformation, stating that it has \u8220\'20\'1C35,000 people working on these challenges\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Facebook\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment about Naral\u8217\'20\'19s findings.\par \par \uc2 Multiple social media platforms have failed to stop disinformation about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Spanish and English, but \uc2 Facebook\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s moderation decisions have been \u8220\'20\'1Cparticularly problematic\u8221\'20\'1D, the report said.\par \par \uc2 The failure to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  disinformation has \u8220\'20\'1Cdirect implications for the 72% of Latinx people in the United States who use its platform\u8221\'20\'1D, the report states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLatinx communities are targeted with anti-choice disinformation constantly \u8211\'20\'13 I have seen this not only in my work on this report, but in my own family and community,\u8221\'20\'1D Rico said. \u8220\'20\'1CSpanish-speaking people deserve to receive accurate information about reproductive freedom, not to be lied to and used as pawns in a political game.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220518ei5i000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
