\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Even in  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18safe havens\u8217\'20\'19 finding care can be challenging: \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s a system already strained\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh in Oakland \par \uc2 1,786 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par 17:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In California, the state with the strongest legal protections, funds, distance and lack of information can still present barriers\par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of the leaked supreme court document foretelling the end to federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections, California geared up to fight.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCalifornia will not stand idly by,\u8221\'20\'1D the state\u8217\'20\'19s governor and legislative leaders said in a joint statement. As in other liberal-leaning states, officials and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers vowed to ramp up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections and turn California into an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  safe haven.\par \par \uc2 Already, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2019/08/state-abortion-policy-landscape-hostile-supportive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 no other state}}}\uc2  does more to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. The right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in California is guaranteed in statute and by the sweeping personal privacy rights enshrined in the state constitution. California health insurance plans cover the cost of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for low-income residents, and the state has rejected waiting period requirements and most restrictions on the practice.\par \par \uc2 But even in the state with the best \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in the US, abortions have long been inaccessible for many \u8211\'20\'13 especially those living in rural, conservative areas. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/fact-sheet/state-facts-about-abortion-california#1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Forty per cent}}}\uc2  of California counties have no clinics providing abortions. Many people still have to travel far to get the appropriate care, or struggle to afford abortions, while language barriers and a lack of up-to-date information can make it difficult to find help.\par \par \uc2 Advocates say that the millions of dollars state leaders {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/12/abortion-california-gavin-newsom-funding-low-income"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 are promising}}}\uc2  for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and extended legal protections are a start. But even more may be needed to care for those living within the state, and the thousands coming from afar. \u8220\'20\'1CAs more and more people come in from out of state seeking abortions, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to put more pressure on a system that\u8217\'20\'19s already strained,\u8221\'20\'1D said Laura Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez, the executive director of California Latinas for Reproductive Justice.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, blue states seeking to become \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sanctuaries are facing similar challenges. Even as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/11/abortion-pro-choice-states-safe-havens-funding-legal-protection"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Democrat-controlled legislatures}}}\uc2  from coast to coast convey millions into \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds, advocates are unclear on just how much money and resources will be required by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and services that are already overextended. According to the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a reproductive rights group, about 43% of Massachusetts counties had no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics, and 13% of Massachusetts women lived in those counties. In Vermont, 64% counties had no clinics, and about 38% of Vermont women lived in those counties.\par \par \uc2 Few options in rural areas\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Lopez, who lives in Visalia in California\u8217\'20\'19s agricultural Central Valley, had to drive north over county lines to get a simple medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you drive into Visalia from other parts of California, it\u8217\'20\'19s like you\u8217\'20\'19re going back in time,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lopez, 36.\par \par \uc2 Visalia\uc2  has a small Planned Parenthood clinic, but it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/inside-fight-new-abortion-clinic-one-california-city-2022-03-15/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  effort by Planned Parenthood to expand}}}\uc2  recently inspired furor among conservative groups, and a public hearing on the proposal to build a clinic that would provide abortions was postponed several times this year over the controversy.\par \par \uc2 Lopez, who works at a domestic violence shelter, said she considers herself exceptionally lucky. Many of the clients she works with lack access to transportation or funding to travel for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis isn\u8217\'20\'19t a new problem,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jessica Pinckney, executive director of Access Reproductive Justice, an organisation that helps secure financing and organize travel, lodging and childcare for people seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 About a quarter of those seeking help from Access come from out-of-state, including from Texas, which enacted a near-total ban on abortions last year, Pinckney said. But the organisation has also helped many coming from states like Nevada \u8211\'20\'13 where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are protected, but clinics are sparse \u8211\'20\'13 and in remote regions within California.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have helped folks in rural parts of northern California travel four or five hours to the Bay Area to access abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D Pinckney said, adding that the few \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the rural regions are unable to care for patients with complicated abortions or those who are farther along in their pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There\u8217\'20\'19s still work to be done\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Although a shortage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics is felt most acutely in rural parts of California, people living in underserved parts of big cities can struggle to find care as well. Amelia Torres, 25, said she had to travel from the South Central neighbourhood of Los Angeles, where she lives, all the way across the city to Beverly Hills in order to get a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Torres, thinking she was about five weeks along when she found out she was pregnant, initially sought a medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a nearby clinic. But the medication didn\u8217\'20\'19t work, she said. When she went in for a follow-up appointment, it turned out she was about 18 weeks along and would need a surgical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which more than half of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the state aren\u8217\'20\'19t equipped to perform.\par \par \uc2 She didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a car, but her partner was ultimately able to take time off work to drive her to the appointment, and her family was able to take care of her two daughters while she was being seen. \u8220\'20\'1CI was lucky,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. Though some in her family tried to dissuade her from terminating her pregnancy, they ultimately supported her choice. \u8220\'20\'1CBut without that support, the money, the transportation,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, \u8220\'20\'1Ceven here in California getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be very difficult.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In addition to a lack of providers, language barriers and a lack of information can make it impossible for many to find \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, or the resources to help fund such care. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s always been an enormous stigma around seeking information about abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ushma Upadhyay, a public health researcher at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Advocates in recent weeks have become increasingly concerned about the spread of misinformation that implies abortions aren\u8217\'20\'19t legal in California. The fear of criminalization or deportation may also keep California immigrants who lack legal status or are in the country with a temporary protected status from seeking abortions or public resources to help pay for care, they say.\par \par \uc2 Late-term abortions, once the fetus is viable, are still restricted in California and can be prosecuted, as can abortions performed outside a medical setting. \u8220\'20\'1CThis might include a situation where there\u8217\'20\'19s been rape and incest, and the person doesn\u8217\'20\'19t realize that they are pregnant until much later in the pregnancy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Upadhyay. \u8220\'20\'1CThese are extreme situations that apply only to a small number of patients.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 California could also do more to protect those who miscarry, or attempt self-abortions, advocates said. Just this week, a district attorney in California\u8217\'20\'19s conservative Kings county dropped the prosecution of a woman who spent four years in prison for giving birth to a stillborn child after using drugs. \u8220\'20\'1CCalifornia is definitely a leader in passing legislation to protect people seeking abortions,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jim\u233\'00\'E9nez. \u8220\'20\'1CBut there\u8217\'20\'19s still some work to be done in the decriminalisation of self-managed abortions\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And then there\u8217\'20\'19s the cost. Even with insurance, Lopez had to pay about $550 out-of-pocket for each of the two abortions she sought over the past three years. \u8220\'20\'1CI managed to pay, but it was a big chunk out of my savings,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The median out-of-pocket cost of a first-trimester for those without insurance in California is about $700.\par \par \uc2 One resident of the Central Valley city of Dinuba, whom the Guardian is not naming because they fear stigma from their family, said they ended up paying about $1,000 for a first-trimester medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year because concerns about an ectopic pregnancy required them to seek multiple follow-up appointments.\par \par \uc2 California recently passed legislation eliminating co-pays for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, and the state already covers \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs for those enrolled in the state\u8217\'20\'19s public insurance option for low-income residents. The governor this week announced an additional $57m to prepare for an influx of people from other states seeking abortions in California, including $40m for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers offering care to low-income patients without health insurance, and $15m to help community-based organizations with sexual and reproductive health outreach and education.\par \par \uc2 The funds \u8220\'20\'1Cwill go a long way\u8221\'20\'1D to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access \u8220\'20\'1Cfor those who live here and those who may be forced to seek care here\u8221\'20\'1D, said Jodi Hicks, president and CEO of Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California.\par \par \uc2 Telehealth visits could expand access\par \par \uc2 Researchers and advocates say that telemedicine clinics, which consult with patients online and mail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to clients, could be another way to quickly expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Telemedicine services typically charge about $300 for a simple service, and often don\u8217\'20\'19t take public or private health insurance due to the logistical complications of securing reimbursements for care delivered across county lines, said Mai Fleming, a physician and an assistant clinical professor at \uc2 UCSF\uc2  who also works with an online \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic.\par \par \uc2 The services aren\u8217\'20\'19t right for everyone and infeasible for many in rural California who lack internet access, but they still could be a solution for some who live in areas without clinics, she said. \u8220\'20\'1CGoing forward, I think expanding telehealth could be key to expanding access in rural parts of the state,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that California should work to make it easier for online clinics to accept insurance.\par \par \uc2 Often, brick-and-mortar \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics shy away from offering telehealth \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services precisely because of how insurance reimbursements work. Public and private insurance services reimburse at higher rates for in-person appointments that include an ultrasound, which is often medically unnecessary \u8211\'20\'13 leading clinics to require patients to come in person in order to recoup operational costs, Upadhyay said.\par \par \uc2 In an attempt to ramp up the number of healthcare workers available to perform abortions, California and some other states are also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/04/california-abortion-nurse-practitioners-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moving to allow}}}\uc2  nurse practitioners to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care without the supervision of a physician. And ultimately, more primary care providers and family clinics could start providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, Fleming said \u8211\'20\'13 though as it stands, primary care and family medicine residencies still aren\u8217\'20\'19t required to train doctors to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDespite its very progressive laws, hurdles and barriers to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care have existed for years,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. But now, especially, she added, \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s important to remember that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , legality, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, don\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily equal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  justice or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 13 May 2022. An earlier version named Jennifer Lopez but also stated that the Guardian was withholding her surname for privacy reasons. Lopez was happy to be named and the statement has been removed.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220513ei5d001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
