\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  rights are disappearing in the US. And Democrats have no plan\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,367 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par 15:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In moments like these, it would be helpful if we had a party with spine to vote for\par \par \uc2 The Democrats have no plan. On Wednesday, just over a week after Politico published a leaked draft of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/may/03/as-the-us-supreme-court-moves-to-end-abortion-is-america-still-a-free-country"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Justice Samuel Alito\u8217\'20\'19s forthcoming majority opinion}}}\uc2  in Dobbs v Jackson, which will overrule Roe v Wade next month, the Senate did the only thing they could think to do: they voted on the Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Protection Act, a bill to codify a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The bill had already been brought to a vote this past February \u8211\'20\'13 before the leak of the opinion, but long after it became clear that the supreme court would end the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right \u8211\'20\'13 and it had already failed. On Wednesday, it failed again, by 49 votes to 51, on party lines: only one Democrat, Joe Manchin of West Virginia, sided with the unanimous Republicans in denying women a federally recognized right to make their own decisions for their health and lives. This was no surprise: bringing the act to a vote was a mere symbolic gesture. Democratic women senators and their staff had walked to the floor for the vote chanting \u8220\'20\'1CMy body, my decision.\u8221\'20\'1D But ultimately, they weren\u8217\'20\'19t powerful enough to get it passed. They didn\u8217\'20\'19t have the votes.\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s next in the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 fight to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights? Evidently, not much. After the WHPA failed, Vice-President \uc2 Kamala Harris{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/HerbieZiskend46/status/1524491379958759424"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told ABC News}}}\uc2  that Americans\u8217\'20\'19 best chance to protect their sovereignty over their own bodies was to vote more, and vote harder. \u8220\'20\'1CA priority for all who care about this issue should be to elect pro-choice leaders at the local, the state and the federal level.\u8221\'20\'1D It was a shrugging admission of defeat, and the administration has consistently passed the buck. \u8220\'20\'1CIt will fall on voters to elect pro-choice officials in November,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden himself said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/05/04/white-house-abortion-executive-actions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wednesday statement}}}\uc2 . The responsibility for securing this essential right, the administration seems to say, lies with voters \u8211\'20\'13 not with them.\par \par \uc2 For her part, Harris also added that the Senate majority\u8217\'20\'19s vote did not reflect most Americans\u8217\'20\'19 feelings about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But then again, the Senate majority does not represent most Americans at all: the 49 senators who voted in favor of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights represent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/2021/1/6/22215728/senate-anti-democratic-one-number-raphael-warnock-jon-ossoff-georgia-runoffs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tens of millions}}}\uc2  more people than the 51 senators who voted against them.\par \par \uc2 And here lies a legitimate conundrum for the Democratic leadership: their voters are enraged and scared by the Dobbs opinion, and by the coming end of legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an estimated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/abortion-rights-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 to 28 states}}}\uc2 . But the party leadership insists they cannot intervene, claiming that there is relatively little that can be done to stop, slow or mitigate this coming legal and humanitarian disaster. It is part of the rot of our political system that, like the electoral college, our legislatures have been manipulated by gerrymandering and malapportionment to ensure that Republicans can get fewer votes from the people and yet control more seats in the Capitol \u8211\'20\'13 and Democrats must not just win the popular vote, but win it by impossibly large margins to have any power at all. Republican victories are increasingly inevitable, even as the majority of voters move away from the party\u8217\'20\'19s rapid extremism, and Democratic victories are largely toothless even as they become more and more difficult to achieve. This is a real structural obstacle the Democrats face, and one that is incompatible with continuing American democracy. It threatens to enshrine permanent Republican minority rule.\par \par \uc2 And yet it is more than a little frustrating that the Democratic leadership, who after all do control both Congress and the White House, appear to be so wholly unprepared for the fall of Roe, and even surprised by the Alito opinion. \u8220\'20\'1CWe will be ready when any ruling is issued,\u8221\'20\'1D Biden said in a statement after the opinion became public, perhaps accidentally betraying that he is decidedly not ready now. Reporting from the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  shows the White House in an apparent state of confusion and chaos in the days since the draft leak, scrambling for ways to respond. \u8220\'20\'1CThe leaked document caught the White House off guard,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/05/04/white-house-abortion-executive-actions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Post reports}}}\uc2 . Really?\par \par \uc2 It should not have. The reversal of Roe has been the decades-long, publicly stated, and passionately pursued goal of the Republican right. The conservative legal movement has spent years cultivating young jurists with the express purpose of deploying them to deny reproductive rights. Members of the supreme court have made it abundantly clear that they would consider ending women\u8217\'20\'19s bodily sovereignty to be the pinnacle of their careers.\par \par \uc2 Not only has the problem long been obvious, but so have many of the solutions\par \par \uc2 Feminist and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists, not to mention women\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare providers, have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/sep/04/america-abortion-crisis-roe-v-wade-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sounding the alarm}}}\uc2  for just as long, pleading for help from Democratic officials as increasingly draconian and labyrinthine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have made the procedure harder and more expensive to access. At clinics across the country every Saturday for decades, protesters appear to scream at patients and providers alike that they are murderers. These anti-choice zealots have waged a years-long campaign of terrorism, stalking providers, putting photos of patients online and committing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/07/us/knoxville-planned-parenthood-arson.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arsons}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.upi.com/Archives/1985/01/08/Bombing-suspects-named-plot-for-Bible-hero/6596474008400/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bombings}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://msmagazine.com/2022/04/07/lauren-handy-fetus-fetal-remains-justice-department-anti-abortion-dc-clinic-invasion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 invasions}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2009/06/01/us/01tiller.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 assassinations}}}\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already been illegal after six weeks \u8211\'20\'13 and Roe effectively moot \u8211\'20\'13 in Texas since September of last year. And yet the White House says it was \u8220\'20\'1Ccaught off guard\u8221\'20\'1D? I know some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers who would envy the luxury of that ignorance.\par \par \uc2 Not only has the problem long been obvious, but so have many of the solutions. For months, human rights activists and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/30/opinion/abortion-pills-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 legal experts}}}\uc2  have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/politics/hyde-amendment-abortion-biden/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pleading}}}\uc2  with the Biden administration to take aggressive action to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within its own authority \u8211\'20\'13 for while the legislative avenue to codifying women\u8217\'20\'19s rights seems foreclosed, the Democrats still control the expansive authority of the executive branch. Biden could instruct the FDA to loosen its uniquely onerous and medically unnecessary restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 He could lease federal lands and buildings in red states to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, creating islands of access inside the vast stretches of the American landscape where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will soon be illegal. His administration can sue to block state restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication that are more stringent than the FDA\u8217\'20\'19s rules, citing federal supremacy. They could make a more aggressive push for Congress to end the Hyde Amendment, a budget rider that bans federal funds from covering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Or they could circumvent it, by paying for women\u8217\'20\'19s transportation to legal-care states.\par \par \uc2 As \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  becomes not just illegal, but criminal, the administration can direct the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  to step in to protect patients from being reported to law enforcement and prosecuted. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Emma_Roth/status/1525078274895925248"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Emma Roth}}}\uc2 , an attorney with the advocacy group National Advocates for Pregnant Women, the \uc2 HHS\uc2  has this authority both under HIPAA\u8217\'20\'19s privacy protections, and under {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://public3.pagefreezer.com/content/HHS.gov/31-12-2020T08:51/https:/www.hhs.gov/about/news/2020/06/12/hhs-finalizes-rule-section-1557-protecting-civil-rights-healthcare.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 section 1557}}}\uc2 , a federal regulation preventing sex discrimination in healthcare.\par \par \uc2 But the administration hasn\u8217\'20\'19t done any of this. They don\u8217\'20\'19t seem interested in trying. Maybe it\u8217\'20\'19s because any move to secure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access will be challenged in hostile federal courts \u8211\'20\'13 though the possibility of lawsuits has never deterred the anti-choice side. Or maybe it\u8217\'20\'19s because of embarrassment or disdain for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the Democratic party, coupled with their historic unwillingness to prioritize feminist issues. After all, during the primaries, Biden was the only candidate who supported the Hyde Amendment. And while Democratic leaders admonish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters to vote, they are also backing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/nancy-pelosi-henry-ceuller-support-1352290/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Henry Cuellar}}}\uc2 , an anti-choice Texas Democrat, in his primary opposite the young pro-choice progressive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jessicacisnerosforcongress.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jessica Cisneros}}}\uc2 . After the draft opinion leaked, the White House had no strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, but they did find time to scold frightened pro-choice voters who protested in ways that Biden apparently found indecorous. The administration isn\u8217\'20\'19t wrong that those of us who support women\u8217\'20\'19s human rights must vote. But it would be helpful if we had a party with spine to vote for.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220517ei5h001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
