\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I feel angry and incredibly motivated\u8217\'20\'19: US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  providers consider options for fighting back\b0\par\par\uc2 Carey Dunne \par \uc2 1,430 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par 14:49 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Doctors around the country are anticipating the struggles of patients and providers after the likely fall of Roe v Wade: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19ve been planning for it\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Last week, Dr Maya Bass, a family physician, was on a call with the medical director of Trust Women, one of four \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in Oklahoma. They were scrambling to figure out how to keep their doors open after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.com/news/2022/05/02/supreme-court-abortion-draft-opinion-00029473"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leak}}}\uc2  of a draft opinion signaling the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s intent to overturn Roe v Wade and the Oklahoma governor\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2022/05/03/politics/oklahoma-heartbeat-act-abortion-governor-stitt-signs/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signing of SB1503 into law}}}\uc2 , banning abortions in the state after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith my colleagues, there\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of anger,\u8221\'20\'1D Bass says. \u8220\'20\'1CAs a medical professional, it\u8217\'20\'19s really hard to spend your life studying to become an expert in a certain area and then have a politician with no medical expertise and not even a uterus take away your power to offer patients safe medical care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 About once a month since 2017, Bass has traveled from her home in Camden, New Jersey, to moonlight at Trust Women Oklahoma City to help mitigate the shortage of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in the middle of the country. The tiny clinic\u8217\'20\'19s staff had been stretched to its limit since September, when neighboring Texas banned abortions past six weeks of pregnancy, forcing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sites.utexas.edu/txpep/files/2022/03/TxPEP-out-of-state-SB8.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an estimated 6,000 Texans}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/apr/25/oklahoma-abortion-clinics-texas-new-bans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 travel to Oklahoma}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Oklahoma will no longer be a refuge for Texan patients. A near copycat of the Texas law, Oklahoma\u8217\'20\'19s new ban would allow any private citizen to sue any individual \u8211\'20\'13 doctors, nurses, pharmacists \u8211\'20\'13 who \u8220\'20\'1Caids or abets the performance or inducement of an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D after signs of fetal cardiac activity. And in the aftermath of a Roe reversal, Oklahoma is one of 13 states that would immediately ban almost all abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe staff is exhausted,\u8221\'20\'1D says Bass. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re amazing and resilient and are probably some of the most creative humans I\u8217\'20\'19ve ever met, in that they\u8217\'20\'19ve been figuring out with every new legal hurdle different plans to serve as many people as possible. \u8216\'20\'18How can we legally care for patients with a 72-hour waiting period and a six-week ban? How can we pull this off?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 They considered options for fighting back. Perhaps they could best serve patients by offering consultations and pre-screenings in their Oklahoma City clinic, then sending them to their sister clinic in Wichita, Kansas, a two-and-a-half-hour drive away, for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure itself \u8211\'20\'13 but would that somehow put them at risk of being prosecuted for \u8220\'20\'1Caiding and abetting\u8221\'20\'1D an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ? Would that implicate the Wichita clinic? Would Oklahoma City staffers be able to serve more patients if they moved to Kansas to practice there?\par \par \uc2 After her call with the medical director, Bass cancelled her next trip, which would have been in June. The clinic in Oklahoma City \u8211\'20\'13 which receives some 500 calls a day \u8211\'20\'13 has put a temporary hold on new appointments. \u8220\'20\'1CPatients were too nervous to schedule with us,\u8221\'20\'1D Bass says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe couldn\u8217\'20\'19t promise we\u8217\'20\'19d actually be able to see them.\u8221\'20\'1D The facility is now referring patients to Wichita. Appointments for the Kansas clinic are all currently full.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19ve been planning for this\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPregnancy is hard,\u8221\'20\'1D Bass says. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re tired and nauseous. People have hyperemesis \u8211\'20\'13 they continually vomit. Mental health disorders tend to get worse, especially during an undesired pregnancy. There are so many stories of people coming in being like, \u8216\'20\'18I can\u8217\'20\'19t function, I can\u8217\'20\'19t do my job, I\u8217\'20\'19ve taken too many sick days.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , negative pregnancy side-effects subside. But trigger laws that require patients to travel out of state to access care force more patients to stay pregnant longer. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s an extra four weeks of suffering,\u8221\'20\'1D Bass says. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd that is definitely something that will worsen because even if a person has resources, the clinics that exist after Roe falls will see bigger volumes and wait periods.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For doctors around the country, the struggles of patients and providers in Texas and Oklahoma offer a glimpse of what\u8217\'20\'19s to come after the likely fall of Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel sad and I feel tired and I feel angry and incredibly motivated,\u8221\'20\'1D said Dr Katherine Farris, chief medical officer of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic in Raleigh, North Carolina. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s stunning and gut-wrenching, but we\u8217\'20\'19re not surprised. We\u8217\'20\'19ve been planning for it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In so-called sanctuary states like North Carolina, that planning largely revolves around figuring out how already overwhelmed clinics can serve even bigger waves of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Crefugees\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Even with efforts to bolster staffing, clinics expect increased wait times, which will force more patients to carry pregnancies past the limit for medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , leading to a demand for more second-trimester procedural abortions. \u8220\'20\'1CSo the people who say they\u8217\'20\'19re so upset by later pregnancy abortions are creating a scenario where more will occur,\u8221\'20\'1D Farris says.\par \par \uc2 In a landscape rife with misinformation and stigma, where Googling \u8220\'20\'1Chow to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D often turns up misleading ads for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/aug/16/georgia-abortion-crisis-pregnancy-centers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  anti-\b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  \u8220\'20\'1Ccrisis pregnancy centers\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , providers are preparing to educate more patients about options for safe and affordable reproductive healthcare. Even if Trust Women Oklahoma City is completely barred from providing abortions in-house, they plan to stay open, offering informational consultations and other reproductive healthcare services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur patients need us as resource guides more than ever now,\u8221\'20\'1D Bass says. \u8220\'20\'1CIf someone calls me, it\u8217\'20\'19s not enough for me to say, \u8216\'20\'18Here\u8217\'20\'19s a number for the nearest clinic.\u8217\'20\'19 I need to say, \u8216\'20\'18Here\u8217\'20\'19s the clinic, here\u8217\'20\'19s the number for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  fund in this area if you have money issues, here\u8217\'20\'19s the free hotline for emotional consultations.\u8217\'20\'19 Healthcare providers need to serve our patients now by giving them clear resources, websites with up-to-date information.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Preparing for a post-Roe America also means providers are figuring out how to legally and ethically counsel patients on how to safely self-source and self-manage abortions.\par \par \uc2 In a pre-Roe landscape, before the advent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, self-managing posed serious risks of injury or death. But now, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are safe ways to manage at home\u8221\'20\'1D, Bass says. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m hoping more providers recognize the safety of [self-managed medication abortions] so we can support our patients through that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Patients may self-manage through other means, such as contacting the loose underground networks of women trained to provide safe at-home abortions outside the law, using devices such as manual vacuum aspirators made from mason jars, or herbal abortifacients, such as pennyroyal tea or black cohosh.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have friends in the more natural, herbal world talking about going back to the old days of using different herbs,\u8221\'20\'1D Farris says. \u8220\'20\'1COther patients will go back to physical attempted inductions. And those will be dangerous.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Even as they plan how best to handle this latest blow to reproductive health, these doctors expect their efforts won\u8217\'20\'19t be enough to stem the devastation of a Roe reversal. Providers are increasingly forced into interactions with patients that feel crude and absurd.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had patients sitting in front of me sobbing, saying, \u8216\'20\'18Please, please, I need you to do this today,\u8217\'20\'19 but they didn\u8217\'20\'19t get their 72-hour consent,\u8221\'20\'1D Farris says, referring to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/state-policy/explore/counseling-and-waiting-periods-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 time mandated by states}}}\uc2  between counseling and getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1COr their 72-hour consent time isn\u8217\'20\'19t up for five more hours, and I have to say, \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m sorry, I need you to wait five more hours before I hand you your pill in order to be in compliance with the law.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She says she has to explain to patients that though it breaks her heart, she can\u8217\'20\'19t make exceptions because it would jeopardize her being able to treat thousands of other women who need her care every year. She knows her work will continue to be sought out, probably with increasing desperation and a dire outlook. Research {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://osf.io/preprints/socarxiv/sb5f2/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 suggests}}}\uc2  that a total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban would lead to a 21% increase in pregnancy-related deaths.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is going to be a lot of forced birth. People\u8217\'20\'19s lives are going to be devastated by this. People are going to be emotionally and financially traumatized. People\u8217\'20\'19s futures are going to be derailed,\u8221\'20\'1D Farris says. \u8220\'20\'1CI will be stunned if people don\u8217\'20\'19t die. And it should not come down to people dying for us to reconsider autonomy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220517ei5h0018i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
