\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California proposes new funding to help low-income and out-of-state  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  seekers\b0\par\par\uc2 Maanvi Singh and agencies \par \uc2 477 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par 14:59 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  wants $57m for organizations offering the procedure to the uninsured and for reproductive health outreach\par \par \uc2 California\u8217\'20\'19s governor has announced a new reproductive health spending plan, dedicating an additional $57m to prepare for an influx of people from other states seeking abortions in California.\par \par \uc2 About $40m in Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s proposed budget would fund grants for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers offering care to low-income patients without health insurance. An additional $15m would go to help community-based organizations do sexual and reproductive health outreach and education.\par \par \uc2 The state\u8217\'20\'19s governor and legislators have vowed to make California a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/california-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 safe haven for \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  care}}}\uc2  in anticipation of a supreme court ruling this summer that could end federal protections for abortions. No other US state does more to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, according to a report by the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 , a pro-choice research group.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCalifornia will not stand idly by as extremists roll back our basic constitutional rights; we\u8217\'20\'19re going to fight like hell, making sure that all women \u8211\'20\'13 not just those in California \u8211\'20\'13 know that this state continues to recognize and protect their fundamental rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Newsom said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Although insurance companies are required to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in California, and the state\u8217\'20\'19s public health insurance plan for low-income residents covers abortions, those without insurance generally pay between $655 and $940.\par \par \uc2 If the supreme court rolls back federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , officials and reproductive health advocates expect thousands of people to come to California from more conservative states to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Newsom\u8217\'20\'19s proposed grants could help lower the costs for those people, who wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t otherwise qualify for state health subsidies or coverage.\par \par \uc2 A separate bill in California\u8217\'20\'19s legislature would also set up a fund, financed by state and private contributions, to cover the travel, lodging and childcare costs of those coming to California for reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 Newsom\u8217\'20\'19s budget announcement \u8220\'20\'1Cwill go a long way\u8221\'20\'1D to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access \u8220\'20\'1Cfor those who live here and those who may be forced to seek care here\u8221\'20\'1D, said Jodi Hicks, president and CEO of Planned Parenthood Affiliates of California.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in the state legislature have proposed 13 bills to improve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in California. Newsom has already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/abortion-us-supreme-court-business-health-california-d4b58d86434c9c790b8bbfcb7240f2af"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed one of them into law}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 a bill that makes abortions cheaper by banning private insurance plans from charging co-pays or deductibles for the procedure. The other proposals are moving through the legislature.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have opposed these proposals in California, but blocking them will be difficult. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ppic.org/blog/a-broad-range-of-californians-oppose-overturning-roe-v-wade/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Polling shows}}}\uc2  a majority of California voters support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, including a majority of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Any new spending must first be approved by the state legislature. Lawmakers must pass a budget by 15 June.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220516ei5g002pw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
