\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 UN\uc2  official on Roe v Wade: reversal would \u8216\'20\'18give legitimacy to growing anti-women\u8217\'20\'19s rights\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 944 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par 15:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Special rapporteur Dr Tlaleng Mofokeng urges the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  not to reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, which speak to who owns women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies in society\par \par \uc2 The United Nations special rapporteur on the right to health, one of the international body\u8217\'20\'19s most important human rights advocates, has urged the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  not to end federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in America.\par \par \uc2 The comments from the special rapporteur, Dr Tlaleng Mofokeng, come after a leaked supreme court draft opinion showed a majority of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative justices support overturning the nearly 50-year-old precedent in Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The 1973 decision provided federal protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and invalidated dozens of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. It also became a beacon for the global feminist movement, which over the next five decades helped liberalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws across dozens of countries, including most recently in Latin America, and enshrine the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in international human rights law.\par \par \uc2 The right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Cspeaks directly to women and girls, who are often in many societies not seen as people\u8221\'20\'1D, Mofokeng said in an interview with the Guardian. The right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  speaks to who owns women\u8217\'20\'19s bodies in society and, \u8220\'20\'1Cwho fully can decide \u8211\'20\'13 and benefit from that bodily autonomy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If the court were to end protections for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 US states would be certain or likely to outlaw \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2 , forcing women to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/05/abortion-deserts-clinics-access-closed-map"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 travel hundreds of miles to the nearest clinic}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/07/abortion-pill-at-home-activists-future-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-manage abortions with medication}}}\uc2  and heighten the risk of prosecution, abuse and violence for both women and doctors.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt sends chills down my spine to think that the court is being brought on to play \u8211\'20\'13 as a very powerful player \u8211\'20\'13 to decide on an issue of human rights that has jurisprudence, and has a basis in legal findings, that will actually lead to restriction of rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mofokeng.\par \par \uc2 Mofokeng was the lead expert in an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/08/catastrophic-implications-un-health-expert-condemns-us-over-threat-to-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 amicus brief to the supreme court}}}\uc2 , sometimes called a \u8220\'20\'1Cfriend of the court\u8221\'20\'1D brief, in which she urged justices not to reverse US \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on human rights grounds.\par \par \uc2 In both her brief and interview with the Guardian, she said a reversal of Roe v Wade would, \u8220\'20\'1Clegitimize the use of morality and theological reasoning\u8221\'20\'1D to take away human rights, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/may/03/erosion-of-abortion-rights-gathers-pace-around-the-world-as-us-signals-new-era"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 empower anti-rights activists globally}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/06/abortion-bans-patient-doctor-medical-advice"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criminalize the practice of medicine}}}\uc2 , undermine the doctor-patient relationship and expose healthcare providers and patients to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jul/06/anti-abortion-activists-operation-save-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heightened abuse and violence}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhether you are in the United States, whether you are in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/may/05/us-far-right-abortion-laws-europe-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Europe}}}\uc2 , whether you are in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global-development/2022/may/05/latin-america-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latin America}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 it matters not where you are,\u8221\'20\'1D Mofokeng said. The rights at issue in the case, \u8220\'20\'1Care human rights, important human rights issues\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey deal with human rights principles of dignity, they deal with human rights principles of equality,\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cimportant issues of human rights of nondiscrimination\u8221\'20\'1D. Considering access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a human right helps people see, \u8220\'20\'1Cwhy then the overturning would be catastrophic to those very principles of human rights\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 People who are anti-women\u8217\'20\'19s rights and anti-equality see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a low hanging fruit\par \par \uc2 Dr Tlaleng Mofokeng\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey are part of the bigger global issue, where you have anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements growing but also anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation being implemented more widely across the world,\u8221\'20\'1D Mofokeng said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe court will give legitimacy to an ever-growing anti-women\u8217\'20\'19s rights [movement] in totality,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mofokeng. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who are anti-women\u8217\'20\'19s rights and anti-equality see \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a low hanging fruit.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her comments have been echoed by other international human rights groups such as the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ibanet.org/IBAHRI-expresses-concern-over-US-Supreme-Court-potential-curbing-of-the-right-to-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 International Bar Association}}}\uc2  (Iba), the foremost association of international attorneys, and by Ipas, a US-based civil society organization that seeks to strengthen \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Iba said overturning Roe v Wade would, \u8220\'20\'1Creverse one of the most important precedents in US history\u8221\'20\'1D, and represent a \u8220\'20\'1Cbackward step\u8221\'20\'1D when countries such as Argentina, Colombia and Mexico are protecting reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 The president and CEO of Ipas, Anu Kumar, said \u8220\'20\'1Cthe US already has a tainted reputation on reproductive justice,\u8221\'20\'1D and that any reversal of Roe v Wade would energize, \u8220\'20\'1Canti-choice white supremacist organizations\u8221\'20\'1D globally.\par \par \uc2 Should Roe v Wade be overturned, the fact that Americans of color, low-income women, sexual minorities and the young will be disproportionately likely to be forced to carry unwanted pregnancies to term, Mofokeng said underscores her concerns.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhere will people stop? Will the next thing be about putting in jail or criminalizing people who treat drug users with dignity? Where will we draw the line? People who are already in a vulnerable situation should not be further marginalized by a law or political context, in fact it\u8217\'20\'19s precisely those people who the law must ensure protection of rights,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Although outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  across more than half of states may violate \uc2 UN\uc2  treaties to which the US is party, including the convention against torture, it is unlikely the \uc2 UN\uc2  could hold the US accountable. That is because the US holds a powerful position within the \uc2 UN\uc2 , as a founding member.\par \par \uc2 However, even if the \uc2 UN\uc2  cannot hold the US accountable directly, it will probably have other impacts on US standing, especially the ability of the US to advocate for the rights of women and girls globally.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you then have the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  making judgment that threatens the very human rights of people who are pregnant, when that court legitimizes attacks on healthcare workers, when that court legitimizes austerity and cuts to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics \u8211\'20\'13 you can see then how this is a global problem,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mofokeng.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220516ei5g0028x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
