\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 IVF treatment faces \u8216\'20\'18clear and present danger\u8217\'20\'19 from US anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  effort\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Kirchgaessner in Washington \par \uc2 942 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par 14:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 32\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Warning from reproductive rights advocate comes as US states pass or debate legislation that would give full rights to embryos\par \par \uc2 In vitro fertilization treatment is facing a \u8220\'20\'1Cclear and present danger\u8221\'20\'1D and could be a \u8220\'20\'1Ccasualty\u8221\'20\'1D of some of the proposed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws that are emerging across the US, according to an advocate of reproductive medicine.\par \par \uc2 The warning comes as US states, including Louisiana, have passed or are debating new proposed legislation that would give full rights to embryos, which in some cases means fertilized eggs, created in the process of IVF and not implanted in a woman, would have the same legal rights as children.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing is that in their fervor to curry the most favor with the forced birth movement, politicians are crafting some really sloppily worded legislation,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Sean Tipton\uc2 , the chief advocacy and policy officer of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reproductivefacts.org/news-and-publications/news-and-research/press-releases-and-bulletins/asrm-issues-statement-regarding-roe-v.-wade-and-its-possible-implications-on-access-to-reproductive-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a fever to be as restrictive as you can.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Experts say that the passage of so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonhood\u8221\'20\'1D laws, which give rights to fertilized eggs, could dramatically curtail the availability and practice of IVF in some states, and could in effect make it illegal for IVF facilities to freeze or discard embryos created in the process.\par \par \uc2 Tipton said he believed that proposed laws \u8211\'20\'13 which could be passed if Roe v Wade is overturned by the supreme court in coming weeks \u8211\'20\'13 could have \u8220\'20\'1Cunintended consequences\u8221\'20\'1D on issues ranging from ectopic pregnancies to IVF. If, for example, Tipton said, laws are not written to give doctors the right to perform abortions for women who have an ectopic pregnancy, it would represent a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeath sentence for the mother\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 While most Republican legislators have not explicitly criticized IVF, some of the most extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists \u8211\'20\'13 especially those affiliated with the Catholic church \u8211\'20\'13 have long been critical of the practice.\par \par \uc2 Steve Aden\uc2 , chief legal officer for \uc2 Americans United for Life\uc2 , recently likened the process of IVF to \u8220\'20\'1Ceugenics\u8221\'20\'1D, a term coined in the 1880s which refers to the bogus science of selecting desirable characteristics to improve and strengthen future generations.\par \par \uc2 Aden said in an interview with the Guardian that \u8220\'20\'1Cmost\u8221\'20\'1D forms of IVF created \u8220\'20\'1Cembryonic human beings\u8221\'20\'1D in a \u8220\'20\'1Ceugenic way\u8221\'20\'1D, making it \u8220\'20\'1Cinevitable\u8221\'20\'1D that embryos would be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI do think that as people understand better what IVF does \u8211\'20\'13 even people who are engaged in the process of IVF sometimes don\u8217\'20\'19t understand the implications of it \u8211\'20\'13 that we will turn away from this creation and freezing of hundreds of thousands of distinct individual human beings who remain in limbo forever or are ultimately cast off as if the trash,\u8221\'20\'1D Aden said. \u8220\'20\'1CI think that\u8217\'20\'19s untenable in a culture that respects all human life.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Students for Life, another anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that has gained traction in recent years for its support of total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, has said IVF comes with its own \u8220\'20\'1Cunique set of risks\u8221\'20\'1D and called it a \u8220\'20\'1Cdamaging and destructive process\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The group has instead promoted what it has called a \u8220\'20\'1Cbreakthrough\u8221\'20\'1D in reproductive health called NaProTechnology. The process, which is backed and promoted by Catholic institutions that oppose IVF, relies on the monitoring of cervical mucus and bleeding changes in a woman\u8217\'20\'19s menstrual cycle to try to assess why a woman might be experiencing infertility.\par \par \uc2 Republican legislators have in the past sought to ensure that IVF procedures were carved out of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. But Tipton of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reproductivefacts.org/news-and-publications/news-and-research/press-releases-and-bulletins/asrm-issues-statement-regarding-roe-v.-wade-and-its-possible-implications-on-access-to-reproductive-care/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 American Society for Reproductive Medicine}}}\uc2  said he believed some legislators were increasingly seeing \u8220\'20\'1Cscientific realities\u8221\'20\'1D as political \u8220\'20\'1Ccompromises\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If the draft decision that was leaked last week that overturns Roe vs Wade is ultimately issued by the supreme court, it would leave questions around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to individual states.\par \par \uc2 Justice Amy Coney Barrett, who sided with the majority in the draft opinion, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.usatoday.com/videos/news/nation/2020/10/13/barrett-declines-state-her-views-ivf/3645094001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was asked about her views about IVF }}}\uc2  during her 2020 confirmation hearing. The questions followed a report in the Guardian that detailed how, years earlier, when Barrett was a law professor at Notre Dame, she publicly stated her opposition to Roe v Wade and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/oct/01/amy-coney-barrett-supported-group-fertilization"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed a newspaper advertisement}}}\uc2  by an extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization that said it believed life began at \u8220\'20\'1Cfertilization\u8221\'20\'1D, a common refrain for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups that oppose IVF and certain forms of contraception.\par \par \uc2 The organization, called St Joseph County Right to Life, has said it believes that discarding excess embryos created during the IVF process was akin to the act of having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that the process of discarding embryos ought to be criminalized.\par \par \uc2 Pressed on the matter by Senator Richard Blumenthal, a Democrat, Barrett said the statement she signed had validated the views of her church. She declined to answer questions about whether criminalizing IVF would be constitutional, saying the question was \u8220\'20\'1Cabstract\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Barrett said in her confirmation hearings that her personal religious views would not have a bearing on her legal judgements.\par \par \uc2 In Indiana, before she was a supreme court justice, Barrett served on the council of St Joseph Catholic church, which is part of the Fort Wayne-South Bend diocese in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 The diocese has spoken forcefully against IVF, including one 2019 newsletter report by a priest, \uc2 Tadeusz Pacholczyk\uc2 , who said creating children in the \u8220\'20\'1Ccold, clinical and commercial venture that is in vitro fertilization\u8221\'20\'1D was an act against the \u8220\'20\'1Chuman dignity of our offspring\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe heart of the IVF process itself, the practice of joining sperm and egg together in the fertility clinic, remains an intrinsic evil, flowing from the decision to allow our offspring to be \u8220\'20\'1Cmanufactured,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220512ei5c000ry\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
