\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I became the  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  lady of Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19: the mother of seven who devoted her life to the pro-choice cause\b0\par\par\uc2 Emine Saner \par \uc2 1,991 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par 13:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Raised a fundamentalist Christian, Laurie Bertram Roberts grew up believing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was evil. Then a pregnancy put her life at risk \u8211\'20\'13 and she was denied the termination she desperately needed\par \par \uc2 When Laurie Bertram Roberts was 17, she was sent home from hospital and almost bled to death. Pregnant and experiencing bleeding, she had gone to the emergency department of her nearest hospital in Indiana twice, and was told she was miscarrying, but, because a scan showed the foetus still had a heartbeat, she was also told there was nothing they could do. What she needed, in order to end a pregnancy that was ending anyway, was an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but she says the Catholic hospital would not provide one. \u8220\'20\'1CThey had the power to end my pregnancy right there, when I was already bleeding fairly heavily, in a tremendous amount of pain. Instead, they sent my scared 17-year-old self, a mother of two already, home.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There Bertram Roberts collapsed. She closes her eyes, visualising the scene. \u8220\'20\'1CI remember what it feels like to think you\u8217\'20\'19re dying. Laying on the floor, in my mom\u8217\'20\'19s kitchen, I passed out.\u8221\'20\'1D She was taken to hospital to have the emergency procedure that she could have had earlier.\par \par \uc2 She had twins already, having got pregnant and married at 16 (though the marriage didn\u8217\'20\'19t last). By the time she was 25, Bertram Roberts, now 44, had seven children. She had wanted to terminate one of these pregnancies \u8211\'20\'13 her health was suffering so soon after her previous birth, and she was a single parent living in poverty. It seemed impossible to have another baby, but when she arrived at the clinic, she found she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI got turned away, and then I ended up being too far along. The trauma of that, even though I love my child \u8230\'20\'26 I came to terms with having to stay pregnant, but not everybody does. I was fortunate \u8211\'20\'13 I had my mom and my grandmother to help me. That\u8217\'20\'19s the only reason I made it through. I can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine what it would have been like if I didn\u8217\'20\'19t have that.\u8221\'20\'1D Both these events \u8211\'20\'13 needing or wanting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not being able to have one \u8211\'20\'13 crystallised Bertram Roberts\u8217\'20\'19s belief in reproductive rights, and set her on a course as an activist.\par \par \uc2 She has always been an activist \u8211\'20\'13 at 11, she started a petition at school to get a bullying janitor fired\par \par \uc2 Bertram Roberts, who identifies as a non-binary femme, is executive director of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.yellowhammerfund.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Yellowhammer Fund}}}\uc2 , an organisation based in Alabama that provides \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care and campaigns for reproductive justice. When we speak over Zoom, Bertram Roberts \u8211\'20\'13 warm, funny, just the person you\u8217\'20\'19d want to get you out of a difficult situation \u8211\'20\'13 is at home in Tuscaloosa, with a Wonder Woman poster just behind her. The previous organisation she founded and is still involved with, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.msreprofreedomfund.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mississippi Reproductive Freedom Fund}}}\uc2  (MRFF), is now run by two of her daughters.\par \par \uc2 Since the draft opinion {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/supreme-court-abortion-law-leak-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overturning Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , which had established a legal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was leaked, Bertram Roberts says: \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t been able to sleep, but before this, I was having trouble sleeping.\u8221\'20\'1D In the states she has lived and worked in, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has always been fragile, and increasingly limited. Mississippi has a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/09/mississippi-governor-contraception-abortion-rights"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trigger law}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, which means that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be banned if, as looks likely, Roe v Wade is overturned. It will almost certainly become illegal in Alabama, too, as well as neighbouring states, meaning options for women \u8211\'20\'13 even if they could afford to travel \u8211\'20\'13 will become extremely limited. It will disproportionately affect women of colour, who accounted for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.alabamapublichealth.gov/healthstats/assets/itop_2019al.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 nearly 67% of abortions}}}\uc2  in Alabama in 2019, and those living below the poverty level, who will be more likely to self-manage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with pills at the risk of being caught, \u8220\'20\'1Cand criminalisation is going to disproportionately fall on black and brown bodies, because it always does.\u8221\'20\'1D Forced birth, says Bertram Roberts, \u8220\'20\'1Cis going to kill people. You can\u8217\'20\'19t force people to stay pregnant and then not expect that people are going to die, when it\u8217\'20\'19s more risky to give birth than to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And that\u8217\'20\'19s just in general, that\u8217\'20\'19s not even specific to black women.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yellowhammer and MRFF have provided women with money to pay for not only the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself (since 2020, Yellowhammer has had its own clinic, but both funds enable women to access clinics across state lines), but transport, childcare costs, and food. Bertram Roberts sees reproductive justice as all-encompassing \u8211\'20\'13 not just providing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and contraception, but supporting families to look after the children they already have. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re also doing work around self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making sure people know how to safely use pills.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bertram Roberts was raised a fundamentalist Christian and grew up believing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was evil. As a child, she would go on \u8220\'20\'1Cplenty of anti-choice events. I got told that I was a survivor of the \u8216\'20\'18post-Roe holocaust\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D A grim smile. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd they would specifically say to me I should be very grateful that my white mother decided to have me because she was pregnant with a black man\u8217\'20\'19s baby.\u8221\'20\'1D Then, she says, \u8220\'20\'1Clife happened, and I became a teenage mom and wife. I got pregnant again and again and again, and you just learned that no issue is as black and white as anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists try to make it seem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She has always been an activist \u8211\'20\'13 when she was about 11, she started a petition at school to get a janitor, who had been bullying children, fired. In her 20s, she went to a community college, and excelled, despite having her last two children during her time there. At 27, she got a scholarship to \uc2 Jackson State University\uc2  in Mississippi, leaving her children with her mother while she studied for a political science degree. She would call them every evening, and spend every night marking their homework. In her third year, they were allowed to come and live with her on campus.\par \par \uc2 University \u8211\'20\'13 where she embraced racial justice and feminist campaigning \u8211\'20\'13 politicised her. As an older student, and mother of seven, young students sought her out if they were pregnant and wanted an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWhen folks needed to figure out how to get to a clinic or how to get the money, I was kind of that person.\u8221\'20\'1D She was also part of a group fighting to get the university to take the domestic violence she had witnessed on campus seriously (before university, she had survived domestic abuse). During this time, a fellow student, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://eu.clarionledger.com/story/news/2021/11/18/latasha-norma-death-mississippi-domestic-violence-awareness-run-jackson-state-counseling-center/8606354002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latasha Norman, was killed by her boyfriend}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe could have been doing actual preventative work,\u8221\'20\'1D says Bertram Roberts. \u8220\'20\'1COne of the lessons that I learned from that is that we help us. It\u8217\'20\'19s good to work on policy and within the system, but at the same time, we got us. That really influenced the work I do now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She became more involved in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights around the time Mississippi was considering {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2011/nov/09/personhood-amendment-abortion-mississippi-rejected"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonhood amendment\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , which, had it passed, would have defined life as the moment of conception, but was rejected by voters. Involved with the local branch of the \uc2 National Organization for Women (NOW)\uc2 , she was the only person who would go on television to talk about the incendiary issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of people had jobs to lose,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. Bertram Roberts was unable to work because of chronic health problems. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t fire me from being disabled,\u8221\'20\'1D she says with a laugh. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat are you going to do? Fire me from being a caretaker to my kids?\u8221\'20\'1D Funny and forceful, she is a natural communicator, and didn\u8217\'20\'19t go in for euphemisms such as \u8220\'20\'1Creproductive health\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ! Say the word, otherwise a lot of times people don\u8217\'20\'19t even know what you\u8217\'20\'19re talking about. So that became my job. I became the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lady of Mississippi.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 By 2013, she was escorting women to clinics. What was that like? She pauses. \u8220\'20\'1CI won\u8217\'20\'19t say I wasn\u8217\'20\'19t ready, because I knew what they [the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters outside clinics] were going to be like, but I don\u8217\'20\'19t think I expected them to be so personal. It was like they did research on me \u8211\'20\'13 they knew how many kids I had, they knew when I had them.\u8221\'20\'1D Her teenage daughters would go with her and Bertram Roberts remembers protesters calling them \u8220\'20\'1Cjezebels\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CLike, who does that?\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CThey would come up to [my daughter] and be like, \u8216\'20\'18how many abortions have you had to be so eager to kill black children?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Bertram Roberts wasn\u8217\'20\'19t, she says with a wry smile, \u8220\'20\'1Cready for them to lecture me about black history. The audacity of these white people!\u8221\'20\'1D They would tell her that one day she would be on trial \u8220\'20\'1Cjust like Nazis. \u8216\'20\'18OK, thank you, people who think The Handmaid\u8217\'20\'19s Tale is a fanfic. Please get the fuck out of my way.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D She gets abusive emails now, including one this week that ended by saying her mother should have aborted her. She laughs and says, \u8220\'20\'1Cthere\u8217\'20\'19s nothing like being so anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that you think I should be aborted.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bertram Roberts was meeting women who were sometimes a few dollars short, sometimes more, who couldn\u8217\'20\'19t pay for their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and so she would scramble to raise money. At first funding came through NOW, then in 2015 she established MRFF as a separate organisation. \u8220\'20\'1CIt really came out of seeing people who didn\u8217\'20\'19t have a taxi fare, who came hungry. The need is so great.\u8221\'20\'1D Sometimes accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would mean travelling for hours, and women would tell Bertram Roberts they had food for the trip, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut really, what they mean is they\u8217\'20\'19re going to buy a box of cereal on their food stamps\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The reputation of MRFF grew; at one point, she says, it was getting 100 calls a week. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s just no way to get back to that many people, and we were out of money.\u8221\'20\'1D It had to close down for a few months to cope with the backlog. Now the MRFF gets about 20 calls a week, \u8220\'20\'1Cand we\u8217\'20\'19re able to help about half of those\u8221\'20\'1D. (Yellowhammer receives 50 to 60 calls a week.)\par \par \uc2 The majority of women who access the services, she says, \u8220\'20\'1Care low-income or working class and already have kids. They understand the decision that they\u8217\'20\'19re making. And some folks just don\u8217\'20\'19t want to have kids.\u8221\'20\'1D They have included women whose sterilisation surgery has failed. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople who were using contraception and it failed, people who had their contraception sabotaged, young people who are preyed upon or assaulted, grandmas who thought they were in menopause. People who get abortions are the people you know. That\u8217\'20\'19s what I\u8217\'20\'19ve learned doing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\u8221\'20\'1D \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she says, is simply part of wider reproductive healthcare. \u8220\'20\'1CYou never know when a pregnancy that is going as planned will turn into a pregnancy where you need \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. The bravado that some people have to think, \u8216\'20\'18It could never be me \u8230\'20\'26\u8217\'20\'19 I didn\u8217\'20\'19t choose to need an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 17. I was a fully pro-life, never-have-an-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  person and then almost died because no one would give me one.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, she says, nobody should have to feel like a choice was made for them. \u8220\'20\'1CYou should have support whether you choose to parent, or have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D She smiles, clearly frustrated by the fight but ready for it. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t see what the issue is. What is so hard about this?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Donate to to support reproductive justice in Alabama via the Yellowhammer Fund {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://action.yellowhammerfund.org/onlineactions/VJwyf79UF0yW0qhFnyakGw2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220512ei5c001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
