\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Democrats lose Senate vote to codify  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights into federal law\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Gambino in Washington \par \uc2 1,221 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par 09:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 28\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Final tally was 49-51, with all Republicans and one conservative Democrat, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , voting against the measure\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Senate\uc2  on Wednesday failed to advance legislation that would codify the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into federal law, after it was blocked by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 It was a largely symbolic vote by Democrats to mobilize Americans around the issue ahead of a likely supreme court decision striking down the protections enshrined by Roe v Wade.\par \par \uc2 The Senate roll call was a stark reflection of the partisan divide over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with all Republicans and one conservative Democrat, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia, voting against the measure. The final tally was 49-51, well short of the 60 votes necessary to overcome a filibuster in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSadly the Senate failed to stand in defense of a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to make decisions about her own body,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the first woman and woman of color to serve as vice-president, told reporters outside of the Senate chamber, where she presided over the doomed vote. Pointing to the onslaught of laws restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Republican-led states, Harris said that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe priority should be to elect pro-choice leaders at the local, the state and the federal level\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who has called on the Democratic-controlled Congress to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, blamed Republicans, saying they \u8220\'20\'1Chave chosen to stand in the way of Americans\u8217\'20\'19 rights to make the most personal decisions about their own bodies, families and lives\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The president vowed to sign any bill that would secure reproductive rights and pledged to explore ways his administration could act in lieu of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Democrats moved quickly to stage the vote after a leak last week of a draft opinion, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  in February and confirmed as authentic, indicated that the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservative majority had privately voted to strike down Roe and subsequent rulings. The extraordinary disclosure ignited protests around the country, pushing reproductive rights to the center of the political debate six months before the congressional midterms. A final ruling from the court is expected this summer.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of the vote, a group of House Democratic women marched across the Capitol to protest against the end of Roe, chanting: \u8220\'20\'1CMy body, my decision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Democrats, under intensifying pressure to act, saw a political opportunity in forcing Republicans to vote against a bill protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a moment when the threat to access is urgent and polls show a majority of Americans want the procedure to remain legal in all or some cases.\par \par \uc2 They hope to use the Republican blockade as a data point in their midterm message to voters: that the GOP has become a party of \u8220\'20\'1Cultra-Maga\u8221\'20\'1D extremists, on the cusp of fulfilling a decades-long goal to strip women of their reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 It is an issue Democrats hope will energize young voters disenchanted by the Biden administration and persuade Republican-leaning suburban women to back them again this cycle.\par \par \uc2 The Senate majority leader, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , urging support for the measure, warned that a failure to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access would put at risk other individual rights like contraception and same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we do not take a stand now to protect a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose, then mark my words, it will be open season, open season on our God-given freedoms,\u8221\'20\'1D Schumer said in a floor speech ahead of a vote that he called \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the most consequential we will take in decades\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 If passed, the bill would have codified Roe v Wade into federal law, ensuring the right of healthcare providers to perform abortions and the right of patients to receive them. But it would also go further, in some cases invalidating state-level restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access enacted after the Roe decision in 1973.\par \par \uc2 As such, Republicans cast the bill as a \u8220\'20\'1Cradical\u8221\'20\'1D attempt to expand reproductive rights that goes far beyond Roe and would legalize \u8220\'20\'1C\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  on demand\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will stand with the American people, stand with innocent life, and block the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 extreme bill,\u8221\'20\'1D the Senate minority leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are betting the economy will take precedence over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this November. Polling shows Republicans are well positioned to make significant gains in the midterm elections, buoyed by historical headwinds, discontent with the party in power and widespread concern over the rising cost of gas, food and rent.\par \par \uc2 But there are signs that Republicans do worry about a potential political backlash if Roe is overturned and states move swiftly to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as many are preparing to do.\par \par \uc2 A day ahead of the vote, McConnell, who effectively secured a conservative super-majority on the supreme court, sought to downplay calls for a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if they take control of the chamber in November, telling reporters: \u8220\'20\'1CHistorically, there have been \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  votes on the floor of the Senate. None of them have achieved 60 votes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The two Senate Republicans who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  of Maine and Lisa Murkowski of Alaska, opposed the bill, instead urging support for an alternative measure that they say is tailored to reflect the landscape of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But many Democrats see their proposal, which is not expected to receive a vote, as too weak.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnlike some far-left activists, Senator Murkowski and I want the law today to be the law tomorrow,\u8221\'20\'1D Collins said on Wednesday, objecting to the lack of protections for religious exemptions in the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 bill.\par \par \uc2 In a dramatic shift, one of the only other Democrats in Congress with conservative views on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Bob Casey of Pennsylvania, said he would support the measure and voted in favor of advancing it. In a statement citing the leaked supreme court ruling, Casey said the \u8220\'20\'1Ccircumstances around the entire debate on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D had changed since the last time the Senate voted on the measure.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also likely to be a major issue in races for governor and state offices, as the battle lines shift to the states, where Democrats are racing to secure and expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and Republicans are working to limit, or in some cases, outlaw it.\par \par \uc2 The show vote on Wednesday only intensified calls from progressives for Democrats to eliminate the filibuster or carve out an exception for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. But at this point, there are not 60 votes to codify Roe, and there is not enough Democratic support to eliminate or amend the Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Without a clear legislative path forward, Democrats are turning to the fall elections, urging Americans to elect them as the \u8220\'20\'1Clast lines of defense\u8221\'20\'1D against the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI am angry and I\u8217\'20\'19m disappointed,\u8221\'20\'1D Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , a Democrat from Washington and a long-time advocate of reproductive rights, said after Wednesday\u8217\'20\'19s vote. She urged Americans concerned over the threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to not lose hope.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow is not the time to back down or sit down,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the time to lift up our voices and fight back. And that\u8217\'20\'19s exactly what we\u8217\'20\'19re going to do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This article was amended on 12 May 2022. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  serves as a senator for West Virginia, not Virginia as an earlier version said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220511ei5b0033m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
