\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The second American civil war is already happening\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Reich \par \uc2 1,135 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par 09:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 America will still be America. But it is fast becoming two versions of itself. The open question is: how will the two be civil toward each other?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s upcoming decision to reverse Roe v Wade (an early draft of which was leaked last week) doesn\u8217\'20\'19t ban abortions; it leaves the issue to the states. As a result, it will put another large brick in the growing wall separating blue and red America.\par \par \uc2 The second American civil war is already occurring, but it is less of a war than a kind of benign separation analogous to unhappily married people who don\u8217\'20\'19t want to go through the trauma of a formal divorce.\par \par \uc2 One America is largely urban, racially and ethnically diverse, and young. The other is largely rural or exurban, white and older.\par \par \uc2 The split is accelerating. Red zip codes are getting redder and blue zip codes, bluer. Of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s total 3,143 counties, the number of super landslide counties \u8211\'20\'13 where a presidential candidate won at least 80% of the vote \u8211\'20\'13 jumped from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://centerforpolitics.org/crystalball/articles/the-big-sort-continues-with-trump-as-a-driving-force/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 6% in 2004 to 22% in 2020}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Surveys show Americans find it increasingly important to live around {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/02/18/1081295373/the-big-sort-americans-move-to-areas-political-alignment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 people who share their political values}}}\uc2 . Animosity toward those in the opposing party is higher than at any time in living memory. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/03/13/opinion/hate-politics.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Forty-two per cent}}}\uc2  of registered voters believe Americans in the other party are \u8220\'20\'1Cdownright evil\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Almost {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://today.yougov.com/topics/politics/articles-reports/2020/09/17/republicans-democrats-marriage-poll"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 40%}}}\uc2  would be upset at the prospect of their child marrying someone from the opposite party. Even before the 2020 election, when asked if violence would be justified if the other party won the election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dannyhayes.org/uploads/6/9/8/5/69858539/kalmoe___mason_ncapsa_2019_-_lethal_partisanship_-_final_lmedit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 18.3%}}}\uc2  of Democrats and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dannyhayes.org/uploads/6/9/8/5/69858539/kalmoe___mason_ncapsa_2019_-_lethal_partisanship_-_final_lmedit.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 13.8%}}}\uc2  of Republicans responded in the affirmative.\par \par \uc2 Increasingly, each America is running under different laws.\par \par \uc2 Red states are making it nearly impossible to get abortions but easier than ever to buy guns.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re also suppressing votes. (In Florida and Texas, teams of \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/17/us/politics/republican-voter-fraud.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 election police}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D have been created to crack down on the rare crime of voter fraud, another fallout from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s big lie.)\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re banning the teaching of America\u8217\'20\'19s history of racism. They\u8217\'20\'19re requiring transgender students to use bathrooms and join sports teams that reflect their gender at birth.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re making it harder to protest; more difficult to qualify for unemployment benefits or other forms of public assistance; and almost impossible to form labor unions.\par \par \uc2 And they\u8217\'20\'19re passing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/02/22/texas-abortion-law-vigilante-loophole-supreme-court/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cbounty\u8221\'20\'1D laws}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 enforced not by governments, which can be sued in federal court, but by rewards to private citizens for filing lawsuits \u8211\'20\'13 on issues ranging from classroom speech to abortions to vaccinations.\par \par \uc2 Blue states are moving in the opposite direction. Several, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://leg.colorado.gov/bills/hb22-1279"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Colorado}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vpr.org/show/vermont-edition/2022-02-11/vermonters-will-vote-on-adding-abortion-rights-to-the-constitution-this-fall-what-would-prop-5-do"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vermont}}}\uc2 , are codifying a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Some are helping cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expenses for out-of-staters.\par \par \uc2 When Idaho proposed a ban on abortions that empowers relatives to sue anyone who helps terminate a pregnancy after six weeks, nearby {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oregonlive.com/politics/2022/03/oregon-invests-15-million-to-prepare-for-fallout-of-idaho-anti-abortion-legislation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oregon}}}\uc2  approved $15m to help cover the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  expenses of patients from other states.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.baltimoresun.com/politics/bs-md-pol-abortion-access-20220330-viqzxzhl5bfrbgqhoxndtvvnyq-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/inslee-abortions-lawsuit-e9a59f7c-3720-4ac0-9a34-43de6400d930.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington}}}\uc2  have expanded access and legal protections to out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/08/us/california-abortion-sanctuary-state.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 One package of pending California bills}}}\uc2  would expand access to California abortions and protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers from out-of-state legal action.\par \par \uc2 After the governor of Texas ordered state agencies to investigate parents for child abuse if they provide certain medical treatments to their transgender children, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/business-health-texas-california-sacramento-ec677d6b6c4807235a1d44c304d5a25a?campaign_id=49&emc=edit_ca_20220329&instance_id=57023&nl=california-today&regi_id=129667017&segment_id=86884&te=1&user_id=b28558b29a7ba478a6a9382d773ed7e0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2  lawmakers proposed making the state a refuge for transgender youths and their families.\par \par \uc2 Another California proposal would thwart enforcement of out-of-state court judgments removing children from the custody of parents who get them gender-affirming health services.\par \par \uc2 California is also about to enforce a ban on ghost guns and assault weapons with a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/12/12/us/politics/newsom-texas-abortion-law-guns.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California version}}}\uc2  of Texas\u8217\'20\'19 recent six-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , featuring $10,000 bounties to encourage lawsuits from private citizens against anyone who sells, distributes or manufactures those types of firearms.\par \par \uc2 Blue states are also coordinating more of their policies. During the pandemic, blue states joined together on policies that red states rejected \u8211\'20\'13 such as purchasing agreements for personal protective equipment, strategies for reopening businesses as Covid subsided, even on travel from other states with high levels of Covid.\par \par \uc2 At one point, New York, New Jersey and Connecticut required travelers from states with high positivity rates \u8211\'20\'13 Arkansas, Florida, North and South Carolina, Texas and Utah \u8211\'20\'13 to quarantine for two weeks before entering.\par \par \uc2 But what will happen to the poor in red states, who are disproportionately people of color?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStates rights\u8221\'20\'1D was always a cover for segregation and harsh discrimination. The poor \u8211\'20\'13 both white and people of color \u8211\'20\'13 are already especially burdened by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation because they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford travel to a blue state to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re also hurt by the failure of red states to expand Medicaid eligibility under the Affordable Care Act; by red state de facto segregation in public schools; and by red state measures to suppress votes.\par \par \uc2 One answer is for Democratic administrations and congresses in Washington to prioritize the needs of the red state poor and make extra efforts to protect the civil and political rights of people of color in red states. The failure of the Senate to muster enough votes to pass the Freedom to Vote Act, let alone revive the Voting Rights Act, suggests how difficult this will be.\par \par \uc2 Blue states have a potential role here. They should spend additional resources on the needs of red state residents, such as Oregon is now doing for people from outside Oregon who seek abortions.\par \par \uc2 They should prohibit state funds from being spent in any state that bans abortions or discriminates on the basis of race, ethnicity, or gender. California already bars anyone on a state payroll (including yours truly, who teaches at \uc2 UC Berkeley\uc2 ) from getting reimbursed for travel to states that discriminate against LGBTQ+ people.\par \par \uc2 Where will all this end? Not with two separate nations. What America is going through is analogous to Brexit \u8211\'20\'13 a lumbering, mutual decision to go separate ways on most things but remain connected on a few big things (such as national defense, monetary policy and civil and political rights).\par \par \uc2 America will still be America. But it is fast becoming two versions of America. The open question is like the one faced by every couple that separates: how will the two find ways to be civil toward each other?\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Robert Reich, a former US secretary of labor, is professor of public policy at the \uc2 University of California at Berkeley\uc2  and the author of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/saving-capitalism-for-the-many-not-the-few/9780345806222"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Saving Capitalism: For the Many, Not the Few}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-common-good-9780525436379/9780525436379"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Common Good}}}\uc2 . His new book, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bookshop.org/books/the-system-who-rigged-it-how-we-fix-it/9780525659044"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The System: Who Rigged It, How We Fix It,}}}\uc2  is out now. He is a Guardian US columnist. His newsletter is at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://robertreich.substack.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 robertreich.substack.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220511ei5b001h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
