\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Pro-choice states rush to pledge legal shield for out-of-state abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 Maya Yang \par \uc2 1,569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par 08:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Impending end to legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in half the states will strain capacity and resources in states preserving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access\par \par \uc2 After the bombshell leak of a supreme court draft opinion last week revealed that the majority of justices plan to overturn Roe v Wade, a handful of states have been reaffirming and expanding their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 In recent days, a flurry of bills and statements have been emerging in largely Democratic-run states as lawmakers and organizations scramble to prepare for the possibility of a post-Roe America.\par \par \uc2 As some states seek to become \u8220\'20\'1Csafe havens\u8221\'20\'1D for out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers, other states have been increasing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funding, expanding provider access and offering legal protections from lawsuits launched by citizen \u8220\'20\'1Cbounty-hunters\u8221\'20\'1D against those involved in abortions.\par \par \uc2 Only 38% of women of reproductive age live in states that have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2019/08/state-abortion-policy-landscape-hostile-supportive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  shown}}}\uc2  support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, according to the research organization \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 . In contrast, 58% live in states that have demonstrated hostility towards \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Only 4% of women live in middle-ground states.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the New York state senator \uc2 Liz Krueger\uc2  introduced a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2021/S8778"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  bill}}}\uc2  that would shield New York doctors who offer \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services to out-of-state patients by prohibiting law enforcement from cooperating with out-of-state investigations on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provisions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt this critical moment New York must ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access both to New Yorkers and refugees from other states who are being denied their basic rights,\u8221\'20\'1D Krueger told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe must provide iron-clad protection for New York healthcare providers against abhorrent and regressive laws in other states,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, \uc2 Alessandra Biaggi\uc2 , another New York state senator, introduced a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysenate.gov/newsroom/press-releases/alessandra-biaggi/senator-biaggi-planned-parenthood-brigid-alliance-and"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  bill}}}\uc2  that would create an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access fund and allow taxpayers to contribute to it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe created this bill to make sure that we were not just saying that people have a right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but also that they have access to it because we know that simply by just saying the right does not mean that people have the means to do it,\u8221\'20\'1D Biaggi told the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 In addition to transportation costs, the fund would cover childcare services, lodging, various medical expenses and other logistical support.\par \par \uc2 Biaggi also introduced another {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2021/S9039"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  bill}}}\uc2  that would offer out-of-state women who seek \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or gender-affirming services the claim of \u8220\'20\'1Cunlawful interference\u8221\'20\'1D with their rights if they end up facing criminal or civil complaints.\par \par \uc2 In Connecticut, Governor \uc2 Ned Lamont\uc2  signed a bill into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://portal.ct.gov/Office-of-the-Governor/News/Press-Releases/2022/05-2022/Governor-Lamont-Reaffirms-Commitment-To-Signing-Recently-Approved-Reproductive-Rights-Legislation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  law}}}\uc2  that will protect people who provide or receive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in Connecticut and are then sued in another state.\par \par \uc2 The first-in-the-nation law, which is set to take effect on 1 July, will also improve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in Connecticut by expanding the type of practitioners eligible to perform certain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related care.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to make sure that we have the healthcare infrastructure to provide for not only our own residents, but also the folks who are going to be coming here for care,\u8221\'20\'1D state representative Matt Blumenthal, who co-sponsored the bill, said.\par \par \uc2 As Connecticut is poised to become one of the country\u8217\'20\'19s few safe havens, lawmakers like Blumenthal are well aware of the challenges that will come with an uptick in out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA possible influx in out-of-state patients \u8230\'20\'26 is definitely a concern. We\u8217\'20\'19ve already seen individuals coming from Texas to get \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care here in Connecticut and that was one of the reasons we wanted to expand the providers eligible to give the care,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Blumenthal added, \u8220\'20\'1CWe got important sources of funding in our budget this year to protect reproductive healthcare in the state and that\u8217\'20\'19s very important because you know the people who are going to be affected by these bans in other states are going to be disproportionately people of low income, people of color who can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to travel freely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another state that has been weaving additional \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care funds into its laws is Oregon. In February, the state legislature {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oregon.gov/oha/PH/HEALTHYPEOPLEFAMILIES/REPRODUCTIVESEXUALHEALTH/Pages/reproductive-health-equity-act.aspx#:~:text=The%20Reproductive%20Health%20Equity%20Act%20ensures%20that%20people%20with%20Oregon,with%20the%20Affordable%20Care%20Act."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  established}}}\uc2  the Reproductive Health Equity Fund which will allocate $15m to the non-profit Seeding Justice for expanded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Seeding Justice has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seedingjustice.org/reproductive-health-equity-fund/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  described}}}\uc2  the fund, which will help Oregon brace for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in neighboring Idaho, as a \u8220\'20\'1Conce-in-a-lifetime opportunity to invest in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access as a central part of Oregon\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare infrastructure\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese funds are meant to be holistic \u8230\'20\'26Those funds are really focused on patients get[ting] care and extending provider capacity,\u8221\'20\'1D An Do, executive director of Planned Parenthood Advocates of Oregon, said.\par \par \uc2 Do continued, \u8220\'20\'1CWithin Oregon, we know that we need to be ramping up our healthcare infrastructure \u8230\'20\'26 and shrinking the access desert \u8230\'20\'26 so I think that is a big place to be able to make investments.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 California has similarly positioned itself as \u8220\'20\'1Ca refuge state\u8221\'20\'1D of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. In January, the state legislature introduced a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://healthlaw.org/news/california-future-of-abortion-council-announces-support-for-package-of-legislation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  package}}}\uc2  of 13 bills to designate California as a sanctuary for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 In addition to offering more legal protection to out-of-state patients and providers\u8217\'20\'19 privacy, the bills seek to expand the number of providers that are able to perform \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 One bill seeks to remove co-pays, deductibles and other cost-sharing requirements for abortions for all state-licensed health care service plans that are issued after 2022. Another bill would deny out-of-state subpoenas for patient information on reproductive health services that are granted in California.\par \par \uc2 Other bills seek to protect California \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers by prohibiting the removal or suspension of medical licenses for a California licensee providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care who is complying with state law.\par \par \uc2 In Colorado, lawmakers are also rushing to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access protections. Colorado is one of the few states that does not require a 72-hour waiting period after \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  counseling. It also does not have any restrictions surrounding when in a pregnancy an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  can take place.\par \par \uc2 In April, Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cpr.org/2022/05/02/if-roe-v-wade-really-is-really-overturned-what-happens-to-abortion-in-colorado/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  signed}}}\uc2  the Reproductive Health Equity Act, officially encoding the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  into state law.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdphe.colorado.gov/center-for-health-and-environmental-data/registries-and-vital-statistics/vital-statistics-program"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to}}}\uc2  the Colorado health department, 13% of the 10,400 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers who had the procedure in Colorado in 2020 were from out of state. As at least three of Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s immediate neighbors are certain or likely to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  if Roe v Wade gets overturned, the state is preparing for an uptick in out-of-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seekers, which may push Colorado residents further way to seek care.\par \par \uc2 Greer Donley, a law professor at the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2  who specializes in reproductive healthcare, told the Guardian, \u8220\'20\'1CPeople will feel the effects of Roe being overturned, even if their state continues to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provision.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe primary way they will see these effects is through the wait times \u8230\'20\'26 If half the states ban all or most abortions within the next few months, then the clinics in blue states will be inundated with patients traveling from out of state,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 As a result, Colorado clinics have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/local/denver/2022/05/04/colorado-abortion-providers-out-of-state-patients"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  vowed}}}\uc2  to do everything they can in order to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers as they struggle with worker shortages, lengthy wait times and post-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  complications.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCapacity is critical. We know that clinics in access states are scheduling patients in weeks rather than days and they have added more exam rooms by consolidating offices and waiting areas. And opening new facilities is difficult, as is finding staff, since we are in a healthcare worker shortage,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2 , \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s state policy expert.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor patients, helping them pay for the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related travel is key,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 In Maryland, lawmakers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/04/10/us/maryland-abortion-rights-veto.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  passed}}}\uc2  a bill last month that allows medical professionals other than physicians to perform abortions. The law will allow nurse practitioners, nurse midwives and trained physician assistants to carry out the procedure.\par \par \uc2 In addition, the law will require the state to invest $3.5m annually in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -care training and will require the majority of insurance providers in the state to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  of Michigan filed a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsfromthestates.com/article/whitmer-files-suit-enshrine-abortion-rights-asks-mich-supreme-court-take-case"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  lawsuit}}}\uc2  in a state court that seeks to strike down the state\u8217\'20\'19s 1931 law that criminalizes abortions unless they are performed to protect a woman\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit also seeks to establish the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state\u8217\'20\'19s constitution.\par \par \uc2 Despite the various steps that progressive states have taken to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, some still have certain restrictions in place that need to be removed to ensure \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is fully accessible.\par \par \uc2 In addition to capacity problems, another significant restriction is costs. \u8220\'20\'1CStates need to look at how they help people pay for abortions, as we know that most patients pay out-of-pocket for their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D Nash said.\par \par \uc2 With at least 26 states poised to, likely or certain to ban abortions if Roe v Wade is overturned, the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights is more urgent than ever.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t underscore how seismic a change this is,\u8221\'20\'1D said An Do. \u8220\'20\'1CWe never lost a constitutional right. This is just the beginning \u8230\'20\'26 They [the justices] have written us all out of the constitution. What\u8217\'20\'19s next is marriage equality, what\u8217\'20\'19s next is so many people\u8217\'20\'19s access to birth control.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot be apathetic and we cannot lose ourselves into despair. We need to fight tooth and nail.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220511ei5b001e8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
