\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How soon could US states outlaw abortions if Roe v Wade is overturned?\b0\par\par\uc2 Jessica Glenza \par \uc2 1,200 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par 12:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 If the draft supreme court decision is not substantially altered it would result in 26 states banning the procedure\par \par \uc2 An unprecedented leak of a draft supreme court decision shows a majority of justices support ending federal protections for abortions in arguably the most controversial court case in generations.\par \par \uc2 While the draft could still change, if it is not substantially altered it would result in 26 states immediately, or as soon as practicable, banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a sea change in the American legal and political landscape.\par \par \uc2 Why would half of the US outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 If the draft decision remains substantially unchanged, it would return the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the states, 26 of which stand poised to ban or greatly restrict it. Until the court issues a final decision, the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is protected under federal law.\par \par \uc2 That right was established in the landmark 1973 case Roe v Wade. Roe found pregnant people have a constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy up to the point a fetus can survive outside the womb, roughly considered 24 weeks gestation, and a legal principle called \u8220\'20\'1Cviability\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The court\u8217\'20\'19s finding in Roe invalidated dozens of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, and made it illegal for states to outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  before viability. The case that was the subject of the leak on Monday, called {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organization}}}\uc2 , considered a Mississippi law that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks. Even though this is not a total ban, it strikes at the heart of the holding that established a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Do Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/supreme-court-poised-reverse-roe-americans-support-abortion/story?id=84468131"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent poll}}}\uc2  found 70% of Americans think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a choice that should be left to a woman and her doctor, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/1576/abortion.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 polling over time}}}\uc2  has shown support for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has changed little since Roe v Wade was decided. Only a small minority think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be completely illegal.\par \par \uc2 If most Americans support the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , can Congress intervene?\par \par \uc2 The federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  hangs on a supreme court decision because, in the nearly 50 years since Roe v Wade was decided, congressional leaders failed to protect the right in statute.\par \par \uc2 While there are Democrat-led efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they are stalled in the Senate. That\u8217\'20\'19s because any new statute would need 60 votes to pass, and the 100-member Senate is evenly split. Democrats broadly support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, while Republicans almost universally oppose efforts to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats have proposed ending the 60-vote rule, called the filibuster, to move legislation on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other key items on Joe Biden agenda\u8217\'20\'19s forward. The president has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/11/joe-biden-to-back-filibuster-rule-change-to-push-voting-rights-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 endorsed such a change}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, two key Democratic senators, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , oppose such a change. It is unclear how the leaked opinion may change their position. Sinema has said she supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, while Manchin has issued {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hasanthehun/status/1521505888313352192?s=20&t=3xy9I9Uo4JxNsQa0OjbKvQ"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 conflicting messages}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Biden has called on Americans to elect more members of Congress who support reproductive rights in the upcoming midterm elections in November.\par \par \uc2 How soon would \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  become illegal?\par \par \uc2 If the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to return to the states, 26 would immediately or as soon as practicable ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 States would do so through a mix of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that were invalidated by Roe v Wade but remained on the books, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans \u8220\'20\'1Ctriggered\u8221\'20\'1D if Roe is overturned, and laws that limit the gestational age at which a person can terminate a pregnancy. All will probably be the subject of court challenges.\par \par \uc2 For example, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban enacted in 1931 could go back into effect in Michigan. There, the Republican-led legislature supports the ban, but the state\u8217\'20\'19s Democratic governor is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.clickondetroit.com/news/politics/2022/04/07/michigan-governor-sues-to-secure-abortion-rights-vacate-ban/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 challenging it in state court}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, the state legislature has enacted a \u8220\'20\'1Ctrigger\u8221\'20\'1D ban. There, the state attorney general would need to certify the central holding of Roe was indeed struck down. The attorney general, a Republican, would likely seek to do so quickly.\par \par \uc2 Trigger bans vary from state to state. While Arkansas requires an attorney general certification, Wyoming requires the governor to certify Roe has been overturned before the law goes into effect five days later.\par \par \uc2 Other bans, once blocked by the courts because of Roe, could also go into effect. Enforcement of a six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Iowa could go into effect. Because that is just two weeks after a pregnant person might miss a period, and before most people know they are pregnant, it is effectively a near-total ban.\par \par \uc2 In all cases, laws will probably be challenged by reproductive rights groups, and it will take time for cases to move through state and perhaps federal courts. Some experts have estimated it could take between six months to two years for most cases to be settled.\par \par \uc2 What is certain is in that time, Republican-led states would probably seek to push the envelope in terms of criminalizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and seek to enforce the bans they already have on the books.\par \par \uc2 How will people terminate pregnancies in states where it is banned?\par \par \uc2 Making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  illegal will not stop abortions. People who live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is outlawed but want to terminate a pregnancy may seek to travel to states where it remains legal, or obtain medication to end a pregnancy.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/07/us-mail-order-abortions-oklahoma"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Medication \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22 }}}\uc2  can safely end pregnancies up to 10 weeks gestation using a two-pill protocol. However, in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal, those pills would need to be obtained illicitly, perhaps through through the mail.\par \par \uc2 Patients who want to go to clinics, or whose pregnancy is too far along to be self-managed with medication, would need to travel potentially hundreds of miles to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal. That may be an impossible hurdle for women whose finances are strained or who cannot find childcare.\par \par \uc2 People who live in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal would also be impacted, because they would face {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fivethirtyeight.com/features/it-can-already-take-weeks-to-get-an-abortion/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longer wait times}}}\uc2  for appointments as patients flood into clinics from out-of-state. This could create a secondary wave of travel.\par \par \uc2 Even more broadly, outlawing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would substantially undermine care for basic obstetric procedures, such as miscarriage and ectopic pregnancies, in a country which already has among the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/issue-briefs/2020/nov/maternal-mortality-maternity-care-us-compared-10-countries"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest maternal mortality rates}}}\uc2  in the developed world.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://bixbycenter.ucsf.edu/news/almost-half-us-ob-gyn-residents-won%E2%80%99t-get-abortion-training-if-roe-v-wade-overturned"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nearly half}}}\uc2  (44%) of all future obstetricians and gynecologists are trained in states that would outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , making it impossible to train all new doctors in the skills needed to manage induced or spontaneous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Even more {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/nov/29/us-abortion-supreme-court-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 doctors may be frightened}}}\uc2  to provide evidence-based care to women who face life-threatening complications, if their condition is not imminently emergent \u8211\'20\'13 but may become so.\par \par \uc2 How are liberal states responding?\par \par \uc2 Some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/04/blue-states-abortion-rights-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Democrat-led states}}}\uc2 , such as Oregon, are working to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by providing funds for people who travel to their states to obtain abortions. Others, such as California, are working to build capacity for the thousands of woman who could suddenly find the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic there.\par \par \uc2 But in all cases, these efforts will be incomplete. Not all people will be able to travel or obtain medication abortions, and may forced to carry an unwanted pregnancy to term.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220504ei54000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
