\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The strongest protection a state could give\u8217\'20\'19: how Delaware is improving access to  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Melody Schreiber \par \uc2 1,545 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state has eased barriers to getting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills \u8211\'20\'13 and has significantly reduced the rate of unplanned pregnancies\par \par \uc2 When patients came to Kelly Nichols, a nurse practitioner at a clinic in Newark, Delaware, needing care for their miscarriages, Nichols often prescribed medications to help with the process.\par \par \uc2 But if patients came to Nichols seeking to end a pregnancy, she was not able to prescribe them the exact same medications.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was just purely a political block on it,\u8221\'20\'1D Nichols said, that had nothing to do with health or safety \u8211\'20\'13 medication abortions, which now {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/article/2022/02/medication-abortion-now-accounts-more-half-all-us-abortions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 account for the majority}}}\uc2  of abortions in the US, have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanam/article/PIIS2667-193X(22)00017-5/fulltext"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 shown}}}\uc2  to be very safe.\par \par \uc2 Instead, her patients seeking medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  need to schedule a second appointment with a state-certified physician at a different facility, a time-consuming and sometimes arduous process, especially for those who weren\u8217\'20\'19t able to take more time off from work or travel to a different facility.\par \par \uc2 But all of that is now changing. A new law allows physician assistants and advanced practice registered nurses, who are already able to prescribe many medications, to prescribe the medication for abortions as well. Democratic governor \uc2 John Carney\uc2  signed the legislation into law last week.\par \par \uc2 The law will increase the number of providers in Delaware and make the process of getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  less complicated. Medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , also known as the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, can be used safely up to 10 weeks into a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 The new law is one of several ways \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other forms of reproductive health care are becoming more accessible in Delaware, even as restrictions and bans loom in other states across the country.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Known as the First State for being the first to ratify the US constitution, Delaware was also the first in the wake of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election to codify Roe into state law in 2017, protecting the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  even if the landmark supreme court decision is overturned.\par \par \uc2 The 2017 law ensures access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until viability, defined at 20 weeks of pregnancy in Delaware. Procedures are also permitted later if the patient\u8217\'20\'19s life or health is threatened or if there\u8217\'20\'19s a serious fetal anomaly.\par \par \uc2 Codifying \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections into state law \u8220\'20\'1Creally is the strongest protection that a state could give,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 John Culhane\uc2 , professor of law at Widener University Delaware Law School.\par \par \uc2 And on 1 December, the same day the court heard arguments on the 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in Mississippi that could overturn Roe, the first Planned Parenthood clinic in a decade opened in Delaware\u8217\'20\'19s most rural county.\par \par \uc2 Delaware is one of several blue states introducing new laws and planning to increase services as the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the US looks to be in peril. Other states looking to expand access include {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/politics/2022/02/08/abortion-roe-vermont/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vermont}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nysenate.gov/legislation/bills/2021/s7002"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/california-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade-scotus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 California}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2022/05/04/maryland-abortion-funding-governor-hogan/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Maryland}}}\uc2 . {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.forbes.com/sites/alisondurkee/2022/03/17/washington-enacts-law-blocking-texas-style-abortion-ban-as-more-states-copy-texas-law/?sh=6122f61c21b9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Washington}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://portal.ct.gov/Office-of-the-Governor/News/Press-Releases/2022/05-2022/Watch-Governor-Lamont-Signs-Reproductive-Rights-Legislation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Connecticut}}}\uc2  have also passed laws to extend protections to providers aiding in the abortions of people who travel from out of state.\par \par \uc2 With the overturn of Roe, about 16 states will be able to continue providing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services without restriction or with minor restrictions, said Ruth Lytle-Barnaby, president and CEO of Planned Parenthood of Delaware, which runs the clinic where Nichols works.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose of us that are left are going to have to absolutely gear up to be able to see a lot more people,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. The new law on medication prescription \u8220\'20\'1Copens up more access for more patients, because we will be able to expand the number of providers providing this care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The Delaware expansions are not without opposition. Although the state reliably goes blue, the southern part is more rural and conservative.\par \par \uc2 News of the new clinic in the southern town of Seaford, for instance, was greeted with protests and local restrictions before it even opened.\par \par \uc2 Seaford, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seafordde.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 describes}}}\uc2  itself as a \u8220\'20\'1Ctraditional\u8221\'20\'1D community, is an industrial town home to less than 8,000 people. It\u8217\'20\'19s nestled in the most rural part of the state, where chickens tend to outnumber people \u8211\'20\'13 and Trump signs still hang, even in the state \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  represented in Congress for decades.\par \par \uc2 Up north, the populous New Castle county always goes deep blue, pulling the rest of the state with it. But here in Sussex county, the political map is red, and residents tend to hold more conservative views.\par \par \uc2 In December, after news of the new clinic, the Seaford city council passed an ordinance requiring fetal tissue from miscarriage or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be buried or cremated, a cost to be borne by patients or providers.\par \par \uc2 According to Delaware law, however, fetal tissue prior to 20 weeks of gestation is not considered human remains, and a death certificate \u8211\'20\'13 which is required for cremation or burial \u8211\'20\'13 is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.delawareonline.com/story/news/local/2022/01/11/historical-suit-delaware-ag-sues-seaford-over-fetal-remains-ordinance-abortion-rights/9006253002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not issued}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In January, the state\u8217\'20\'19s justice department took the unusual step of suing the city because of the ordinance\u8217\'20\'19s conflicts with state legislation on handling fetal tissue. It would have also added prohibitive costs, opponents say.\par \par \uc2 For now, this ordinance is blocked, and the Seaford clinic opened without the restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Delaware\u8217\'20\'19s intrastate struggles are \u8220\'20\'1Ca microcosm of the sorts of things we can expect to see in states that continue to allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, where there\u8217\'20\'19s resistance to that in certain localities\u8221\'20\'1D, Culhane said.\par \par \uc2 Every year, Delaware state legislators introduce at least one bill to restrict reproductive health, Lytle-Barnaby said. \u8220\'20\'1CEven in the progressive states, we have to re-litigate this every single year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 And even in states that do not have restrictive laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , there are limits on receiving care. There may be areas with no providers for miles around, and it can be difficult for some patients to book appointments and take off work to travel within or outside the state.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDelaware has three counties, and they\u8217\'20\'19re all classified as medically underserved,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lytle-Barnaby. The average wait time for any patient to see a new doctor is 28 days for Sussex county and 32 days for Kent county, she said.\par \par \uc2 Sussex county is also growing the fastest, with a 23% increase in the past decade, Lytle-Barnaby said \u8211\'20\'13 and at the same time, the county has the highest rates of uninsurance and child poverty in the state.\par \par \uc2 The last clinic in Sussex county, located in Rehoboth Beach, closed in 2011, and the clinic in nearby Salisbury closed in 2015.\par \par \uc2 The location in Seaford was chosen, in part, to be more accessible to patients from outside the state as well, since those areas also have poor access to care and high poverty rates.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 Delaware is taking other steps to strengthen reproductive health. A program on contraceptives has proven a model for other states to reduce unintended pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 Long-term contraceptives, like IUDs and implants, are very effective at preventing pregnancies. But it can be difficult to find providers who offer this type of birth control, and some people may not know they even exist.\par \par \uc2 DelCAN, which started in 2014, helps smooth the process \u8211\'20\'13 training providers and making these forms of contraception easy to access and covered by insurance.\par \par \uc2 Medical providers ask a simple question at health visits: \u8220\'20\'1CDo you want to get pregnant in the next year?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If the answer is yes, they talk about prenatal vitamins and health care. If it\u8217\'20\'19s no, they talk about \u8220\'20\'1Cset it and forget it\u8221\'20\'1D contraceptives that are more effective than methods like the pill or condoms.\par \par \uc2 When it started, Delaware had one of the highest rates of unintended pregnancies in the nation, but that rate fell by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://upstream.org/blog/2021/10/06/delaware-unintended-pregnancy-rate-declines-more-than-2x-compared-to-other-states/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 20%}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 twice as much as other states \u8211\'20\'13 after the introduction of the program.\par \par \uc2 Abortions in Delaware also declined by 37% between 2014 and 2017, according to a Guttmacher {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.guttmacher.org/report/abortion-incidence-service-availability-us-2017"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 States can go even further to shore up \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Delaware, for instance, still requires parental notification for patients under 16, and public funds like Medicaid can\u8217\'20\'19t cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in the cases of rape, incest or danger to the patient\u8217\'20\'19s life.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are some states where Medicaid funds \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s a health service,\u8221\'20\'1D Lytle-Barnaby said.\par \par \uc2 Expanding Medicaid to cover \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would also help people living in poverty, Culhane said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause a lot of the burden of these laws is going to fall on poor women\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oregonlive.com/politics/2022/03/oregon-invests-15-million-to-prepare-for-fallout-of-idaho-anti-abortion-legislation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bill}}}\uc2  in Oregon, introduced in the wake of neighboring Idaho\u8217\'20\'19s proposed restrictions, would provide $15min funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services, including for out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 States can also pass laws protecting providers if patients travel from states where abortions are banned, as Washington and Connecticut have done. Such protections may be necessary given the likelihood of states seeking to crack down on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/us-abortions-travel-wave-of-restrictions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 out-of-state travel for abortions}}}\uc2 , Lytle-Barnaby and Culhane said.\par \par \uc2 But amid all the politics, the health care Nichols provides is straightforward. The law now allowing her to prescribe medications for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  will make the care she offers easier and more accessible to patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CManagement of other pregnancy complications is really pretty much using the same exact skill set and knowledge and medications as provision of medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CSo it really just makes sense \u8211\'20\'13 it makes sense to be able to provide a continuity of care for the patients.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220510ei5a001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
