\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: Putin ties Ukraine invasion to second world war\b0\par\par\uc2 Nicola Slawson \par \uc2 1,409 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par 10:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Soldiers \u8216\'20\'18fighting for same thing their fathers and grandfathers did\u8217\'20\'19, says president in Victory Day speech. Plus, why film posters for comedies have huge red letters\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has told Russian soldiers they are \u8220\'20\'1Cfighting for the same thing their fathers and grandfathers did\u8221\'20\'1D as he used his Victory Day speech to tie the war in Ukraine to the memory of the second world war and justify his invasion.\par \par \uc2 Prior to the speech, foreign officials had said Putin could use it to launch a full mobilisation of Russian troops or formally declare war in Ukraine, but there were no large policy announcements.\par \par \uc2 Instead he suggested Russia was \u8220\'20\'1Cforced\u8221\'20\'1D into the war by Nato and pledged to provide aid for the families of soldiers who had died in what the Kremlin is calling a \u8220\'20\'1Cspecial operation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the 77th annual celebration of the defeat of Nazi Germany, the Russian president launched a defence of his war in Ukraine, pivoting from a recognition of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cgreatest generation\u8221\'20\'1D to a description of how it was believed Ukraine was being armed by the west for an imminent attack on Crimea, the peninsula annexed by Russia in 2014.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What did the \uc2 G7\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s statement marking the anniversary say? \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/08/putins-actions-in-ukraine-bring-shame-on-russia-says-g7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 brought shame on Russia}}}\uc2  and the sacrifices its people made to defeat Nazi Germany in the second world war, leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2  group of leading western economies said.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What else is happening? Here\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/09/russia-ukraine-war-what-we-know-on-day-75-of-the-invasion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 what we know on day 75}}}\uc2  of the invasion.\par \par \uc2 Capitol attack panel moves closer to issuing subpoenas to Republicans\par \par \uc2 Members on the House select committee investigating the Capitol attack on 6 January are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/09/capitol-attack-panel-subpoenas-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 moving closer to issuing subpoenas to Republican members of Congress}}}\uc2  to compel their cooperation in the inquiry \u8211\'20\'13 though it has started to dawn on them that they may be out of time.\par \par \uc2 The panel is expected to make a final decision on the subpoena question over the next couple of weeks, according to sources directly familiar with internal deliberations, with House investigators needing to start wrapping up their work before public hearings in June.\par \par \uc2 While the members on the committee remain undecided about whether to subpoena Republican members of Congress, their refusal to assist the investigation has caused the sentiment to turn towards taking that near-unprecedented action, the sources said.\par \par \uc2 The shifting view has come as a result of the dismay among the members in January, when the House minority leader, Kevin McCarthy, and others turned down requests for voluntary cooperation, turning to anger after three more of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s allies last week refused to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What has changed in recent weeks? Sources said the select committee\u8217\'20\'19s assessment can no longer ignore the deep involvement between some Republican members of Congress and the former president\u8217\'20\'19s unlawful schemes to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 Canada and Mexico prepare to accept Americans seeking abortions\par \par \uc2 In the years before Canada\u8217\'20\'19s supreme court legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1988, it was common for Canadians who needed abortions to travel to the US. \u8220\'20\'1CWe had a network of people who could make referrals and help them get there [to the US]. If it\u8217\'20\'19s necessary, that probably would happen again \u8211\'20\'13 but the other way,\u8221\'20\'1D said Carolyn Egan, spokesperson for the \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights Coalition of Canada.\par \par \uc2 If, as a leaked draft decision indicates, the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  votes to overturn Roe v Wade, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/09/canada-mexico-abortions-american"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 many Americans in need of surgical \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  could be forced to travel}}}\uc2  not just across state lines but, once again, across international borders, writes Hilary Beaumont .\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Canada\u8217\'20\'19s minister of families, \uc2 Karina Gould\uc2 , reaffirmed that Americans can access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services in Canada. \u8220\'20\'1CIf they, people, come here and need access, certainly, you know, that\u8217\'20\'19s a service that would be provided,\u8221\'20\'1D she told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbc.ca/news/politics/canada-provide-abortion-access-american-women-1.6440238"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CBC News}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 South of the US border, Mexican advocates are also preparing for an increase in Americans visiting to access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 How are GOP lawmakers prepping to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as soon as legally possible? Here is what Republican state lawmakers across the country are doing in the lead up to the decision to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/09/republican-lawmakers-abortion-ban-roe-v-wade-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 assure that \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  restrictions will swiftly go into effect}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 What about the Democrats? Senator Kirsten Gillibrand called the battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the US the \u8220\'20\'1Cbiggest fight of a generation\u8221\'20\'1D. The New York Democrat urged her party to stand up to Republicans seeking to abolish the constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The US first lady, Jill Biden, made an unannounced visit to western Ukraine on Sunday, holding a surprise Mother\u8217\'20\'19s Day meeting with the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first lady, Olena Zelenskiy. She traveled under the cloak of secrecy, becoming {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/08/jill-biden-visits-ukraine-meets-olena-zelenskiy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the latest high-profile American to enter Ukraine}}}\uc2  during the 10-week-old conflict.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Polls have opened in the Philippines as the country decides its next president in a polarising race between frontrunner \uc2 Ferdinand Marcos Jr\uc2 , the son of the late dictator, and a human rights lawyer who has vowed to tackle old, rotten politics. Opinion surveys suggest Marcos Jr, known as Bongbong, is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/09/philippines-presidential-election-2022-voting-begins-leni-robredo-ferdinand-bongbong-marcos-jr-polls-open-president"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poised to win}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The torsos of two statues of boxers, dating back to the iron age, have been discovered at the Mont\u8217\'20\'19e Prama necropolis in Sardinia. The latest finds, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/08/iron-age-boxer-statues-torsos-discovered-in-sardinian-necropolis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 sculpted in limestone by the Nuragic civilisation}}}\uc2 , add to several other statues of boxers, wrestlers and archers dug up at the site since the 1970s.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2022/may/09/new-york-times-drops-fetus-as-an-answer-to-wordle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 removed the word \u8220\'20\'1Cfetus\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  from its Wordle answers to keep the game \u8220\'20\'1Cdistinct from the news\u8221\'20\'1D, a move apparently related to last week\u8217\'20\'19s leaked Roe v Wade supreme court draft ruling. The change caused confusion as it was only implemented for some of the game\u8217\'20\'19s players.\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day: \u8216\'20\'18Forever chemicals\u8217\'20\'19 may have polluted 20m acres of US cropland, study says\par \par \uc2 About 20m acres of cropland in the United States may be contaminated from PFAS-tainted sewage sludge that has been used as fertilizer, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/may/08/us-cropland-may-be-contaminated-forever-chemicals-study"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a new report estimates}}}\uc2 . PFAS, or per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances, are a class of about 9,000 compounds used to make products heat-, water- or stain-resistant. Known as \u8220\'20\'1Cforever chemicals\u8221\'20\'1D because they don\u8217\'20\'19t naturally break down, they have been linked to cancer, thyroid disruption, liver problems, birth defects, immunosuppression and more.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: why do film posters for comedies always have huge red letters?\par \par \uc2 From The Nan Movie to Fun with Dick and Jane to American Pie to the Nutty Professor, ads for comedies nearly always follow one distinctive style. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/film/2022/may/09/scarlet-ohaha-film-posters-comedies-always-have-huge-red-letters-nan-fun-dick-jane"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The titles are big and they are red}}}\uc2 . But who started the trend for big red titles on empty backgrounds trend and why is it red that tickles our funny bones? Amelia Tait looked into it and found that the humble bus shelter may have the answer.\par \par \uc2 Climate check: The growing scandal of fish waste\par \par \uc2 Dumped at sea, lost on land or left to rot in shops and fridges, the global catch of fish is being wasted like never before \u8211\'20\'13 hurting not only the oceans but the nutrition of billions of people. Figures from WWF show that in 2019, at least 230,000 tonnes of fish were dumped in EU waters {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/may/09/millions-of-tonnes-of-dead-animals-the-growing-scandal-of-fish-waste"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 . But this figure is a fraction of an even larger global issue}}}\uc2 . The \uc2 UN Food and Agriculture Organization\uc2  (FAO) estimates 35% of all fish, crustaceans and molluscs harvested from oceans, lakes and fish farms are wasted or lost before they ever reach a plate.\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: Painting swapped in 70s for grilled cheese sandwich serves up windfall\par \par \uc2 Running a small restaurant in Ontario in the 1970s, Irene and Tony Demas soon learned the value of trading their dishes for the talents of local bakers, craftspeople and artisans. For an English painter with a predictable palate, the couple struck a deal: they would get paintings from him and his friends in exchange for grilled cheese sandwiches. By chance, that deal unwittingly netted them a painting by the acclaimed Canadian folk artist Maud Lewis \u8211\'20\'13 a work that nearly five decades later is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/08/painting-swapped-grilled-cheese-sandwich-canada-maud-lewis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expected to net more than C$35,000 (US$27,000)}}}\uc2  when it goes to auction this month.\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220509ei59001e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
