\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 British scientist says US anti- \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  lawyers misused his work to attack Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Anna Fazackerley \par \uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par 10:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Giandomenico Iannetti, a pain expert at \uc2 UCL\uc2 , angrily denies that his research suggests foetuses can feel pain before 24 weeks\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 University College London\uc2  scientist has accused lawyers in the US of misusing his groundbreaking work on the brain to justify the dismantling of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/03/what-is-roe-v-wade-and-how-does-it-affect-abortion-rights-in-the-us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Roe v Wade}}}\uc2 , the landmark ruling that legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationally in America.\par \par \uc2 Giandomenico Iannetti said his research, which used imaging to understand the adult brain\u8217\'20\'19s response to pain, had been wrongly interpreted to make an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  argument.\par \par \uc2 Last week an unprecedented leak of a draft legal opinion showed a majority of supreme court judges support overturning Roe v Wade and ending federal protections for abortions, in a move that could result in 26 states banning it. The court is considering a case, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/nov/30/explainer-mississippi-abortion-rights-roe-v-wade"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dobbs v Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organisation}}}\uc2 , which challenges Mississippi\u8217\'20\'19s ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks gestation.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawyers in that case argued that scientific understanding has moved on since the court\u8217\'20\'19s 1973 ruling that enshrined the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and it was no longer accurate to say foetuses cannot feel pain before 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Their argument relied heavily on a controversial discussion paper on foetal pain published in the Journal of Medical Ethics in 2020 by Dr Stuart Derbyshire, a British associate professor of psychology at the \uc2 National University of Singapore\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The paper claims that some of Iannetti\u8217\'20\'19s research results suggest we might not need a cerebral cortex \u8211\'20\'13 which remains undeveloped in a foetus of less than 24 weeks \u8211\'20\'13 to feel pain.\par \par \uc2 Iannetti, an Italian professor of neuroscience who now leads a laboratory in Italy but spent the past 16 years researching at \uc2 UCL\uc2  and \uc2 Oxford University\uc2 , is adamant that this is \u8220\'20\'1Can unjustified leap\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy results by no means imply that the cortex isn\u8217\'20\'19t necessary to feel pain. I feel they were misinterpreted and used in a very clever way to prove a point. It distresses me that my work was misinterpreted and became one of the pillar arguments they [the lawyers] made,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Prof Iannetti had no idea the paper was being used to justify the dismantling of Roe v Wade until American colleagues contacted him to say they were \u8220\'20\'1Cshocked\u8221\'20\'1D at the way his findings were being presented. He helped academics in the US to draft a response for the lawyers but says he feels it is out of his control and \u8220\'20\'1Cthere isn\u8217\'20\'19t much more I can do to stop people claiming my work says something it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Leading pain scientists and academic medical societies on both sides of the Atlantic strongly dispute the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legal argument, insisting the international scientific consensus that it is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/201429"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not possible for foetuses to experience pain}}}\uc2  in the first few weeks of existence remains firm and \u8220\'20\'1Cirrefutable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 John Wood, professor of molecular neurobiology at \uc2 UCL\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CI thought this opinion piece [by Derbyshire] was inaccurate.\u8221\'20\'1D Wood insisted that \u8220\'20\'1Call serious scientists\u8221\'20\'1D agreed a foetus cannot feel pain until 24 weeks, \u8220\'20\'1Cand perhaps not even then\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said lawyers were right to say that science has moved on since 1973, but not in the way they were claiming. \u8220\'20\'1CFor instance, we understand a great deal more about pain in newborn babies,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CInterestingly surgeons who operate on foetuses say that there is movement on surgical intervention from week 36.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Derbyshire told the Observer he is \u8220\'20\'1Cfirmly pro choice\u8221\'20\'1D. He insisted that he had not overstepped in his paper, and claimed that while Iannetti\u8217\'20\'19s work had nothing \u8220\'20\'1Cdirectly\u8221\'20\'1D to do with foetal pain it had \u8220\'20\'1Cunsettled the consensus that the cortex is necessary for pain\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He said: \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t honestly see how we can rule out the foetus having some raw experience that is akin to pain. It may be for ever unknowable, and it will not be an equivalent to what you or I experience, but that does not make it nothing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Prof Vania Apkarian, director of the Centre for Translational Pain Research at the Feinberg School of Medicine in Chicago, who has spent two decades studying pain in humans and animals, said the evidence on foetal pain had not changed since 1973 and remains \u8220\'20\'1Cirrefutable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is no rational basis for arguing a foetus can suffer pain before 24 weeks. The anatomy of the brain is not formed enough for that to be possible,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe foetus is in an essentially sleep-like state in the womb.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Apkarian wrote the scientific briefing for the Jackson Women\u8217\'20\'19s Health Organisation case, on behalf of organisations including the \uc2 Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine\uc2  in the US and the \uc2 Royal College of Obstetricians and Gynaecologists\uc2  in the UK. He spent months checking all the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  scientific references in case his side had missed some piece of serious evidence. \u8220\'20\'1CWe hadn\u8217\'20\'19t,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Apkarian believes science has been roped into a social and religious battle over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to play on people\u8217\'20\'19s emotions. \u8220\'20\'1CThe Mississippi case claimed that the foetus, when aborted, is suffering. They claimed that because it is such an emotionally highly laden statement. But it is also totally untrue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Dr Meera Shah, chief medical officer of Planned Parenthood Hudson Peconic, in New York, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe bottom line is that a patient\u8217\'20\'19s health, not unproven theories, should drive important medical decisions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220511ei5800326\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
