\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Women who fought for US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights in the 70s call for mass global protests\b0\par\par\uc2 Miranda Bryant \par \uc2 964 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par 06:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Veteran activists say the overthrow of Roe v Wade would equate to murder, and should send warning signals around the world\par \par \uc2 It was over the Thanksgiving holiday, catching up with old high school friends, that Frances Beal heard that Cordelia had died. Like the now 82-year-old Black feminist and activist, her friend had left home to go to college, but she didn\u8217\'20\'19t make it through her first year because, like anybody who wanted to terminate a pregnancy in America in 1958, she had been forced to undergo a backstreet \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShe was dead because she\u8217\'20\'19d had an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And it had gone bad. And if you take a look at the statistics, the number of woman that died from illegal abortions was tremendous,\u8221\'20\'1D Beal, who later joined the movement to legalise \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told the Observer.\par \par \uc2 Now, more than 60 years after Cordelia\u8217\'20\'19s death and nearly half a century since Roe v Wade legalised \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she fears many more women could die after a leaked draft document revealed that the supreme court looks like it is preparing to overturn the landmark ruling.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe overthrow of Roe v Wade equals the murder and assassination of women and that\u8217\'20\'19s something that I feel in my heart will happen again,\u8221\'20\'1D said the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.jstor.org/stable/40338758"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 author of the pioneering 1969 pamphlet}}}\uc2 Double Jeopardy: To Be Black and Female.\par \par \uc2 What happened last week should send an urgent warning signal not only to Americans but the world, she said, calling on people to take to the streets in their millions worldwide as they did {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2020/jun/01/george-floyd-protests-take-place-in-cities-around-the-world"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 following the police murder of George Floyd}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, America often acts as a precursor of things to happen in other countries. And if they can attack and destroy a woman\u8217\'20\'19s right to choose an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States it won\u8217\'20\'19t be too far before the right will be destroyed in other countries around the world.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said women around the world needed to return to the attitude of women in the 60s to protect the lives of thousands whose lives would be put under threat. It\u8217\'20\'19s a basic human right for a woman to control her own body,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The death of her friend enabled Beal to immediately take a strong stance on the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and she told her story to mass meetings in New York.\par \par \uc2 To be facing the same struggle again is both depressing and angering, but she hopes it is an opportunity for women to organise and unite as they did in the 1960s and 70s.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe question of access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even as it was legal, was somewhat limited because of class and racial differences. We don\u8217\'20\'19t forget that,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut we can put aside those class and racial differences, as well as generational differences, and stand together and say: \u8216\'20\'18No, this is not something that we are going to accept. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t accept it back in the 1970s and we\u8217\'20\'19re not going to accept it today.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2012/jun/11/merle-hoffman-woman-abortion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Merle Hoffman, 76, has been on the frontlines}}}\uc2  of the movement for more than 50 years since giving up her plan to become a concert pianist. She founded Choices Women\u8217\'20\'19s Medical Center in Queens, New York, one of the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics in the US.\par \par \uc2 In 1989, she declared a \u8220\'20\'1Cstate of emergency\u8221\'20\'1D in women\u8217\'20\'19s\u8217\'20\'19 rights in front of St Patrick\u8217\'20\'19s Cathedral with a huge coat hanger. Today she continues to use the coat hanger at protests and speeches, and she keeps it in her office.\par \par \uc2 While the latest development is \u8220\'20\'1Ca tremendous disaster\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Can egregious take back of a fundamental human and civil and constitutional right\u8221\'20\'1D she said it has been a very long time coming.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve been persistent, consistent and this has been their goal, and with the three new conservative judges on the court, they\u8217\'20\'19ve managed to be in the position where they can actually do this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hoffman, who recently helped to found a new organisation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://riseup4abortionrights.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rise Up 4 \b  \highlight22Abortion\b0 \highlight22  Rights}}}\uc2 , said it is a \u8220\'20\'1Cgenerational struggle\u8221\'20\'1D that she doesn\u8217\'20\'19t see coming to an end any time soon. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s strange, it\u8217\'20\'19s Kafkaesque. In a sense I\u8217\'20\'19m reliving my youth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After Roe v Wade, she said, many people \u8220\'20\'1Cmetaphorically put their political feet up on their desks and said \u8216\'20\'18we\u8217\'20\'19ve got it covered\u8217\'20\'19.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CWell, you never have it covered. We have to fight issues in the civil rights struggle again, voting rights, all sorts of things, it\u8217\'20\'19s never covered because there\u8217\'20\'19s always opposition. It\u8217\'20\'19s a dynamic force.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dr Nori Rost, a minister and clergy leader of the \uc2 New York Society for Ethical Culture\uc2 , was still at high school in Kansas when she attended her first pro-choice event in 1978.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was five years after Roe v Wade, so it was a strange mixture of jubilation and still maybe PTSD at where we had been because it was still so raw and recent in people\u8217\'20\'19s memories,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rost, now 59.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just shocking to think that we\u8217\'20\'19re back to where we were in 1972 \u8211\'20\'13 50 years later, here we are in the same panic, in the same uncertainty about what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen with people who need to access safe legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She also fears the unravelling of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights could also lead to the rollback of marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very helpless feeling that five people can have the right to choose how millions of women in this country can access healthcare to have agency over their own bodies,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s very demoralising.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220511ei580030x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
