\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Observer view on US  \highlight22\uc2 abortion \highlight\uc2  rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Observer editorial \par \uc2 665 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par 09:56 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Overturning the Roe v Wade ruling poses a real threat to women\u8217\'20\'19s lives\par \par \uc2 Universal access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been a constitutional right guaranteed to all women in the US since the 1973 supreme court Roe v Wade ruling. But in some states, this has been chipped away through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://theweek.com/articles/559840/admitting-privileges-sham-future-abortion-america"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stringent restrictions}}}\uc2  on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics and the doctors who practise in them. There is only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/world-us-canada-61327082"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one remaining \b  \highlight22abortion\b0 \highlight22  clinic}}}\uc2  in the whole of Mississippi, for example, making access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a theoretical rather than a practical right for most women in that state.\par \par \uc2 Now a conservative supreme court looks set to scrap this universal right, rolling back access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for many more women across the US. A draft court ruling leaked last week, written by Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  and reportedly supported by a majority of the court, would overturn Roe v Wade. It argues that it was \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 egregiously wrong}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D from the start in making this a constitutional issue and that laws on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be determined by federal and state legislators rather than the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 Several Republican states have already passed highly restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in anticipation of this ruling, meaning most abortions will be illegal in around 25 states in the likely event the court overturns Roe v Wade. This is estimated to mean 18 million women of child-bearing age will be more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-022-01249-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 200 miles}}}\uc2  from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, effectively removing access for the many who cannot afford to travel.\par \par \uc2 Forcing women to give birth after becoming pregnant, even in the case of incest and rape, would represent an extraordinary rollback of their reproductive rights in one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s richest democracies. History tells us there is no way to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 only safe access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Women will always resort to dangerous methods and backstreet clinics to terminate unwanted pregnancies if there is no other way and some will lose their lives as a result. Restricting access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is associated with higher infant and maternal mortality \u8211\'20\'13 and the US already has the worst rates among {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-022-01249-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wealthier countries}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and higher {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nature.com/articles/d41586-022-01249-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rates of poverty}}}\uc2 . Women who seek illegal abortions, and those who help them, could find themselves subject to the harshest of punishments; in Texas, anyone performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be tried for murder and face a life sentence in prison.\par \par \uc2 If the US restricts \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, it will go against the international trends towards liberalisation of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , joining a small club dominated by countries such as Poland, where a rightwing authoritarian government has introduced the most restrictive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws in Europe. There are some uniquely American factors that have left women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights exposed. American women have long enjoyed fewer rights than their British and European counterparts, for example in relation to pregnancy and maternity discrimination. The American religious right has long made overturning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights through a conservative majority on the supreme court its key objective. And the fact that progressive presidents and legislators have never spent the political capital that would have been required to enshrine women\u8217\'20\'19s reproductive rights in legislation means that Roe v Wade was always going to be vulnerable to challenge once the court swung rightwards.\par \par \uc2 So much of women\u8217\'20\'19s oppression is based on their reproductive biology: on taking away their choices, forcing them to continue with unwanted pregnancies and punishing them economically for having children. In the UK, a woman is killed by a man every three days, rape convictions are at a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2020/jul/30/convictions-fall-record-low-england-wales-prosecutions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 historic low}}}\uc2  and most women in prison are survivors of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.prisonreformtrust.org.uk/presspolicy/news/vw/1/itemID/494"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 domestic abuse}}}\uc2 . What is happening in the US shows the danger of taking women\u8217\'20\'19s rights for granted: what is hard won can also be lost.\par \par \uc2 *\