\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Corporate America buckles down for culture war on Roe v Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 1,129 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par 07:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Republicans are mulling retaliation against firms providing benefits such as travel assistance for employees seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 After a supreme court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/may/02/roe-v-wade-abortion-supreme-court-draft-opinion"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decision}}}\uc2  that overturns Roe v Wade was leaked and signaled the impending end of federal constitutional protection for abortions, a trickle of companies have slowly started to announce policies that provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access for their employees. But while the protections may keep employees and consumers happy, the threat of retaliation from conservative lawmakers looms.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , one of the biggest banks in the US, quietly started covering the travel expenses of employees who want to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  but are banned from getting one in their home state.\par \par \uc2 The benefit was not announced publicly. Instead, the company mentioned the change in benefits in a March {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.citigroup.com/citi/investor/quarterly/2022/ar22p.pdf?ieNocache=923"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filing}}}\uc2  for shareholders. Once news outlets began to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-03-16/citi-to-cover-staff-travel-for-abortions-as-states-limit-access?sref=fqqmZ8gi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  on the new benefit, the Republican ire began.\par \par \uc2 Conservatives in Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/abortion-citi-republicans-bank/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 asked}}}\uc2  House and Senate administrators to cancel its contract with the company, which issues credit cards to lawmakers to use for work-related flights, office supplies and other goods. A state lawmaker in Texas, infuriated by \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , introduced a bill that would prevent companies from doing business with local governments in Texas if they provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related benefits to their employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Citigroup\uc2  decided to pander to the woke ideologues in its C-suite instead of obeying the laws of Texas,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Briscoe Cain\uc2 , the Texas state representative who introduced the bill, in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BriscoeCain/status/1504891954475290624/photo/2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe will enact laws necessary to prevent this misuse of shareholder money and hold \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  accountable for its violation of our state\u8217\'20\'19s \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  has now been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/05/05/abortion-travel-benefit-uber-apple-amazon-lyft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joined}}}\uc2  by \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Yelp\uc2 , \uc2 Match Group\uc2 , \uc2 Tesla\uc2  and \uc2 Levi Strauss & Company\uc2 , all which have said they will offer travel assistance to employees who are in states that restrict abortions. Insiders at \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-05-05/goldman-and-jpmorgan-weigh-covering-abortion-travel-amid-fallout?sref=fqqmZ8gi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told news outlets}}}\uc2  they too are considering similar policies.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI expect there will be a significant shift and the most leading companies are going to recognize that they need to protect the healthcare of their employees,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Shelley Alpern\uc2 , director of shareholder advocacy at Rhia Ventures. \u8220\'20\'1CMost companies would like to avoid taking a public stance on this issue because it\u8217\'20\'19s so controversial, but there are higher risks for companies when they don\u8217\'20\'19t protect their employees\u8217\'20\'19 healthcare access.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In today\u8217\'20\'19s heated political climate \u8211\'20\'13 and with midterm elections looming \u8211\'20\'13 corporate America can expect a fiery response to any stance it takes on Roe\u8217\'20\'19s fall. But given the widespread impact the end of Roe v Wade will have on much of the country \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/may/03/what-states-abortion-clinics-access-trigger-laws"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 26 states}}}\uc2  will restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access if the decision is overturned \u8211\'20\'13 it is unlikely that companies can get away with not responding to the issue once the supreme court makes its final decision.\par \par \uc2 Neeru Paharia, an associate professor at Georgetown University McDonough School of Business, said that people expect more out of companies as trust in government has fallen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are enacting their political will in the marketplace,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. For consumers, a purchase from a company can be a symbolic sign of support. For employees, their identities can be tied to the ethical positions of the company they work for.\par \par \uc2 Over the last few years, corporate America has started to become more vocal on various issues that have gotten the attention of conservative lawmakers, including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/mar/31/delta-georgia-voting-restrictions-statement-coca-cola"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting rights}}}\uc2  and LGBTQ+ issues. But conservative politicians have gotten bolder at fighting back against what they consider to be \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2018/02/28/opinion/corporate-america-activism.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 woke capitalism\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While the GOP has historically positioned itself as the business-friendly, tax-cutting political party, conservative lawmakers have been emboldened to threaten and punish companies who speak out on controversial issues.\par \par \uc2 Last month, Florida\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/20/disney-world-florida-dont-say-gay-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 revoked}}}\uc2  special land use privileges the state gave to \uc2 Disney\uc2  for its \uc2 Disney\uc2  World theme park in Orlando after the company \u8211\'20\'13 responding to backlash from employees and consumers \u8211\'20\'13 spoke out against the state\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdon\u8217\'20\'19t say gay\u8221\'20\'1D law. The move appeared to catch people by surprise. \uc2 Lloyd Blankfein\uc2 , former \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  CEO, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/lloydblankfein/status/1516750980901126146?s=21&t=ws-uXX4GKw9U9j7g1rfj_w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweeted}}}\uc2  that the move \u8220\'20\'1Csmacks of government retaliation for exercising free speech. Bad look for a conservative.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was really shocking,\u8221\'20\'1D Paharia said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow you have a situation where consumers and employees want companies to take a political stance, but then you have governments that are possibly retaliating against them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \u8220\'20\'1Ceven though it might not be [explicitly] taking a side \u8230\'20\'26 [companies] are taking a position based on the kind of benefits they are going to offer their employees\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The threats lawmakers have made have so far not come to fruition, but the party seems serious on trying to penalize companies in some way. The Republican senator \uc2 Marco Rubio{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-05-05/rubio-targets-citi-amazon-with-bill-on-abortion-travel-benefits?sref=fqqmZ8gi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 introduced}}}\uc2  a bill this week that would not allow companies to deduct \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related travel benefits as regular employee benefits when a company files its taxes.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur tax code should be pro-family and promote a culture of life,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 With these warnings, companies may try to keep the introduction of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related quiet or downplay their significance. When \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO, \uc2 Jane Fraser\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2020/sep/10/citigroup-wall-street-bank-female-ceo-jane-fraser"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first woman}}}\uc2  to lead a major American bank \u8211\'20\'13 was asked in a shareholders meeting about the company\u8217\'20\'19s new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travel benefit, she said the benefit \u8220\'20\'1Cisn\u8217\'20\'19t intended to be a statement about a very sensitive issue\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we did here was follow our past practices,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, adding that the company had \u8220\'20\'1Ccovered reproductive healthcare benefits for over 20 years. And our practice has also been to make sure our employees have the same health coverage, no matter where in the US they live.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jen Stark, senior director of corporate strategy at Tara Health Foundation, who helped coordinate the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://dontbanequality.com/2019signers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signatures}}}\uc2  of over 180 executives in a statement against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in 2019, said the potential backlash from conservative lawmakers proves that companies need to act on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions beyond mitigating effects for their employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey can buy all the plane tickets their workers need, and that addresses the immediate harm, but the structural deficiency is the collateral damage,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe supreme court case didn\u8217\'20\'19t happen in a bubble \u8230\'20\'26 you\u8217\'20\'19re kind of walking over the rubble.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Beyond benefits for employees, Stark has been advocating for companies to use their lobbying powers and scrutinize political donations as state lawmakers prepare to restrict abortions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are at the moment everyone\u8217\'20\'19s cried wolf about. It\u8217\'20\'19s here, but there was also a lot of headwind,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat companies can do with a stroke of a pen to mitigate some of the harm is important, but the larger issue is getting out of this structural whirlpool that we\u8217\'20\'19re in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220508ei58000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
